// Marginalia reference data, loaded by index.html before the app. Plain JavaScript so it keeps its comments.
// General reference definitions, each with a longer note (x), its thinkers (p), related terms (r), contrasting terms (vs) and branches or traditions (br).
// Some have a picture, img: { file: a public-domain file on Wikimedia Commons, alt, cap: its credit (the app adds the link), wide: true to show it full width }.
// Fallacies carry fal: "formal" (wrong by form alone) or "informal" (wrong by content or context); the Logic branch lists them as a catalog.
// ex: example arguments that commit it, for the quiz's Name the fallacy (written for the app, apart from the classic equivocation on “end”).
// Some have a diagram, fig: a key of FIGURES at the end of this file, drawn as SVG in the app's colours.
// Spinoza is quoted from Curley's translation. Deleuze and Guattari's concepts cite their books by chapter and page, with scholarship on them.
const GLOSSARY = [
  // Metaphysics
  { id: "ontology", t: "Ontology", a: "meta",
    d: "The study of being: what exists, and what the most general kinds and structures of existing things are. Ontology asks which basic categories (substances, properties, events, relations, processes) we need in order to describe reality.",
    x: "The inquiry goes back to Parmenides and to Aristotle's study of “being as being”; the word itself dates from the early seventeenth century, and Christian Wolff made it the standard name for general metaphysics. Quine framed the modern question as what a theory commits us to: “to be is to be the value of a variable.” Heidegger called the difference between being and particular beings the ontological difference, and made the question of the meaning of being his starting point.",
    s: "Aristotle, Metaphysics IV.1; Quine, “On What There Is” (1948); Heidegger, Being and Time (1927), §§1–4",
    p: ["parmenides", "aristotle", "quine", "heidegger"], r: ["metaphysics", "substance", "universals", "dasein"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysics”" },
  { id: "metaphysics", t: "Metaphysics", a: "meta",
    d: "Inquiry into the fundamental nature of reality: being, substance, causation, time, possibility, identity and the relation of mind to world. Aristotle called it “first philosophy”, the study of being as such and of first causes.",
    x: "The name comes from the ancient editors who placed Aristotle's treatise after the Physics (ta meta ta phusika). Kant asked whether metaphysics could be a science at all, and confined theoretical knowledge to possible experience; the logical empiricists, Carnap above all, dismissed it as meaningless. It returned in analytic philosophy through modal logic and debates about properties, persistence and causation, while Heidegger and Derrida made the critique of the metaphysical tradition itself a central task.",
    s: "Aristotle, Metaphysics; Kant, Prolegomena to Any Future Metaphysics (1783); Carnap, “The Elimination of Metaphysics through Logical Analysis of Language” (1932)",
    p: ["aristotle", "kant", "carnap", "heidegger"], r: ["ontology", "substance", "transcendental", "determinism", "necessity"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysics”" },
  { id: "substance", t: "Substance", a: "meta",
    d: "What exists in its own right and bears properties: Aristotle's ousia, the primary being on which qualities, quantities and relations depend. Spinoza: “By substance I understand what is in itself and is conceived through itself…”",
    x: "Aristotle's Categories treats individual things, this man or this horse, as primary substances; Metaphysics VII asks whether substance is form, matter or their compound. Descartes counts two kinds of created substance, thinking and extended, which raises the problem of how they interact. Spinoza argues from his definition that there can be only one substance, God or Nature, of which minds and bodies are modes. Locke held that our idea of substance is only the idea of an unknown support of qualities.",
    s: "Aristotle, Categories 5 and Metaphysics VII; Spinoza, Ethics I, Def. 3 (Curley trans.); Locke, Essay Concerning Human Understanding II.xxiii",
    p: ["aristotle", "descartes", "spinoza", "locke"], r: ["attribute", "mode", "essence", "accident", "monism", "dualism"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysics”" },
  { id: "attribute", t: "Attribute", a: "meta",
    d: "An essential property of a substance. Spinoza: “By attribute I understand what the intellect perceives of a substance, as constituting its essence.”",
    x: "For Descartes each substance has one principal attribute: thought for minds, extension for bodies. Spinoza's God has infinitely many attributes, of which we know two, thought and extension, and a mind and its body are one and the same thing conceived under two attributes. Commentators still debate whether Spinoza's attributes are really distinct features of substance or the ways an intellect grasps it.",
    s: "Spinoza, Ethics I, Defs. 4 and 6; II, Prop. 7, Schol. (Curley trans.); Descartes, Principles of Philosophy I.53",
    p: ["descartes", "spinoza"], r: ["substance", "mode", "dualism", "monism"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Baruch Spinoza”" },
  { id: "mode", t: "Mode", a: "meta",
    d: "A way or modification in which a substance exists. Spinoza: “By mode I understand the affections of a substance, or that which is in another through which it is also conceived.”",
    x: "Modes are the particular, dependent things and states that exist in substance: this body, this idea, a motion, a feeling. Descartes uses the term for the variable features of a substance, such as a particular shape of an extended body. In Spinoza every finite thing, including each human being, is a mode of the one substance, and its striving to persist (conatus) is its actual essence.",
    s: "Spinoza, Ethics I, Def. 5; III, Prop. 7 (Curley trans.); Descartes, Principles of Philosophy I.56",
    p: ["descartes", "spinoza"], r: ["substance", "attribute", "conatus", "immanence"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Baruch Spinoza”" },
  { id: "conatus", t: "Conatus", a: "meta",
    d: "The striving of each thing to persist in existence. Spinoza: “Each thing, as far as it can by its own power, strives to persevere in its being.”",
    x: "The Latin word means effort or endeavour. Hobbes used it for the small beginnings of motion, including appetite and aversion. Spinoza makes conatus the actual essence of every thing; in human beings, who are conscious of it, it is called desire, and joy and sadness are passages to greater or lesser power of acting. Nietzsche called self-preservation a superfluous teleological principle that we owe to Spinoza's inconsistency, and put will to power in its place.",
    s: "Spinoza, Ethics III, Props. 6–9, 11 Schol. (Curley trans.); Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), ch. 6; Nietzsche, Beyond Good and Evil (1886), §13",
    p: ["hobbes", "spinoza", "nietzsche"], r: ["mode", "will-to-power", "teleology"], br: ["metaphysics", "ethics"], k: ["conatus"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Baruch Spinoza”" },
  { id: "immanence", t: "Immanence", a: "meta",
    d: "Existing or operating within something. An immanent cause produces effects that remain in it. Spinoza: “God is the immanent, not the transitive, cause of all things.”",
    x: "The term has several uses. In theology an immanent God dwells within the world; its opposite is a God who transcends it. In Kant, immanent principles apply within the limits of possible experience, while transcendent ones claim to pass those limits. In phenomenology, what is immanent belongs to consciousness itself. Immanent critique applies the same idea to criticism, judging a position by its own standards.",
    s: "Spinoza, Ethics I, Prop. 18 (Curley trans.); Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, A295–96/B352–53",
    p: ["spinoza", "kant", "husserl"], r: ["immanent-critique", "monism", "mode"], vs: ["transcendence"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Baruch Spinoza”" },
  { id: "transcendence", t: "Transcendence", a: "meta",
    d: "Existing above or outside something: a God outside the world, a principle outside what it governs, or the mind's reaching toward what exceeds it.",
    x: "Classical theism holds that God transcends creation while remaining present to it. In Kant, transcendent principles overstep the limits of possible experience, and they must be kept apart from transcendental ones, which concern the conditions of experience. Phenomenologists use the word for consciousness's directedness toward a world: Heidegger describes Dasein's being-in-the-world as transcendence, and Levinas writes of the transcendence of the other person, whom I can never comprehend or possess.",
    s: "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, A296/B352; Levinas, Totality and Infinity (1961)",
    p: ["kant", "heidegger", "levinas"], r: ["transcendental", "noumenon", "sublime"], vs: ["immanence"], br: ["metaphysics", "religion"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Immanuel Kant”" },
  { id: "essence", t: "Essence", a: "meta",
    d: "What a thing is: the properties it must have to be the kind of thing it is. The scholastic name is quiddity, the “whatness” of a thing; the features it could lose are its accidents.",
    x: "Aristotle's “what it was to be” a thing is given in its definition. Ibn Sīnā distinguished a thing's essence from its existence, so that what a horse is can be understood without knowing whether any horse exists, and Aquinas built on the distinction. Locke separated real essences, the hidden constitutions of things, from nominal essences, the abstract ideas by which we sort them. Existentialists reverse the order for human beings: “existence precedes essence.”",
    s: "Aristotle, Metaphysics VII.4–6; Locke, Essay III.iii; Sartre, Existentialism Is a Humanism (1946)",
    p: ["aristotle", "ibnsina", "aquinas", "locke", "sartre"], r: ["essence-existence", "essentialism", "existentialism", "substance"], vs: ["accident"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysics”" },
  { id: "accident", t: "Accident", a: "meta",
    d: "A property a thing has and could lose while remaining the same thing, such as Socrates' being seated or pale.",
    x: "Aristotle lists nine categories of accident (quantity, quality, relation, place, time and others) alongside substance, and holds that accidents exist only in substances. The distinction between essential and accidental properties became a staple of scholastic philosophy and returns in modern modal terms: an essential property is one a thing has in every possible world in which it exists.",
    s: "Aristotle, Categories 4; Metaphysics V.30",
    p: ["aristotle", "aquinas"], r: ["substance", "necessity"], vs: ["essence"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Aristotle”" },
  { id: "necessity", t: "Necessity and contingency", a: "meta",
    d: "A necessary truth or being could not have been otherwise. A contingent one could have been otherwise, or might never have existed.",
    x: "Philosophers distinguish kinds of necessity: logical (denying it yields a contradiction), metaphysical (true in every possible world), physical (fixed by the laws of nature) and conditional (necessary given something else). Leibniz grounded contingent truths in God's choice of the best possible world. Kripke argued that some necessary truths, such as “water is H₂O”, can be known only a posteriori, which pulls necessity apart from a priority.",
    s: "Leibniz, Monadology (1714), §§33–46; Kripke, Naming and Necessity (1980)",
    p: ["aristotle", "leibniz", "kripke"], r: ["a-priori", "determinism", "essence", "accident"], br: ["metaphysics", "logic"], k: ["necessity", "contingency", "contingent"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysics”" },
  { id: "potentiality", t: "Potentiality and actuality", a: "meta",
    d: "Aristotle's dunamis and energeia: a capacity to be or do something, and the realization of that capacity. The acorn is potentially an oak; the grown oak is the actuality toward which it develops.",
    x: "Aristotle introduces the pair to answer Parmenides' denial of change: change is the actualizing of a potential, so nothing comes to be from sheer non-being. Actuality is prior to potentiality in definition and in substance, and the unmoved mover is pure actuality. The distinction shapes medieval accounts of God and the soul, and later theories of development, including Hegel's.",
    s: "Aristotle, Metaphysics IX; Physics III.1",
    p: ["aristotle", "aquinas", "hegel"], r: ["teleology", "four-causes", "substance"], br: ["metaphysics", "ancient"], k: ["potentiality", "actuality"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Aristotle”" },
  { id: "teleology", t: "Teleology", a: "meta",
    d: "Explanation by ends or purposes (Greek telos). A teleological explanation says what something is for: eyes are for seeing, and an acorn grows toward being an oak.",
    x: "Aristotle's final cause is teleological, and he applied it throughout nature. Early modern mechanists such as Descartes and Spinoza expelled final causes from physics; Spinoza called them human fictions projected onto nature. Kant held that we must judge organisms as if they were purposive, while claiming no knowledge that nature has purposes. Darwin explained apparent design by natural selection, and debate continues about whether talk of biological functions is teleological.",
    s: "Aristotle, Physics II.8; Spinoza, Ethics I, Appendix; Kant, Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), §§64–66",
    p: ["aristotle", "spinoza", "kant"], r: ["four-causes", "potentiality", "determinism", "conatus"], br: ["metaphysics", "science"], k: ["teleology", "teleological", "telos"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Aristotle”" },
  { id: "four-causes", fig: "four-causes", t: "Four causes", a: "meta",
    d: "Aristotle's four kinds of explanation: material (what a thing is made of), formal (its form or structure), efficient (what brought it about) and final (what it is for).",
    x: "The Greek aitia is closer to “explanation” or “that because of which” in meaning. A bronze statue is explained by its bronze (material), its shape (formal), the sculptor (efficient) and the purpose it serves (final). Modern science kept the efficient cause and largely dropped the final cause, which is why teleology became controversial.\n\nAristotle held that natural things, unlike artifacts, have their source of change within them, so that in nature the formal, efficient and final causes often coincide: the form of an oak is what the acorn's development aims at and also what guides it. This is why his biology explains organs by what they are for, a pattern of explanation that persisted in natural philosophy until early modern mechanism.",
    s: "Aristotle, Physics II.3",
    p: ["aristotle"], r: ["teleology", "potentiality", "substance", "occasionalism"], br: ["metaphysics", "ancient", "science"], k: ["four causes", "final cause", "efficient cause"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Aristotle”" },
  { id: "materialism", t: "Materialism", a: "meta",
    d: "The view that everything that exists is material, or depends on matter and its motions. Mind, on this view, is a feature or product of physical processes.",
    x: "Democritus held that all is atoms and the void, and Lucretius is usually counted a materialist too; in the seventeenth century Hobbes revived this atomism. Modern physicalism, a term often used interchangeably with materialism, holds that everything is physical, or supervenes on the physical. Marx's materialism makes a different claim, about the priority of material production in social life, and Bataille's “base materialism” treats matter as a movement of expenditure and waste. Thomas Nagel argued that physicalism leaves out the essential subjectivity of experience; “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974) is his best-known statement of it.",
    s: "Lucretius, On the Nature of Things; Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), ch. 46; Bataille, “Base Materialism and Gnosticism” (1930); Nagel, “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974)",
    p: ["epicurus", "lucretius", "hobbes", "marx", "bataille", "nagel"], r: ["dualism", "monism", "historical-materialism"], vs: ["idealism"], br: ["metaphysics", "mind"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Physicalism” (Daniel Stoljar)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx” (Jonathan Wolff and David Leopold)" }, { src: "https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/opth-2024-0016/html", srcName: "“The Remedy for a World Without Transcendence”: Georges Bataille on Sacrifice and the Theology of Transgression, Open Theology 10 (2024) (Luka Trebežnik)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Consciousness” (Robert Van Gulick)" }] },
  { id: "idealism", t: "Idealism", a: "meta",
    d: "The view that reality is fundamentally mental or ideal, or that the world as we know it depends on mind. Berkeley's subjective idealism, Kant's transcendental idealism and Hegel's absolute idealism are very different positions.",
    x: "Berkeley argued that for physical things to be is to be perceived (esse est percipi): they are collections of ideas, sustained by God's perception. Kant's transcendental idealism holds that space, time and the categories are forms of our cognition, so that we know things only as they appear. Hegel's absolute idealism treats reality as the self-development of spirit or reason, to be grasped as a whole. Marx turned Hegel's dialectic toward material conditions.",
    s: "Berkeley, A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710), §3; Kant, Critique of Pure Reason (1781/1787); Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit (1807)",
    p: ["berkeley", "kant", "fichte", "schelling", "hegel"], r: ["noumenon", "transcendental", "dialectic", "monism"], vs: ["materialism"], br: ["metaphysics", "german-idealism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/berkeley/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “George Berkeley”" },
  { id: "dualism", t: "Dualism", a: "meta",
    d: "The view that there are two fundamental kinds of thing; classically, Descartes' thinking substance (mind) and extended substance (body).",
    x: "Descartes argued that he could clearly conceive his mind existing without his body, so the two are distinct. The view leaves the problem of interaction: mind, whose nature is thought, and body, whose nature is extension, seem to lack what they would need in common to act on each other. Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia pressed this objection in her letters to Descartes, beginning in 1643. Property dualists today hold that there are two essentially different kinds of property, mental and physical, without positing immaterial substances. Ryle mocked Cartesian dualism as “the dogma of the Ghost in the Machine”.",
    s: "Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641), VI; Ryle, The Concept of Mind (1949), ch. 1",
    p: ["descartes", "elisabeth", "ryle"], r: ["substance", "category-mistake", "materialism", "being-in-the-world"], vs: ["monism"], br: ["metaphysics", "mind"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Dualism” (Howard Robinson and Ralph Weir)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/elisabeth-bohemia/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Elisabeth of Bohemia” (Lisa Shapiro)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ryle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilbert Ryle” (Julia Tanney)" }] },
  { id: "monism", t: "Monism", a: "meta",
    d: "The view that reality is ultimately one thing or one kind of thing. Spinoza holds that there is a single substance, God or Nature.",
    x: "Substance monism says there is one substance; existence monism says there is one concrete thing; kind monism says everything is of one kind, as materialism and idealism each claim. Parmenides argued that being is one and unchanging, since what is not cannot be thought. Spinoza's “God or Nature” (Deus sive Natura) is the classic early modern monism, and the Advaita Vedānta identification of self and Brahman is often compared with it.",
    s: "Parmenides, fragment B8; Spinoza, Ethics I, Prop. 14; IV, Preface (Curley trans.)",
    p: ["parmenides", "spinoza", "hegel"], r: ["substance", "materialism", "idealism", "immanence", "advaita"], vs: ["dualism"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Baruch Spinoza”" },
  { id: "determinism", t: "Determinism", a: "meta",
    d: "The view that every event is fixed by prior causes together with the laws of nature. If determinism is true, a complete description of the world at one time, with the laws, settles everything that happens afterward.",
    x: "Causal determinism is the thesis that the complete state of the world at one time, together with the laws of nature, fixes everything that happens afterward. Carl Hoefer's Stanford Encyclopedia article frames it as a claim about the whole world at a time, since single events and their “sufficient causes” are hard to define without endless qualifications. The state in question has to be complete and exact, and the laws have to hold everywhere and always, so what determinism amounts to depends partly on what laws of nature are.\n\nPhilosophers disagree about that. Some treat laws as forces that make things happen. The Best Systems Analysis, associated with Hume, Mill, Ramsey and David Lewis, treats them as the simplest true summary of the regularities in the world's history. Nancy Cartwright, Bas van Fraassen and John Dupré doubt that there are universal laws at all, and on their view determinism is simply false. Leibniz's principle of sufficient reason is a close relative of the thesis, though stronger and untied to physical law.\n\nThe idea is ancient, but it was first given a precise, mathematical form in the eighteenth century, and Laplace's version became the classic statement. In his Philosophical Essay on Probabilities (1814), he imagined an intellect that, knowing every force and the position of every thing, could foresee the whole future and recover the whole past; Hoefer notes that Laplace probably had God in mind. Theological determinism traces events to God's will, and Spinoza held that all things follow necessarily from God's nature. Russell attacked the notion of cause in 1912, and John Earman's A Primer on Determinism (1986) remains the fullest study.\n\nDeterminism and predictability come apart. Popper's The Open Universe (1982) defined determinism in terms of prediction, yet finite observers inside the world can never gather a complete state, and chaotic systems show sensitive dependence on initial conditions, so a deterministic world could be thoroughly unpredictable. Patrick Suppes argued that deterministic chaos and genuine chance can look exactly alike in the data. Determinism also differs from fatalism, which appeals to fate, divine foreknowledge or purpose.\n\nClassical mechanics itself turns out to leave room for indeterminism. Hoefer points to particles that reach unbounded speeds and escape to infinity in finite time (Zhihong Xia showed in 1992 that five bodies suffice), to the time-reversed “space invaders” that arrive from infinity unannounced, to collisions the laws leave undecided, and to John Norton's dome (2003), where a resting ball may start to slide without any breach of Newton's laws. Special relativity is the friendliest setting for determinism; general relativity raises the hole argument of Earman and Norton (1987) and unresolved questions about singularities.\n\nQuantum mechanics is usually taken to have settled the matter against determinism, because the Born rule yields only probabilities. Yet the Schrödinger equation itself is deterministic, so the verdict depends on interpretation. The Copenhagen view adds an indeterministic collapse at measurement; Everettian, many-worlds readings keep the smooth evolution throughout; and David Bohm's theory of 1952 restores determinism with particles that always have definite positions, guided by the wave function, while matching the standard predictions. David Wallace argued in 2020 that Bohmian mechanics has yet to be extended to the quantum field theories of the Standard Model.\n\nThe thesis matters most to the question of free will. Incompatibilists hold that determinism rules out free and responsible action; compatibilists deny it, and Hume thought freedom even required determinism. David Lewis (1981) argued that had we acted differently, the laws would have been slightly different, and Jenann Ismael (2016) and Hoefer argue that physics itself gives the past no special fixity, since time-symmetric laws let later states fix earlier ones just as well. Whatever physics finally says, randomness by itself does little to secure freedom.",
    s: "Laplace, A Philosophical Essay on Probabilities (1814); Spinoza, Ethics I, Prop. 29",
    p: ["spinoza", "hobbes", "hume"], r: ["compatibilism", "necessity", "four-causes", "problem-of-evil"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/determinism-causal/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Causal Determinism”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Compatibilism”" }] },
  { id: "compatibilism", t: "Compatibilism", a: "meta",
    d: "The view that free will is consistent with determinism: an action can be fully caused and still free.",
    x: "Compatibilism is the thesis that free will is compatible with causal determinism, the view that everything that happens is the inevitable result of the laws of nature and the state of the world in the distant past. Because free will is usually treated as a condition of moral responsibility, the thesis is often stated about responsibility too. Incompatibilists hold that if determinism is true, our picture of ourselves as free agents is mistaken. D. Justin Coates's Stanford Encyclopedia article identifies two conditions commonly built into free will: the ability to do otherwise, and being the ultimate source of what one does.\n\nClassical compatibilists such as Hobbes, in Leviathan, and Hume, in the Enquiry concerning Human Understanding, define freedom as the ability to act according to one's own will without external obstacles. Compulsion, being forced by an outside power against one's will, is their model of unfree action, and being caused by one's own desires poses no threat to freedom on this view. The position was revived in the early twentieth century.\n\nThe classical view meets an objection from mental illness: a person in the grip of psychosis may do just what she wants, unimpeded, and still fail to act freely. Classical compatibilists answered with a conditional analysis, defended by R. E. Hobart (1934) and A. J. Ayer (1954): to say an agent could have done otherwise is to say she would have, had she wanted to. Roderick Chisholm (1964) and Peter van Inwagen (1983) replied with cases such as Danielle, who is psychologically unable to want to touch a blond dog; the analysis wrongly says she could have picked one up.\n\nThe strongest argument against compatibilism is the Consequence Argument, first developed by Carl Ginet in 1966 and set out formally in van Inwagen's An Essay on Free Will (1983). No one has any power over the distant past or the laws of nature; if determinism is true, every present act is a consequence of those; so no one has power over any present act. David Lewis replied in “Are We Free to Break the Laws?” (1981) that we may be able to act in such a way that the laws would have been different, and Michael Slote questioned the argument's rule of inference.\n\nHarry Frankfurt reshaped the debate twice. In “Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility” (1969) he described Jones, who shoots Smith on his own while Black stands ready to force him if he wavers: Jones could not have done otherwise and still seems responsible. In “Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person” (1971) he distinguished first-order desires from second-order volitions and located freedom of the will in the fit between them, so that a willing addict acts freely and an unwilling one does not. One acts of one's own free will when one's action issues from the will one wants to have.\n\nP. F. Strawson's “Freedom and Resentment” (1962) turned attention to reactive attitudes such as resentment, indignation and guilt. Holding someone responsible is a matter of taking up those attitudes toward her, and Strawson argued that giving them up wholesale is practically inconceivable, whatever the truth of determinism. Later compatibilists built on the idea of responsiveness to reasons: John Martin Fischer and Mark Ravizza (1998) tie responsibility to the agent's own reasons-responsive mechanism, Susan Wolf's Freedom within Reason (1990) holds that blame requires an ability to act otherwise that praise does not, and Gary Watson (1975) pressed a regress problem against hierarchical views.\n\nManipulation cases remain a central challenge: an agent whose desires were installed by brainwashing or a neuroscientist may satisfy every compatibilist condition, and Derk Pereboom (2001) and Alfred Mele (2019) ask how an ordinary deterministic history differs from such manipulation. New dispositionalists, among them Michael Fara (2008), Michael Smith (2003) and Kadri Vihvelin (2004, 2013), treat abilities as dispositions that can survive determinism. Incompatibilists still reply that if our choices are fixed by events before our birth, they are never really up to us.",
    s: "Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), ch. 21; Hume, Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), §8; Frankfurt, “Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person” (1971)",
    p: ["hobbes", "hume", "frankfurt"], r: ["determinism", "autonomy"], br: ["metaphysics", "ethics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Compatibilism”" },
  { id: "universals", t: "Universals", a: "meta",
    d: "Features that many particular things can share, such as redness or justice. Realists hold that universals exist; nominalists hold that only particulars exist.",
    x: "Plato's Forms are the paradigm of realist universals, existing apart from the things that share in them; Aristotle placed universals in particulars. Porphyry's questions set off a long medieval debate: realists held that universals exist, conceptualists that they exist as concepts in the mind, and nominalists, notably Ockham, that only particular things exist and general terms are signs standing for many of them. Contemporary versions include trope theory and resemblance nominalism.",
    s: "Plato, Republic V–VII; Aristotle, Categories; Porphyry, Isagoge; Ockham, Summa Logicae",
    p: ["plato", "aristotle", "ockham"], r: ["essence", "ontology", "essentialism"], br: ["metaphysics", "language", "ancient"], k: ["universals", "nominalism", "nominalist"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysics”" },
  { id: "dao", t: "Dao (the Way)", a: "meta",
    d: "In Chinese thought, the way or path: the right way of conduct for Confucians, and for Daoists the source and pattern of all things, which fixed names cannot capture.",
    x: "The Analects speaks of the way of the former kings and of the gentleman, a way of ritual, learning and humaneness. The Daodejing, attributed to Laozi, opens by saying that a dao that can be spoken of is not the constant dao, and pairs it with de, the virtue or power by which things follow the way. Zhuangzi develops the Daoist dao through stories of skill, spontaneity and shifting perspectives, such as Cook Ding, who carves an ox by following its natural structure.",
    s: "Laozi, Daodejing, ch. 1; Zhuangzi, ch. 3; Confucius, Analects",
    p: ["confucius", "laozi", "zhuangzi"], r: ["ren", "monism", "immanence"], br: ["chinese", "metaphysics"], k: ["daoism", "taoism", "daodejing", "tao te ching"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/laozi/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Laozi”" },
  { id: "sunyata", t: "Emptiness (śūnyatā)", a: "meta",
    d: "In Madhyamaka Buddhism, the lack of svabhāva, intrinsic existence or own-being, in all things. Everything exists only in dependence on causes, parts and conceptual designation.",
    x: "Nāgārjuna argues that anything with an intrinsic nature could neither arise nor change, so a world of change must be empty. He identifies emptiness with dependent origination (pratītyasamutpāda), and holds that emptiness is itself empty: a way of seeing things, with no further ultimate reality behind it. His doctrine of two truths separates conventional truth, in which persons and objects function, from ultimate truth, their emptiness.",
    s: "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā, ch. 24",
    p: ["nagarjuna"], r: ["anatman", "substance", "essence"], br: ["indian", "metaphysics"], k: ["emptiness", "śūnyatā", "sunyata", "shunyata", "madhyamaka"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nagarjuna/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Nāgārjuna”" },
  { id: "anatman", t: "No-self (anātman)", a: "meta",
    d: "The Buddhist teaching that there is no permanent, unchanging self. What we call a person is a changing stream of five aggregates: body, feeling, perception, mental formations and consciousness.",
    x: "The Buddha argued that none of the aggregates is a self: whatever is impermanent cannot be one. In the Questions of King Milinda the monk Nāgasena compares a person to a chariot: “chariot” is a convenient designation for an arrangement of parts, and likewise “Nāgasena”. The sense of an “I” and a “mine” is the ignorance behind suffering, and its end comes with giving up that sense. Buddhist philosophers argued with Brahmanical schools, such as Sāṃkhya and Nyāya, which defended an enduring self.",
    s: "Milindapañha (Questions of King Milinda), Book II; Hume, Treatise of Human Nature (1739), I.iv.6",
    p: ["nagarjuna", "hume"], r: ["sunyata", "substance", "dualism"], vs: ["advaita"], br: ["indian", "metaphysics", "mind"], k: ["no-self", "anatman", "anātman", "anatta"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/buddha/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Buddha” (Mark Siderits)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/abhidharma/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Abhidharma” (Noa Ronkin)" }] },
  { id: "advaita", t: "Brahman and ātman", a: "meta",
    d: "In the Upanishads, Brahman is the ultimate reality and ātman the inner self. Advaita (“non-dual”) Vedānta, systematized by Śaṅkara, holds that they are identical: “You are that” (tat tvam asi).",
    x: "On Śaṅkara's reading, the world of many separate things is māyā, an appearance superimposed on Brahman through ignorance (avidyā), and liberation (mokṣa) is the knowledge that the self was always Brahman. Later Vedāntins disagreed: Rāmānuja's qualified non-dualism treats souls and the world as real modes of Brahman. Comparisons with Spinoza's single substance are common, though the two systems differ in method and aim.",
    s: "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 6.8.7; Śaṅkara, Commentary on the Brahmasūtras",
    p: ["shankara", "ramanuja"], r: ["monism", "idealism", "transcendence"], vs: ["anatman"], br: ["indian", "metaphysics", "religion"], k: ["brahman", "ātman", "atman", "advaita", "vedanta", "vedānta", "tat tvam asi"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/shankara/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Śaṅkara”" },
  { id: "occasionalism", t: "Occasionalism", a: "meta",
    d: "The view that created things have no causal power of their own: God is the only true cause, and what we call causes are merely the occasions on which God acts.",
    x: "Occasionalism is the view that God is the only genuine cause, and that what we take for causes among creatures are at most the occasions on which God acts. Sukjae Lee's Stanford Encyclopedia article separates two theses in a full occasionalist such as Malebranche: a positive one, that God is the only true cause, and a negative one, that no creature is a true cause. Some thinkers held the view only for a restricted domain, such as bodies, so the article also distinguishes global from local occasionalism.\n\nThe first explicit occasionalists were Muslim theologians of the Ash'arite school of kalām, responding in the tenth and eleventh centuries to the Aristotelian and Neoplatonic philosophy then being reworked in Arabic. The school's founder, al-Ash'arī (873–935), was especially influential, and on one reading he held that God recreates the accidents of every atom at each instant, so that the persistence of things and their apparent interactions are continuous divine work.\n\nAl-Ghazālī (c. 1055–1111) argued in The Incoherence of the Philosophers, directed chiefly against Avicenna, that we observe only that burning follows the contact of cotton with fire, never a necessary connection between them, and that God creates the burning each time. His worry was that necessitating natural causes would limit divine omnipotence and rule out miracles. Scholars still disagree about whether he was a thoroughgoing occasionalist or left some room for secondary causes. Ibn Rushd replied in The Incoherence of the Incoherence that things have essences that determine their functions, so that to deny their powers is to deny their natures.\n\nIn the Latin West, Nicholas of Autrecourt (1300–after 1350) attacked the supposed necessary connection between causes and effects, possibly with knowledge of al-Ghazālī's arguments; his writings were publicly burned in 1347. Lee reads him chiefly as a skeptic, and he is often called “the fourteenth-century Hume”.\n\nA new wave came among Descartes' followers in the 1660s, partly as an answer to the Cartesian problem of how an unextended mind and an extended body could act on each other. Louis de La Forge (1632–66) introduced the phrase “occasional cause” and held that bodies are only the remote and occasional causes of our ideas. Géraud de Cordemoy (1614–83), usually read as a global occasionalist, argued that bodies cannot keep themselves in motion. Johannes Clauberg is associated with a version for mind and body, and Arnold Geulincx (1624–69) gave it a famous principle: one is not the cause of what one does not know how to bring about.\n\nNicolas Malebranche gave occasionalism its fullest defense, in The Search after Truth (1674–75), its Elucidations and the later Dialogues, summed up in his claim that there is only one true cause because there is only one true God. Lee sets out four of his arguments: bodies, whose nature is mere extension, are passive and have no powers; a true cause must know how it brings about its effect; a true cause is one whose connection to its effect is necessary, and only God's will has such a connection; and God's conservation of the world is continuous creation, so God alone produces both things and their states.\n\nLeibniz's pre-established harmony shares occasionalism's denial of causal traffic between created substances, while letting each substance be the true cause of its own states, and concurrentists held that God and creatures together produce each effect. Critics answered that God's ability to override a creature's power leaves that power real, as a parent who can stop a child opening a door leaves the child's push effective. Hume's analysis of causation as constant conjunction strips away the theology and keeps the doubt about necessary connection.",
    s: "al-Ghazālī, The Incoherence of the Philosophers (1095), Discussion 17; Ibn Rushd, The Incoherence of the Incoherence; Malebranche, The Search after Truth (1674–75), VI.ii.3; Hume, Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), §7",
    p: ["ghazali", "ibnrushd", "malebranche", "hume"], r: ["induction-problem", "four-causes", "determinism", "dualism"], br: ["islamic", "metaphysics", "religion"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/occasionalism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Occasionalism”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/malebranche/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Nicolas Malebranche”" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-rushd/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ibn Rushd [Averroes]” (Fouad Ben Ahmed and Robert Pasnau)" }] },
  { id: "essence-existence", t: "Essence and existence", a: "meta",
    d: "The distinction between what a thing is and that it is. Ibn Sīnā argued that existence is not contained in the essence of any created thing: knowing what a triangle or a horse is tells you nothing about whether one exists.",
    x: "For Ibn Sīnā only God, the Necessary Existent, is a being whose essence is to exist; everything else is possible in itself and needs a cause to exist. Aquinas took up the distinction in On Being and Essence. It bears on the ontological argument, which tries to derive God's existence from God's essence, and on the existentialist slogan that for human beings existence precedes essence.",
    s: "Ibn Sīnā, The Book of Healing, Metaphysics I; Aquinas, On Being and Essence",
    p: ["ibnsina", "farabi", "aquinas"], r: ["essence", "ontological-argument", "existentialism"], br: ["islamic", "metaphysics"], k: ["necessary existent", "essence and existence"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-sina/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ibn Sina”" },
  // Knowledge
  { id: "epistemology", t: "Epistemology", a: "know",
    d: "The study of knowledge: what it is, how far it reaches, and what justifies belief. Its core questions concern the sources of knowledge (perception, reason, memory, testimony) and the threat of skepticism.",
    x: "Plato's Theaetetus asks what knowledge is and tests three answers. Early modern philosophy put epistemology at the center: Descartes sought certain foundations, the empiricists traced ideas to experience, and Kant asked how knowledge of objects is possible at all. Twentieth-century work turned to the analysis of knowledge after Gettier, to naturalized epistemology after Quine, and to social epistemology, which studies testimony, trust and the effects of power on who is believed.",
    s: "Plato, Theaetetus; Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641); Kant, Critique of Pure Reason (1781)",
    p: ["plato", "descartes", "hume", "kant", "quine", "fricker"], r: ["jtb", "skepticism", "empiricism", "rationalism", "epistemic-injustice"], br: ["epistemology"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Epistemology”" },
  { id: "a-priori", t: "A priori", a: "know",
    d: "Knowable independently of experience, as truths of logic and mathematics are usually taken to be. Experience may be needed to acquire the concepts involved, but the justification rests on reason alone.",
    x: "Kant made the distinction central and asked how synthetic a priori knowledge is possible. The logical empiricists held that all a priori truths are analytic, true by meaning alone. Quine doubted that any statement is immune to revision in the light of experience. Kripke separated the a priori, an epistemic notion, from necessity, a metaphysical one, and argued that some contingent truths can be known a priori.",
    s: "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, B1–3; Kripke, Naming and Necessity (1980)",
    p: ["leibniz", "kant", "quine", "kripke"], r: ["analytic-synthetic", "synthetic-a-priori", "rationalism", "necessity"], vs: ["a-posteriori"], br: ["epistemology"], k: ["a priori"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Epistemology”" },
  { id: "a-posteriori", t: "A posteriori", a: "know",
    d: "Knowable only through experience. Most of what we know about the world, from the boiling point of water to the date of a battle, is a posteriori.",
    x: "Empiricists hold that all substantive knowledge of the world is a posteriori. Kant agreed that all knowledge begins with experience, and denied that it all arises from experience. The terms first meant reasoning from effects to causes (a posteriori) and from causes to effects (a priori); their modern sense became standard with Kant.\n\nThe distinction is epistemic: it classifies truths by how they can be known. Kripke argued that some necessary truths, such as “Hesperus is Phosphorus” or “water is H2O,” can be known only a posteriori, by investigation, and that some contingent truths, such as that the standard meter stick is one meter long, can be known a priori, so that the a posteriori and the contingent come apart.",
    s: "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, B1",
    p: ["locke", "hume", "kant"], r: ["empiricism", "induction"], vs: ["a-priori"], br: ["epistemology"], k: ["a posteriori"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Epistemology”" },
  { id: "analytic-synthetic", t: "Analytic and synthetic", a: "know",
    d: "Kant's distinction. In an analytic judgment the predicate is already contained in the concept of the subject (“all bodies are extended”). A synthetic judgment adds something to it (“all bodies are heavy”).",
    x: "Hume's fork had divided knowledge into relations of ideas and matters of fact. Kant claimed that mathematics consists of synthetic judgments known a priori. Frege recast analyticity in terms of logic and definitions, and the logical empiricists treated all necessary truth as analytic. Quine's “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” argued that no principled line between analytic and synthetic statements can be drawn, a challenge that shaped later analytic philosophy.",
    s: "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, A6–7/B10–11; Quine, “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951)",
    p: ["hume", "kant", "frege", "quine"], r: ["synthetic-a-priori", "a-priori", "tautology", "logicism"], br: ["epistemology", "language", "analytic"], k: ["analytic", "synthetic"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/quine/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Willard Van Orman Quine”" },
  { id: "synthetic-a-priori", fig: "synthetic-a-priori", t: "Synthetic a priori", a: "know",
    d: "Kant's claim that some judgments add content to their concepts and are still known independently of experience, such as the propositions of arithmetic and geometry and the principle that every event has a cause.",
    x: "The question “How are synthetic judgments a priori possible?” organizes the Critique of Pure Reason. Kant's answer is that space and time are forms of our intuition and the categories are forms of our understanding, so they hold necessarily for any object we can experience. Critics argued that non-Euclidean geometry and relativity physics undercut his examples, and the logical empiricists denied that any synthetic a priori knowledge exists.",
    s: "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, B19; Prolegomena (1783), §5",
    p: ["kant"], r: ["analytic-synthetic", "transcendental", "a-priori", "noumenon"], br: ["epistemology", "german-idealism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Immanuel Kant”" },
  { id: "transcendental", t: "Transcendental", a: "know",
    d: "In Kant, concerning the conditions that make experience and knowledge possible. A transcendental argument shows that something must hold for experience to be possible at all.",
    x: "Kant calls transcendental the cognition that is occupied with our way of knowing objects, insofar as it is possible a priori. His transcendental deduction argues that the categories must apply to anything we can experience. Fichte built his system on the transcendental I, and Husserl's transcendental phenomenology studies the structures of consciousness through which any world is given. The word should be kept apart from “transcendent”, which in Kant means passing the limits of possible experience.",
    s: "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, B25, A84–130/B116–69; Husserl, Cartesian Meditations (1931)",
    p: ["kant", "fichte", "husserl"], r: ["synthetic-a-priori", "noumenon", "transcendence", "epoche", "phenomenology"], br: ["epistemology", "german-idealism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Immanuel Kant”" },
  { id: "noumenon", t: "Phenomenon and noumenon", a: "know",
    d: "Kant: phenomena are things as they appear to us under the forms of space and time and the categories. The noumenon, or thing in itself, is the thing considered apart from how it appears, which we cannot know.",
    x: "Kant distinguishes a negative sense of noumenon, a thing insofar as it is no object of our sensible intuition, from a positive sense, an object of an intellectual intuition we do not have. Things in themselves cannot be known, yet we must be able to think them, and practical reason gives grounds for belief in freedom, God and immortality. Fichte and Hegel judged the idea of an unknowable thing in itself incoherent, which set German idealism on its course. Nick Land's collected essays take their title, Fanged Noumena, from the term.",
    s: "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, A235–60/B294–315",
    p: ["kant", "fichte", "hegel", "schopenhauer", "land"], r: ["transcendental", "idealism", "synthetic-a-priori", "transcendence"], br: ["epistemology", "german-idealism"], k: ["noumenon", "noumena", "thing in itself", "things in themselves", "phenomena"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Immanuel Kant”" },
  { id: "empiricism", t: "Empiricism", a: "know",
    d: "The view that knowledge derives from sense experience. Locke held that the mind begins as a blank slate, and that all ideas come from sensation or reflection.",
    x: "Berkeley pushed empiricism toward idealism, and Hume toward skepticism about causation, induction and the self. Kant tried to reconcile empiricism with rationalism. In the twentieth century the logical empiricists joined empiricism to modern logic, and Quine's naturalism treated epistemology as a chapter of psychology.\n\nLocke distinguished simple ideas, received in sensation or reflection, from complex ideas the mind builds from them, and rejected innate ideas. Hume divided perceptions into vivid impressions and fainter ideas copied from them, and used the principle that every idea derives from an impression to test philosophical concepts such as substance and necessary connection. Mill extended empiricism to logic and mathematics, treating even them as generalizations from experience.",
    s: "Locke, An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689), II.i; Hume, An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748)",
    p: ["bacon", "locke", "berkeley", "hume", "carnap", "quine"], r: ["a-posteriori", "induction-problem", "skepticism", "foundationalism"], vs: ["rationalism"], br: ["epistemology"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/locke/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “John Locke”" },
  { id: "rationalism", t: "Rationalism", a: "know",
    d: "The view that reason can yield substantive knowledge independently of experience. Rationalists typically defend innate ideas and hold that reason grasps necessary truths about reality.",
    x: "Descartes built knowledge on clear and distinct ideas known by reason. Spinoza set out his Ethics in geometrical order, from definitions and axioms. Leibniz held that the mind contains innate principles, and grounded his metaphysics in the principle of sufficient reason: nothing is without a reason why it is so. Kant's critique of pure reason was in large part a critique of this tradition's claims about God, the soul and the world as a whole.",
    s: "Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641); Spinoza, Ethics (1677); Leibniz, New Essays on Human Understanding (written 1704)",
    p: ["descartes", "spinoza", "leibniz"], r: ["a-priori", "foundationalism", "monism", "deduction"], vs: ["empiricism"], br: ["epistemology"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/leibniz/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz”" },
  { id: "skepticism", t: "Skepticism", a: "know",
    d: "Doubt about whether knowledge is possible, in general or in some domain. Ancient Pyrrhonism suspends judgment; Descartes uses doubt as a method.",
    x: "Philosophical skepticism holds that we do not know, or are not justified in believing, things we ordinarily take ourselves to know. Peter Klein's Stanford Encyclopedia article defines skepticism about a field of propositions as the claim that suspending judgment is the only justified attitude toward them, and treats suspension as a genuine stance of its own. Skeptical positions vary in scope: some doubt only inferences about the unobserved, some doubt everything about the external world beyond one's own mind, and the most radical doubt every proposition whatever.\n\nThe ancient Greek word skepsis means inquiry, and the ancient skeptics presented themselves as inquirers. Pyrrho of Elis (c. 365/60–275/70 BCE) wrote nothing; his pupil Timon reported his pursuit of tranquility. In Plato's Academy, Arcesilaus, who became its head in the 260s BCE, and later Carneades (214–129/8 BCE) attacked the Stoic claim that some impressions guarantee their own truth, and Sextus reports that the Academics held that nothing can be known.\n\nPyrrhonism was revived in the first century BCE by Aenesidemus, who set out the Ten Modes, patterns of argument from differences among animals, people, senses, circumstances and customs. Its fullest surviving statement is the Outlines of Pyrrhonism of Sextus Empiricus (c. 160–210 CE). Pyrrhonists set equally strong arguments on each side of a question so as to suspend judgment (epochē) and reach tranquility (ataraxia), while continuing to live by appearances. The Five Modes attributed to Agrippa include a trilemma: any justification must rest on an unsupported hypothesis, run in a circle or go on forever.\n\nDescartes' dream and evil-demon arguments raise global doubt about the external world, which he then tries to answer. The modern version uses skeptical hypotheses such as a handless brain in a vat: if I am justified in believing I have hands, I must be justified in believing I am no such brain, since one claim entails the denial of the other; I cannot be justified in believing that; so I am not justified in believing I have hands. The first premise rests on a closure principle, that justification carries over to what one's justified beliefs obviously entail.\n\nHume posed a narrower skepticism about induction, since every argument that the future will resemble the past either assumes it or reasons in a circle. Hume found skeptical arguments unanswerable in theory and held that nature compels us to believe anyway. Klein sorts the replies: inductivists accept the circularity, Kant claimed a priori knowledge that nature is uniform, and Popper accepted the conclusion and held that science needs no inductive knowledge.\n\nResponses to the closure argument take several forms. G. E. Moore's “Proof of an External World” (1939) appealed to the certainty that here is a hand. Fred Dretske and Robert Nozick denied closure, Nozick through a tracking account on which knowing requires that one would not believe the proposition were it false, and Ernest Sosa proposed a safety condition in its place. Contextualists such as Keith DeRose, Stewart Cohen and David Lewis hold that the standards for “knows” rise when skeptical possibilities are raised, Crispin Wright argues that we are entitled to reject skeptical hypotheses without evidence, and James Pryor's dogmatism (2000) holds that experience justifies belief immediately.\n\nThe ancient regress argument shapes the main theories of justification. Foundationalists hold that some beliefs are justified without support from others; coherentists that justification comes from beliefs hanging together; infinitists, including Klein, that an endless chain of reasons can justify. A fourth response, which Klein calls positism, finds in Wittgenstein's late remarks On Certainty (1969) certain propositions that every practice of justifying presupposes, though they are not themselves justified.",
    s: "Sextus Empiricus, Outlines of Pyrrhonism; Descartes, Meditations I (1641); Wittgenstein, On Certainty (1969)",
    p: ["pyrrho", "sextus", "descartes", "hume", "moore", "wittgenstein"], r: ["epistemology", "foundationalism", "induction-problem", "jtb", "epoche"], br: ["epistemology", "ancient"], k: ["skepticism", "scepticism", "skeptic", "sceptic", "pyrrhonism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Skepticism”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-ancient/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ancient Skepticism”" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sextus-empiricus/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Sextus Empiricus”" }] },
  { id: "jtb", fig: "jtb", t: "Justified true belief", a: "know",
    d: "The traditional analysis of knowledge: S knows that p when p is true, S believes that p, and S is justified in believing that p.",
    x: "Plato's Theaetetus considers knowledge as true belief with an account (logos), and the Meno says true beliefs become knowledge when they are tied down by reasoning. Edmund Gettier's three-page paper of 1963 gave cases in which all three conditions are met by luck, and launched decades of attempts to add a fourth condition or to replace justification with reliability, causal connection or intellectual virtue.",
    s: "Plato, Theaetetus 201c–210b; Meno 97e–98a; Gettier, “Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?”, Analysis (1963)",
    p: ["plato", "gettier"], r: ["epistemology", "skepticism", "foundationalism", "conditions"], br: ["epistemology", "analytic"], k: ["justified true belief", "gettier"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Epistemology”" },
  { id: "foundationalism", t: "Foundationalism", a: "know",
    d: "The view that justified beliefs rest on basic beliefs, which need no further justification. Basic beliefs, such as beliefs about one's own experience or self-evident truths, stop the regress of reasons.",
    x: "Descartes' “I am, I exist” is the classic foundation. Empiricist foundationalists took reports of sense experience as the base. Wilfrid Sellars attacked the “myth of the given”, the idea that experience can justify belief without itself involving concepts, and coherentists deny that any belief is basic. Modest foundationalists today allow basic beliefs to be fallible.\n\nThe motivation is the regress argument. If every justified belief needs another belief as its reason, the chain of reasons must either go on forever, circle back on itself, or end. Foundationalists choose the end, coherentists the circle, and infinitists such as Peter Klein the endless chain; the ancient skeptics' modes of Agrippa concluded that none of these succeeds. Classical foundationalism required basic beliefs to be infallible, self-evident or incorrigible. Alvin Plantinga, rejecting that standard, argued that belief in God can be properly basic, rational without being inferred from other beliefs.",
    s: "Descartes, Meditations II (1641); Sellars, “Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind” (1956)",
    p: ["descartes", "sellars"], r: ["skepticism", "jtb", "rationalism", "empiricism"], vs: ["coherentism"], br: ["epistemology"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Epistemology”" },
  { id: "coherentism", t: "Coherentism", a: "know",
    d: "The view that a belief is justified by how well it fits within a coherent system of beliefs. No belief is basic; justification is a matter of mutual support.",
    x: "Otto Neurath compared us to sailors who must rebuild their ship on the open sea, never able to take it into dry dock and start from the bottom. Quine's web of belief, in which any statement can be revised to keep the whole in order, is a coherentist picture. Critics ask how a coherent system connects to the world, since a well-made fiction can be perfectly coherent.\n\nCoherence requires consistency, and also inferential and explanatory connections that let beliefs support one another. Donald Davidson's “A Coherence Theory of Truth and Knowledge” (1983) argued that nothing can count as a reason for holding a belief except another belief, and Laurence BonJour's The Structure of Empirical Knowledge (1985) gave the most developed defense of the view, which BonJour later abandoned for a form of foundationalism. Two standard objections are the isolation objection, that a coherent system might float free of experience, and the alternative-systems objection, that many incompatible systems could be equally coherent.",
    s: "Neurath, “Protocol Sentences” (1932); Quine, “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951)",
    p: ["neurath", "quine"], r: ["jtb", "hermeneutic-circle"], vs: ["foundationalism"], br: ["epistemology"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/neurath/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Otto Neurath”" },
  { id: "induction-problem", t: "Problem of induction", a: "know",
    d: "Hume's argument that inferences from observed cases to unobserved ones can be given no justification that avoids circularity.",
    x: "The problem of induction asks what justifies the step from observed cases to unobserved ones: from the sun's rising every day so far to its rising tomorrow, or from samples to general laws. David Hume posed it in A Treatise of Human Nature (1739) and again, more briefly, in An Enquiry concerning Human Understanding (1748). Causal reasoning, he argued, rests on experience of constant conjunction: having found that gunpowder has always exploded when lit, we expect the next lighting to do the same, and the question is what reasoning carries us past what we have seen.\n\nHume's answer took the form of a dilemma. All reasoning concerns either relations of ideas, which can be demonstrated, or matters of fact, which can only be made probable. No demonstration can show that the future will resemble the past, since its denial involves no contradiction. Any probable argument for it already assumes that resemblance, which Leah Henderson's Stanford Encyclopedia article calls the Uniformity Principle, or else appeals to the past success of induction, which is itself an inductive inference. Hume concluded that our expectations rest on custom or habit, a natural instinct of the mind.\n\nSome responses reject the first horn. Kant argued in 1781 and 1783 that synthetic knowledge a priori is possible, because experience itself depends on categories of the understanding, causation among them. D. M. Armstrong, Laurence BonJour and John Foster argued that the best explanation of observed regularities is a law of nature that also covers unobserved cases, though critics reply that inference to the best explanation may be a form of induction too.\n\nProbability theory offered another route. Thomas Bayes's posthumous essay (1764) and Laplace's rule of succession (1814) show how evidence should raise or lower our confidence in hypotheses, and Rudolf Carnap and Bruno de Finetti refined the machinery. Henderson concludes that these results always depend on prior probabilities and assumptions about the world, so they clarify what induction presupposes and leave Hume's question open.\n\nOther responses accept a kind of circularity. James Van Cleve (1984) and David Papineau (1992) argue that a rule can be shown reliable by using that same rule, though a counterinductive rule can be made to support itself in just the same way. P. F. Strawson argued that asking whether induction is reasonable is like asking whether it is reasonable to proportion belief to evidence, since that is part of what being reasonable means. John Norton's material theory holds that each inductive inference is licensed by particular facts about its subject matter.\n\nHans Reichenbach gave induction a pragmatic vindication: if any method can find the limiting frequency of a series, following the straight rule of induction will eventually find it too. Critics pointed out that many other rules also converge in the long run, which says little about the short run where we actually live, and Gerhard Schurz's work on meta-induction extends the strategy. Karl Popper accepted Hume's conclusion and gave science a method of bold conjecture and attempted falsification that needs no inductive inference at all.\n\nNelson Goodman's “new riddle” in Fact, Fiction, and Forecast (1955) showed that the problem runs deeper than Hume saw. Call an object grue if it is examined before some future time and found green, or is otherwise blue. Every emerald examined so far is both green and grue, so the same evidence seems to support the prediction that future emeralds will be green and the prediction that they will be blue. Goodman asked why “green” supports predictions while an invented predicate such as “grue” fails to, and his question about which predicates are projectible remains open.",
    s: "Hume, Treatise of Human Nature (1739), I.iii.6; Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), §4; Goodman, Fact, Fiction, and Forecast (1955)",
    p: ["hume", "popper", "goodman"], r: ["induction", "falsifiability", "occasionalism", "skepticism"], br: ["epistemology", "science"], k: ["problem of induction"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/induction-problem/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “The Problem of Induction”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Skepticism”" }] },
  { id: "falsifiability", t: "Falsifiability", a: "know",
    d: "Popper's criterion for scientific theories: a theory is scientific if some possible observation could refute it. Bold theories that rule out many outcomes say more about the world and are easier to test.",
    x: "Popper offered it as a solution to the problem of demarcation and as an answer to Hume: science proceeds by conjectures and refutations, and needs no inductive confirmation. He argued that Marxism and psychoanalysis, as their followers practiced them, could explain everything and so risked nothing. Kuhn and Lakatos replied that scientists rarely abandon a theory at the first anomaly, and the Duhem–Quine thesis notes that a failed prediction refutes a whole group of hypotheses at once.",
    s: "Popper, The Logic of Scientific Discovery (1934; English 1959); Conjectures and Refutations (1963), ch. 1",
    p: ["popper", "kuhn", "quine"], r: ["induction-problem", "paradigm", "abduction", "modus"], br: ["science", "epistemology"], k: ["falsifiability", "falsification", "falsifiable", "demarcation"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/popper/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Popper”" },
  { id: "paradigm", img: { file: "Duck-Rabbit_illusion.jpg", alt: "Drawing that can be seen as a duck or as a rabbit", cap: "The duck-rabbit, from Fliegende Blätter (1892). Kuhn: “What were ducks in the scientist's world before the revolution are rabbits afterwards.” Public domain, via" }, t: "Paradigm", a: "know",
    d: "Kuhn: a scientific community's shared framework of exemplary achievements, theories, methods and standards, which defines what counts as a problem and a solution during a period of “normal science”.",
    x: "When anomalies pile up, a crisis can end in a scientific revolution, such as the move from Ptolemaic to Copernican astronomy or from Newtonian to relativistic physics. Kuhn argued that rival paradigms are incommensurable: they share no neutral standard of comparison, and scientists on either side in some sense work in different worlds. Critics charged him with relativism, and in a 1969 postscript he replaced “paradigm” with “disciplinary matrix” and “exemplar”.",
    s: "Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962; postscript 1969)",
    p: ["kuhn", "popper"], r: ["falsifiability", "episteme", "social-construction"], br: ["science", "epistemology"], k: ["paradigm", "paradigm shift", "normal science", "incommensurability"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/thomas-kuhn/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Thomas Kuhn”" },
  { id: "pragmatic-maxim", t: "Pragmatic maxim", a: "know",
    d: "Peirce's rule for making ideas clear: consider what practical effects the object of a conception could have; our conception of those effects is the whole of our conception of the object.",
    x: "Peirce's example is hardness: to call a thing hard is to say that many other substances will fail to scratch it. James turned the maxim into a theory of truth, on which true ideas are those we can assimilate, validate, corroborate and verify; Peirce, unhappy with this, renamed his own view “pragmaticism”. Dewey extended it into a theory of inquiry as the resolution of problematic situations.",
    s: "Peirce, “How to Make Our Ideas Clear” (1878); James, Pragmatism (1907), Lecture VI; Dewey, Logic: The Theory of Inquiry (1938)",
    p: ["peirce", "james", "dewey"], r: ["abduction", "induction", "jtb"], br: ["pragmatism", "epistemology"], k: ["pragmatism", "pragmatist", "pragmatic maxim"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pragmatism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Pragmatism”" },
  { id: "ethnophilosophy", t: "Ethnophilosophy", a: "know",
    d: "The attempt to reconstruct a people's collective worldview, as expressed in language, proverbs, myths and customs, and to present it as their philosophy. The name was given critically to work in the line of Placide Tempels' Bantu Philosophy (1945).",
    x: "Paulin Hountondji argued that ethnophilosophy treats philosophy as an unwritten system held unanimously by a whole people, which no one actually argues for, and that African philosophy should mean the critical, written work of African philosophers themselves. Kwasi Wiredu called for conceptual decolonization, examining concepts inherited through colonial languages. Defenders replied that oral traditions contain real argument, and Odera Oruka's sage philosophy sought out individual thinkers within those traditions.",
    s: "Tempels, Bantu Philosophy (1945); Hountondji, African Philosophy: Myth and Reality (1976); Wiredu, Conceptual Decolonization in African Philosophy (1995); Oruka, Sage Philosophy (1990)",
    p: ["tempels", "hountondji", "wiredu", "oruka"], r: ["personhood", "negritude", "subaltern", "epistemic-injustice"], br: ["african", "epistemology"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-sage/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Sage Philosophy”" },
  { id: "situated-knowledges", t: "Situated knowledges", a: "know",
    d: "Haraway: all knowledge is produced from particular, embodied positions, and objectivity means taking responsibility for that location. Her target is the “god trick”, a view that claims to see everything from nowhere.",
    x: "Haraway rejects both objectivism and relativism, since each lets knowers escape responsibility for the representations they construct; she treats relativism as the mirror image of the totalizing view it opposes. Knowledge is to be built by acknowledging the situated choices that go into it. Her subtitle answers Sandra Harding's The Science Question in Feminism (1986).",
    s: "Haraway, “Situated Knowledges: The Science Question in Feminism and the Privilege of Partial Perspective”, Feminist Studies 14, no. 3 (1988)",
    p: ["haraway", "harding"], r: ["standpoint", "cyborg", "epistemic-injustice"], br: ["feminist", "epistemology", "science"], k: ["situated knowledge", "situated knowledges", "god trick"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Feminist Epistemology and Philosophy of Science” (Elizabeth Anderson)" },
  // Logic
  { id: "validity", fig: "validity", t: "Validity", a: "logic",
    d: "An argument is valid when the truth of its premises guarantees the truth of its conclusion. Validity depends on form: “All A are B; x is A; so x is B” is valid whatever A and B are.",
    x: "A valid argument may have false premises and a false conclusion; the one combination validity rules out is true premises with a false conclusion. Aristotle's syllogistic set out valid forms as schemes, every instance of which is valid. Modern logic gives two accounts of validity: model-theoretic (no model makes the premises true and the conclusion false) and proof-theoretic (the conclusion can be derived by the rules of a proof system). A system is sound when everything it proves is valid and complete when it proves everything valid; Gödel's completeness theorem, from his 1929 dissertation, shows that first-order logic is complete.",
    s: "Aristotle, Prior Analytics; Gödel, completeness theorem (1930)",
    p: ["aristotle", "frege", "godel"], r: ["soundness", "deduction", "modus", "tautology"], br: ["logic"], k: ["validity", "valid argument", "invalid"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-consequence/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Logical Consequence” (Jc Beall, Greg Restall and Gil Sagi)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goedel/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Kurt Gödel” (Juliette Kennedy)" }] },
  { id: "soundness", t: "Soundness", a: "logic",
    d: "A sound argument is valid and has true premises, so its conclusion is true.",
    x: "Validity is a matter of logic alone; soundness also requires that the premises be true, which logic by itself cannot settle. In metalogic, a proof system is called sound when everything it proves is valid, and complete when it proves everything that is valid.\n\nPhilosophical arguments are usually attacked in one of two ways: by showing that the conclusion does not follow, which challenges validity, or by denying a premise, which challenges soundness. G. E. Moore's “Proof of an External World” (1939) rests on soundness: holding up his hands and saying “Here is one hand” and “here is another”, he concluded that external things exist, and called the proof rigorous because its premises entail its conclusion and he knew them to be true.",
    p: ["aristotle"], r: ["validity", "deduction"], br: ["logic"], k: ["soundness", "sound argument"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-consequence/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Logical Consequence” (Jc Beall, Greg Restall and Gil Sagi)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “George Edward Moore” (Tom Baldwin)" }] },
  { id: "deduction", t: "Deduction", a: "logic",
    d: "Reasoning in which the conclusion follows necessarily from the premises: if the premises are true, the conclusion cannot be false.",
    x: "Deduction is not ampliative: its conclusion makes explicit what the premises already contain, which is the price of its certainty. Inferences that go beyond their premises, such as induction and abduction (inference to the best explanation), can be strong without being certain.\n\nModern logic studies deduction through formal systems. A proof system is sound if everything it proves is valid and complete if it proves everything that is valid, and Gödel showed in his 1929 dissertation that first-order logic is complete. His incompleteness theorems, published in 1931, showed by contrast that any consistent formal system in which a certain amount of arithmetic can be carried out is incomplete, and cannot prove its own consistency.",
    p: ["aristotle", "descartes", "spinoza"], r: ["validity", "abduction", "modus", "rationalism"], vs: ["induction"], br: ["logic"], k: ["deduction", "deductive"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-consequence/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Logical Consequence” (Jc Beall, Greg Restall and Gil Sagi)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/abduction/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Abduction” (Igor Douven)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goedel-incompleteness/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gödel's Incompleteness Theorems” (Panu Raatikainen)" }] },
  { id: "induction", t: "Induction", a: "logic",
    d: "Reasoning from observed cases to general conclusions or to unobserved cases. Its conclusions are probable at best.",
    x: "Enumerative induction generalizes from instances: every observed raven is black, so all ravens are black. Statistical induction moves from samples to populations. Mill set out methods for isolating causes by comparing cases, among them the methods of agreement and of difference. Hume's problem asks what justifies any of this, and Bayesian approaches treat induction as the updating of degrees of belief on evidence.\n\nCarl Hempel's paradox of the ravens (1937, 1945) shows how puzzling confirmation can be: “all ravens are black” is logically equivalent to “all non-black things are non-ravens”, so a green apple or a shoe seems to confirm it. Popper denied that science uses induction at all, describing it as a matter of bold conjectures and attempted refutations. Hans Reichenbach offered a pragmatic vindication: induction is a necessary condition of success, so if any method of prediction can succeed, induction will. P. F. Strawson replied that asking for a general justification of induction is confused, since being reasonable just means proportioning belief to evidence of this kind. Goodman's new riddle asks why we project “green” and not “grue”.",
    s: "Bacon, Novum Organum (1620); Mill, A System of Logic (1843), III.viii",
    p: ["bacon", "hume", "mill"], r: ["induction-problem", "abduction", "falsifiability", "a-posteriori"], vs: ["deduction"], br: ["logic", "science"], k: ["induction", "inductive"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/induction-problem/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “The Problem of Induction” (Leah Henderson)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confirmation/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Confirmation” (Vincenzo Crupi)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/popper/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Popper” (Stephen Thornton)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mill/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “John Stuart Mill” (Christopher Macleod)" }] },
  { id: "abduction", fig: "abduction", t: "Abduction", a: "logic",
    d: "Inference to the best explanation: from a surprising fact, infer the hypothesis that would best explain it.",
    x: "Peirce described abduction as the only kind of reasoning that introduces new ideas: “The surprising fact, C, is observed; But if A were true, C would be a matter of course, Hence, there is reason to suspect that A is true.” Gilbert Harman named the pattern “inference to the best explanation” in 1965. Explanatory virtues such as simplicity, scope and fit with background knowledge decide which hypothesis is best.",
    s: "Peirce, Harvard Lectures on Pragmatism (1903), CP 5.189; Harman, “The Inference to the Best Explanation” (1965)",
    p: ["peirce", "harman"], r: ["induction", "deduction", "pragmatic-maxim", "falsifiability"], br: ["logic", "science", "pragmatism"], k: ["abduction", "abductive", "inference to the best explanation"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/peirce/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Charles Sanders Peirce”" },
  { id: "conditions", fig: "conditions", t: "Necessary and sufficient conditions", a: "logic",
    d: "A is necessary for B when B cannot hold without A. A is sufficient for B when A's holding guarantees B. Oxygen is necessary for fire; being a square is sufficient for being a rectangle.",
    x: "In logical notation, A is sufficient for B when “if A then B” holds, and necessary for B when “if B then A” holds. Stating necessary and sufficient conditions has traditionally been the business of philosophical analysis, as in the traditional analysis of knowledge as justified true belief. Wittgenstein rejected definitions of this kind for many concepts: there is no essential definition of “game”, only a network of overlapping similarities, a “family resemblance”.\n\nConfusing the two produces a classic fallacy. From “if A then B” and B, one cannot infer A; affirming the consequent treats a necessary condition as if it were sufficient. When A is both necessary and sufficient for B, the two hold “if and only if” each other, the form a full definition takes. Gettier's cases (1963) showed that justified true belief falls short of being sufficient for knowledge. In the analysis of causation, J. L. Mackie proposed that what we call a cause is usually an INUS condition: an insufficient but necessary part of a condition which is itself unnecessary but sufficient for the result.",
    s: "Wittgenstein, Philosophical Investigations (1953), §§66–67",
    p: ["wittgenstein"], r: ["jtb", "modus", "validity"], br: ["logic"], k: ["necessary condition", "sufficient condition"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/necessary-sufficient/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Necessary and Sufficient Conditions” (Andrew Brennan)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “The Analysis of Knowledge” (Jonathan Jenkins Ichikawa and Matthias Steup)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ludwig Wittgenstein” (Anat Biletzki and Anat Matar)" }] },
  { id: "modus", fig: "modus", t: "Modus ponens and modus tollens", a: "logic",
    d: "Modus ponens: if P then Q; P; therefore Q. Modus tollens: if P then Q; not Q; therefore not P.",
    x: "Two invalid look-alikes are common errors: affirming the consequent (if P then Q; Q; therefore P) and denying the antecedent (if P then Q; not P; therefore not Q). A falsifying test has the form of modus tollens: if the theory is true, this will be observed; it is not observed; so the theory is false.\n\nThe names are Latin: modus ponens, the mode that affirms, and modus tollens, the mode that denies. Both forms are among the Stoics' five indemonstrables, the basic arguments of their logic: the first concludes the consequent from a conditional and its antecedent, the second the contradictory of the antecedent from a conditional and the contradictory of its consequent. A well-known saying holds that one philosopher's modus ponens is another's modus tollens: from “if p then q,” a person confident of p concludes q, while one confident that q is false concludes that p is false.",
    p: [], r: ["validity", "deduction", "falsifiability", "reductio"], br: ["logic"], k: ["modus ponens", "modus tollens", "affirming the consequent"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-ancient/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ancient Logic” (Susanne Bobzien)" }] },
  { id: "reductio", fig: "reductio", t: "Reductio ad absurdum", a: "logic",
    d: "Proving a claim by showing that its denial leads to a contradiction or an absurdity.",
    x: "The Pythagorean proof that a square's diagonal is incommensurable with its side (that the square root of 2 is irrational) is a classic example. Zeno defended Parmenides by reductio, arguing that absurdities follow from the denial of his views, that is, from plurality and motion. A weaker form derives from a view a consequence that is only implausible or anomalous, rather than a contradiction.\n\nReductio is the basis of indirect proof: to prove p, assume not-p and derive a contradiction. Intuitionistic logic, which abstracts from Brouwer's mathematics, keeps the step from a contradiction derived from p to not-p, but gives up excluded middle and double negation elimination, so for intuitionists reductio only proves negative statements.",
    p: ["parmenides"], r: ["modus", "tautology", "validity"], br: ["logic"], k: ["reductio", "reductio ad absurdum"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/reductio/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Reductio ad Absurdum” (Nicholas Rescher)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paradox-zeno/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Zeno's Paradoxes” (Nick Huggett)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-intuitionistic/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Intuitionistic Logic” (Joan Moschovakis)" }] },
  { id: "begging", fig: "begging", t: "Begging the question", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["This newspaper is reliable, because it says it only prints the truth, and it's reliable.", "Smoking is unhealthy because it is bad for your health."],
    d: "Assuming in the premises what the argument sets out to prove (petitio principii).",
    x: "The Latin petitio principii renders Aristotle's name for it. A question-begging argument is not faulted for invalidity: its fault is that it gives anyone who doubts the conclusion no reason to accept it. In everyday English “begs the question” often means “raises the question”, a usage logicians resist.\n\nIt is an informal fallacy: the argument's form may be valid, and the fault lies in the relation between its premises and the conclusion at issue. Whether an argument begs the question depends on the context of argument: it smuggles in as settled a question that remains open between the parties.",
    s: "Aristotle, Prior Analytics II.16",
    p: ["aristotle"], r: ["validity", "induction-problem"], br: ["logic"], k: ["begging the question", "petitio principii", "circular argument"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)" },
  { id: "equivocation", t: "Equivocation", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["The end of a thing is its perfection; death is the end of life; so death is the perfection of life.", "Banks are beside rivers; my money is in a bank; so my money is beside a river."],
    d: "Using a word in two different senses within one argument: “A feather is light; what is light cannot be dark; so a feather cannot be dark.”",
    x: "Equivocation can make an invalid argument look valid, because the premises are true only on different readings of the shared term. Philosophical disputes often turn on whether a key term, such as “freedom”, “nature” or “law”, keeps one sense throughout.\n\nThe study of fallacies begins with Aristotle's Sophistical Refutations, which lists equivocation first among the fallacies that depend on language. It is an informal fallacy: the fault lies in the meaning of the words, so it cannot be detected from the argument's form alone. Fixing the sense of a term, by definition or by distinguishing senses, is the usual remedy.",
    p: ["aristotle"], r: ["category-mistake", "validity"], br: ["logic", "language"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)" },
  { id: "category-mistake", t: "Category mistake", a: "logic",
    d: "Ryle's term for attributing to something a property that belongs to things of a different logical type. His example is a visitor who sees Oxford's colleges, libraries and offices and then asks where the University is.",
    x: "Ryle argued that Cartesian dualism makes this kind of error, treating the mind as an extra thing alongside the body, the “ghost in the machine”. He proposed that talk of mental states is largely talk of dispositions to behave. The idea of logical types comes from Russell's theory of types, which was designed to block the paradoxes of set theory.\n\nRyle's examples include a foreigner who watches a cricket match, identifies the bowlers, batsmen and fielders, and then asks who contributes team spirit. The Concept of Mind treats much traditional philosophy of mind as a category mistake of this kind, and analyses mental concepts in terms of dispositions and the manner of performances, as when a clever person acts intelligently without first performing a separate inner act of thinking.",
    s: "Ryle, The Concept of Mind (1949), ch. 1",
    p: ["ryle", "russell"], r: ["dualism", "equivocation"], br: ["logic", "mind", "analytic"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ryle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilbert Ryle”" },
  { id: "tautology", fig: "tautology", t: "Tautology and contradiction", a: "logic",
    d: "A tautology is true in virtue of its logical form alone. A contradiction asserts and denies the same thing.",
    x: "Wittgenstein's Tractatus argued that the propositions of logic are tautologies: they say nothing about the world, which is why they are certain. Truth tables test for tautology by checking every assignment of truth values. Aristotle defended the principle of non-contradiction, that the same attribute cannot at once belong and not belong to the same thing in the same respect, as the firmest of all principles; Hegel's dialectic gives contradiction a productive role in thought.",
    s: "Wittgenstein, Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921), 4.46–4.4661; Aristotle, Metaphysics IV.3",
    p: ["aristotle", "wittgenstein", "hegel"], r: ["validity", "analytic-synthetic", "dialectic", "reductio"], br: ["logic"], k: ["tautology", "contradiction", "non-contradiction"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ludwig Wittgenstein”" },
  { id: "logicism", t: "Logicism", a: "logic",
    d: "The program of reducing mathematics to logic: defining mathematical concepts in purely logical terms and deriving mathematical truths from logical laws.",
    x: "Frege tried to derive arithmetic from logic in The Foundations of Arithmetic and Basic Laws of Arithmetic. In 1902 Russell wrote to him that one of his basic laws led to a contradiction, the set of all sets that are not members of themselves. Russell and Whitehead's Principia Mathematica rebuilt the program with a theory of types, at the cost of axioms whose logical status was doubtful. Gödel's incompleteness theorems (1931) showed that no consistent formal system of this kind can prove every arithmetical truth.",
    s: "Frege, The Foundations of Arithmetic (1884); Whitehead and Russell, Principia Mathematica (1910–13); Gödel, “On Formally Undecidable Propositions” (1931)",
    p: ["frege", "russell", "godel"], r: ["a-priori", "analytic-synthetic", "tautology", "validity"], br: ["mathematics", "logic", "analytic"], k: ["logicism", "russell's paradox"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-mathematics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Philosophy of Mathematics”" },
  // Fallacies (fal: formal or informal), from the Stanford and Internet Encyclopedias' entries on fallacies
  { id: "affirming-consequent", t: "Affirming the consequent", a: "logic", fal: "formal", ex: ["If he's a surgeon, he went to university. He went to university. So he's a surgeon.", "If the theory is right, the experiment will show a spike. It showed a spike, so the theory is right."],
    d: "Inferring the “if” part of a conditional from its “then” part: if P then Q; Q; so P. “If it rained, the street is wet; the street is wet; so it rained.” It is invalid, since something else may have wet the street.",
    x: "It looks like modus ponens run backwards (modus ponens goes: if P then Q; P; so Q). A truth table shows the gap: the row where P is false and Q is true makes both premises true and the conclusion false. As a formal fallacy it fails because of its form alone, whatever P and Q are about.\n\nThe pattern still has a use. Finding what a hypothesis predicts is evidence for the hypothesis, and much reasoning in science and daily life, inference to the best explanation, has this shape. The mistake is treating that evidence as proof. Aristotle listed a version of it, the fallacy of the consequent, among the fallacies outside language: assuming that because one thing follows from another, the second must bring the first with it.",
    s: "Aristotle, Sophistical Refutations 5",
    p: ["aristotle"], r: ["validity", "denying-antecedent", "deduction"], vs: ["modus"], br: ["logic"], k: ["affirming the consequent", "fallacy of the consequent"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)" }] },
  { id: "denying-antecedent", t: "Denying the antecedent", a: "logic", fal: "formal", ex: ["If it's a sonnet, it has fourteen lines. This poem isn't a sonnet, so it doesn't have fourteen lines.", "If you study, you'll pass. You didn't study, so you won't pass."],
    d: "Inferring that the “then” part of a conditional is false because its “if” part is: if P then Q; not P; so not Q. “If it's a dog, it's a mammal; it isn't a dog; so it isn't a mammal.” A cat shows the conclusion can fail.",
    x: "It looks like modus tollens (if P then Q; not Q; so not P). On a truth table the counterexample is the row where P is false and Q true: both premises hold and the conclusion fails. A conditional says only that P is enough for Q, not that P is needed; the argument would be valid only with the different premise “only if P, Q”.\n\nIt often slips in when a conditional is heard as a biconditional, as when “if you finish your work, you can go out” is taken to mean “you can go out only if you finish your work”.",
    p: [], r: ["validity", "affirming-consequent", "deduction"], vs: ["modus"], br: ["logic"], k: ["denying the antecedent"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" },
  { id: "ad-hominem", t: "Ad hominem", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["Why take his theory of justice seriously? He was fired from two jobs.", "She says we should all fly less, but she flies to a conference every month, so her argument fails."],
    d: "Rejecting a claim or argument by attacking the person who makes it instead of what they say: their character (abusive), their situation or interests (circumstantial), or their failure to live by it (tu quoque, “you too”).",
    x: "Whether an appeal to the person is fallacious turns on relevance. Someone's character has no bearing on whether their proof is valid, but a witness's honesty bears on whether to believe their testimony, and a conflict of interest is a reason to check a claim more carefully, though not a reason to think it false.\n\nThe Latin name comes from Locke's Essay, where an argument ad hominem meant something else: pressing someone with what follows from their own principles or admissions. Locke grouped it with appeals to authority and to ignorance, as ways people press others to agree without giving evidence for the point itself, and contrasted all three with argument ad judicium, from proofs drawn from knowledge.",
    s: "Locke, An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1690), IV.xvii.19–22",
    p: ["locke"], r: ["appeal-authority", "genetic-fallacy", "straw-man"], br: ["logic"], k: ["ad hominem", "tu quoque"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" }] },
  { id: "straw-man", t: "Straw man", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["My opponent wants to cut the police budget, so she wants a city with no law at all.", "Utilitarians say happiness is what matters, so they must think a life of drugs is the best life."],
    d: "Refuting a distorted or weakened version of an opponent's position, one easier to knock down than what they actually hold, and then claiming to have refuted them.",
    x: "The remedy is charity: restate an opposing view in a form its defenders would accept, ideally its strongest form, before criticizing it. Refuting the distortion leaves the real position untouched, which makes the straw man a species of irrelevant conclusion: it proves something other than what was at issue.\n\nSchopenhauer's Art of Being Right, a catalog of debating tricks published after his death, opens with a related move: stretching an opponent's claim beyond its natural limits, so that it becomes sweeping and easy to attack, while narrowing one's own.",
    s: "Schopenhauer, The Art of Being Right (Eristische Dialektik, written c. 1830), stratagem 1",
    p: ["schopenhauer"], r: ["irrelevant-conclusion", "ad-hominem"], br: ["logic"], k: ["straw man", "strawman"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" }] },
  { id: "appeal-authority", t: "Appeal to authority", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["A famous actor says this vaccine is unsafe, so it is.", "My physics professor says raising the minimum wage hurts workers, so it must."],
    d: "Accepting a claim because someone said it, when they are not an authority on the matter, when the experts on it disagree, or when the claim could be checked directly.",
    x: "Relying on real expertise is no fallacy; most of what anyone knows is taken on trust. The questions are whether the person is an authority in the relevant field, whether the claim falls inside it, and whether those who are authorities agree. A physicist's opinion on economics carries no special weight.\n\nLocke named the argument ad verecundiam, “to modesty”: pressing someone to accept an opinion out of respect for the learning or standing of those who hold it, so that disagreeing seems presumptuous. The name has stayed, with its sense widened to any misplaced appeal to authority.",
    s: "Locke, An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1690), IV.xvii.19",
    p: ["locke"], r: ["ad-populum", "ad-hominem"], br: ["logic"], k: ["appeal to authority", "ad verecundiam"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" }] },
  { id: "appeal-ignorance", t: "Appeal to ignorance", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["No one has proved the drug is harmful, so it's safe.", "Nobody has ever shown that free will is an illusion, so we have it."],
    d: "Concluding that a claim is false because it hasn't been proved, or true because it hasn't been disproved: “No one has shown that ghosts don't exist, so they do.”",
    x: "The fault is shifting the burden of proof onto whoever doubts the claim. The inference is reasonable when the search has been thorough and the evidence would very likely have turned up if the claim were true: a careful search of a small room that finds no keys is good reason to think they aren't there. Courts adopt a rule of this shape on purpose, treating an accused person as not guilty unless guilt is proved.\n\nLocke's argument ad ignorantiam was again different: requiring an opponent to accept one's view or else offer a better one, as if the lack of an alternative proved it.",
    s: "Locke, An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1690), IV.xvii.20",
    p: ["locke"], r: ["hasty-generalization", "begging"], br: ["logic"], k: ["appeal to ignorance", "ad ignorantiam", "argument from ignorance"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)" }] },
  { id: "ad-populum", t: "Appeal to popularity", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["Most people believe in luck, so there must be something to it.", "Millions of readers can't be wrong: this diet works."],
    d: "Taking the fact that many people believe or do something as proof that it is true or right: “Everyone uses it, so it must work.”",
    x: "Widespread agreement can be evidence when the people agreeing are in a position to know and judged independently. It is no evidence when they are following one another, or when the question is one that popularity cannot settle. A related form, playing to the gallery, sways an audience through its feelings and loyalties instead of reasons.",
    p: [], r: ["appeal-authority"], br: ["logic"], k: ["ad populum", "appeal to popularity", "bandwagon"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)" },
  { id: "false-dilemma", t: "False dilemma", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["Either you support every part of this bill or you don't care about the poor.", "We either cut taxes now or the economy collapses."],
    d: "Presenting too few options, often just two, as if one of them must be chosen, when there are others: “Either we ban cars from the center or the city will choke.”",
    x: "The test is whether the list of options is fair. Some choices really are between two (give or don't give), and a genuine dilemma can be a strong argument; the fallacy lies in an unfairly short list. It is also called black-or-white thinking or a false dichotomy.",
    p: [], r: ["slippery-slope"], br: ["logic"], k: ["false dilemma", "false dichotomy", "black-or-white"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" },
  { id: "slippery-slope", t: "Slippery slope", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["If we let students redo one exam, soon they'll redo every exam, grades will mean nothing, and degrees will be worthless.", "Allow one food truck downtown and before long the restaurants will close and the street will be a parking lot."],
    d: "Arguing that a first step will lead, through a chain of further steps, to an unacceptable result, and so must not be taken.",
    x: "The form is not fallacious in itself. A slippery-slope argument is as strong as the likelihood that each step leads to the next, and those likelihoods multiply: five steps that are each 80 percent likely give an outcome only about 33 percent likely. The fallacy lies in asserting the chain without support for its links, or in treating steps that are merely possible as inevitable.",
    p: [], r: ["false-dilemma", "hasty-generalization"], br: ["logic"], k: ["slippery slope"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" },
  { id: "hasty-generalization", t: "Hasty generalization", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["The first two philosophy books I read were dull, so philosophy is dull.", "My grandfather smoked and lived to ninety-five, so smoking can't be that harmful."],
    d: "Drawing a general conclusion from too few cases, or from cases that aren't representative: meeting two rude people from a town and concluding that its people are rude.",
    x: "It overrates the strength of an inductive argument. How many cases are enough depends on how varied the population is and how the cases were chosen; a small random sample can support more than a large biased one. Mill's System of Logic, whose fifth book is on fallacies, treats faulty generalization as one of its main kinds.\n\nQuick conclusions are not always errors. When more evidence would be impractical or too costly to gather, acting on a small sample can be reasonable, as long as its weakness is kept in mind.",
    s: "Mill, A System of Logic (1843), Book V",
    p: ["mill"], r: ["induction", "post-hoc"], br: ["logic"], k: ["hasty generalization", "hasty generalisation", "overgeneralization"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)" }] },
  { id: "post-hoc", t: "False cause (post hoc)", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["Crime fell the year the new mayor took office, so her policies cut crime.", "I took the vitamin and my cold was gone two days later, so the vitamin cured it."],
    d: "Concluding that one thing caused another because it came first: “I wore my lucky scarf and we won, so the scarf helped.” In full, post hoc ergo propter hoc, “after this, therefore because of this”.",
    x: "Coming first is necessary for being a cause but far from enough. A cause has to be told apart from coincidence and from a third thing producing both events, which is what controlled comparison is for. The related fallacy cum hoc ergo propter hoc infers causation from two things merely occurring together, whatever their order.\n\nHume's analysis of causation, which finds in experience only succession and constant conjunction, makes the question of what more a causal claim asserts one of philosophy's standing problems.",
    p: ["hume"], r: ["hasty-generalization", "induction-problem"], br: ["logic"], k: ["post hoc", "false cause", "cum hoc"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" }] },
  { id: "composition-division", t: "Composition and division", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["Each part of this engine is light, so the engine is light.", "The orchestra is world-class, so every violinist in it is world-class."],
    d: "Composition: concluding that a whole has a property because its parts do (“every player is excellent, so the team is excellent”). Division: concluding that the parts have a property because the whole does (“the company is profitable, so each branch is”).",
    x: "Some properties do pass between parts and wholes: if every brick is red, the wall is red. Others don't: a team of stars can play badly together, and a heavy machine can be built from light parts. The fallacy is assuming the transfer without reason to think the property is of a kind that carries over.",
    p: [], r: ["category-mistake"], br: ["logic"], k: ["fallacy of composition", "fallacy of division"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" }] },
  { id: "genetic-fallacy", t: "Genetic fallacy", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["The idea of human rights came out of European thought, so it can't apply anywhere else.", "That argument was first made by a tobacco company, so it must be false."],
    d: "Judging a claim true or false by where it came from instead of by the evidence for it: “That idea began as a myth, so it can't be right.”",
    x: "Origins are sometimes relevant. A source with a record of errors, or one that stands to profit, is a reason to check a claim more carefully, and in the history of ideas a belief's origin is often the point of interest. What doesn't follow is that a claim's truth is settled by its source.\n\nThe question matters for genealogical method, which traces how values and practices came about (Nietzsche's On the Genealogy of Morality, Foucault's histories): whether, and how, such a history bears on their validity is a standing question about the method.",
    p: ["nietzsche", "foucault"], r: ["ad-hominem"], br: ["logic"], k: ["genetic fallacy"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" },
  { id: "many-questions", t: "Many questions", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["“Have you stopped copying your essays from the internet?”", "“Why do you always ignore the evidence?”, asked in a debate where no one has shown that you do."],
    d: "A question that presupposes something unproved, so that any direct answer concedes it: “Have you stopped cheating at cards?” Yes and no both admit to having cheated. Also called a loaded or complex question.",
    x: "Aristotle listed it among the fallacies outside language in the Sophistical Refutations: making two questions into one, so that a single answer is demanded where two are needed. The reply is to reject the presupposition, or to split the question and answer each part.",
    s: "Aristotle, Sophistical Refutations 5",
    p: ["aristotle"], r: ["begging"], br: ["logic"], k: ["many questions", "loaded question", "complex question"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)" },
  { id: "irrelevant-conclusion", t: "Irrelevant conclusion", a: "logic", fal: "informal", ex: ["The question is whether the defendant took the money; the prosecutor spends the hour arguing that theft is a terrible crime.", "Asked whether the new policy will reduce emissions, the minister explains how hard her team worked on it."],
    d: "An argument that supports some conclusion other than the one at issue, while seeming to settle it: answering “should this law pass?” with proof that the problem it addresses is serious.",
    x: "Aristotle's name for it, ignoratio elenchi, means not knowing what a refutation requires: the arguer proves something, but not the contradiction of the opponent's claim. A red herring is the deliberate version, a distraction that draws a discussion away from the point, and the straw man is a common species, refuting a position nobody holds.",
    s: "Aristotle, Sophistical Refutations 5–6",
    p: ["aristotle"], r: ["straw-man"], br: ["logic"], k: ["irrelevant conclusion", "ignoratio elenchi", "red herring"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies”" }] },
  // Ethics & politics
  { id: "normative", t: "Normative ethics and metaethics", a: "ethics",
    d: "Normative ethics asks what we ought to do and what makes actions right or wrong. Metaethics asks what moral claims mean and whether they can be true. Applied ethics takes up particular questions, such as war, punishment or the treatment of animals.",
    x: "The three major approaches in normative ethics are virtue ethics, deontology and consequentialism. Metaethics studies the presuppositions of moral thought and talk: what moral words mean, whether moral facts exist, how we could know them, and how moral judgment bears on motivation. Hume's point that an “ought” cannot be derived from an “is”, and Moore's open-question argument, set much of its modern agenda.\n\nNon-cognitivists deny that moral judgments state facts. Emotivists such as A. J. Ayer take them to express emotion; R. M. Hare's prescriptivism takes them to express prescriptions of a universal sort; Allan Gibbard took them to express the acceptance of norms. Simon Blackburn's quasi-realism tries to explain why we are entitled to treat moral judgments as if they were true or false. J. L. Mackie, who coined the term “error theory” in 1977, held that moral judgments aim at objective facts but are never true, since properties that made such demands would be “queer”.",
    s: "Hume, Treatise of Human Nature (1740), III.i.1; Moore, Principia Ethica (1903)",
    p: ["hume", "moore"], r: ["deontology", "consequentialism", "virtue-ethics"], br: ["ethics"], k: ["normative", "metaethics", "is-ought", "is–ought"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaethics” (Geoff Sayre-McCord)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Virtue Ethics” (Rosalind Hursthouse and Glen Pettigrove)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-cognitivism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism” (Mark van Roojen)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Moral Anti-Realism” (Richard Joyce)" }] },
  { id: "deontology", t: "Deontology", a: "ethics",
    d: "Ethics of duty: some acts are required or forbidden whatever their consequences. Rights, duties and constraints limit what may be done even for good ends.",
    x: "Kant grounds duty in the categorical imperative and holds that a good will acts from duty. Deontologists distinguish doing from allowing and intending from foreseeing, as in the doctrine of double effect. W. D. Ross defended plural prima facie duties, such as fidelity, gratitude and non-maleficence, which must be weighed in particular cases. Critics ask why one should keep a rule when breaking it would prevent a greater harm.",
    s: "Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785); Ross, The Right and the Good (1930)",
    p: ["kant"], r: ["categorical-imperative", "autonomy", "virtue-ethics", "natural-law"], vs: ["consequentialism"], br: ["ethics"], k: ["deontology", "deontological"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Immanuel Kant”" },
  { id: "consequentialism", t: "Consequentialism", a: "ethics",
    d: "The view that the rightness of an act depends only on its consequences. Utilitarianism counts consequences in terms of happiness or welfare.",
    x: "Bentham's utilitarianism measured pleasures and pains; Mill distinguished higher and lower pleasures. Elizabeth Anscombe coined the word “consequentialism” in “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958). Act consequentialists judge each act; rule consequentialists judge rules by the results of their general acceptance. Critics object that it can permit grave injustice to individuals whenever that maximizes the total good, and that it demands too much.",
    s: "Bentham, An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789); Mill, Utilitarianism (1861); Anscombe, “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958)",
    p: ["bentham", "mill", "anscombe"], r: ["normative", "virtue-ethics", "veil-of-ignorance"], vs: ["deontology"], br: ["ethics"], k: ["consequentialism", "utilitarianism", "utilitarian"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/bentham/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jeremy Bentham”" },
  { id: "virtue-ethics", t: "Virtue ethics", a: "ethics",
    d: "Ethics centered on character, and on the virtues a person needs in order to live well. It asks what kind of person to be, and treats right action as what a virtuous person would characteristically do.",
    x: "Aristotle defines virtue as a settled disposition to choose the mean between excess and deficiency, as courage lies between cowardice and rashness, guided by practical wisdom (phronēsis). Confucian ethics also centers on the cultivation of character. Anscombe's 1958 essay and Alasdair MacIntyre's After Virtue (1981) revived virtue ethics in opposition to modern rule-based theories.",
    s: "Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics II.6; MacIntyre, After Virtue (1981)",
    p: ["aristotle", "confucius", "anscombe", "macintyre"], r: ["eudaimonia", "ren", "deontology", "consequentialism"], br: ["ethics", "ancient"], k: ["virtue ethics", "virtue", "phronesis", "phronēsis"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Aristotle”" },
  { id: "eudaimonia", t: "Eudaimonia", a: "ethics",
    d: "Aristotle's term for the highest human good, usually translated “happiness” or “flourishing”: activity of the soul in accordance with virtue.",
    x: "For Aristotle eudaimonia belongs to a complete life of activity, and it needs some external goods such as friends and resources; its highest form is contemplation. The Stoics held that virtue alone suffices for it, and the Epicureans identified it with tranquility and freedom from pain. Modern research on well-being uses the term for flourishing, which includes meaning and growth along with pleasure.",
    s: "Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics I.7, X.7–8",
    p: ["aristotle", "epicurus", "epictetus"], r: ["virtue-ethics", "teleology"], br: ["ethics", "ancient"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Aristotle”" },
  { id: "categorical-imperative", t: "Categorical imperative", a: "ethics",
    d: "Kant's supreme principle of morality, which binds unconditionally. One formulation: act only on a maxim that you can at the same time will to be a universal law.",
    x: "Kant (1724–1804) held that the supreme principle of morality is a principle of reason binding on every rational agent, whatever that agent happens to want. He called it the categorical imperative. Its most influential statements are in the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), and he developed and sometimes revised them in the Critique of Practical Reason (1788) and The Metaphysics of Morals (1797). Because the principle has to hold for all rational beings, Kant insisted that it be sought and established a priori, independently of facts about human psychology.\n\nThe Groundwork opens with the claim that only a good will is good without qualification. A good will, a firm commitment to do what morality requires, would “shine like a jewel” even if it achieved nothing, and an action has moral worth only when it expresses such a will. Acting from self-interest or sympathy may be praiseworthy, but for Kant it lacks moral worth, a claim that remains widely disputed. Duty appears to us as constraint because our desires can pull against morality; a holy will, with no such pull, would need no imperatives at all.\n\nImperatives are commands addressed to agents who could obey them and might not. Hypothetical imperatives say what to do if you want some end: problematic ones concern ends you might or might not adopt, and assertoric ones concern happiness, which Kant thought every human being necessarily pursues, though advice about it is only a counsel. The categorical imperative commands whatever your ends. Kant describes agents as acting on maxims, subjective principles which commentators set out in the form “I will A in C in order to realize or produce E”, and the categorical imperative is a test for maxims.\n\nThe first formulation tells us to act only in accordance with that maxim through which we can at the same time will that it become a universal law. Following Onora O'Neill and John Rawls, Robert Johnson and Adam Cureton's Stanford Encyclopedia article sets out the test in four steps: state the maxim, imagine it as a universal law of nature, ask whether such a world is even conceivable, and ask whether one could rationally will it. A maxim that cannot be conceived as universal law violates a perfect duty, which admits no exceptions; one that can be conceived but not willed violates an imperfect duty, which requires acting a certain way sometimes and to some extent.\n\nKant illustrates the test with four examples. Ending one's life to escape suffering violates a perfect duty to oneself, and making a lying promise to get money violates a perfect duty to others, since a practice of promising could not survive universal deception. Letting one's talents rust violates an imperfect duty to oneself, and refusing ever to help others in need violates an imperfect duty to others: both can be conceived as universal, but a rational agent cannot will them. Johnson and Cureton find the argument about talents unconvincing, since full rationality seems to require developing only some of them.\n\nThe Formula of Humanity reads: “So act that you use humanity, whether in your own person or in the person of any other, always at the same time as an end, never merely as a means”. Ordinary life depends on others' work, and the formula permits that; what it forbids is using people's rational capacities in ways they could not consent to, as deception and coercion do. Humanity limits what we may pursue, which grounds perfect duties, and is also an end to promote, which grounds imperfect duties such as developing one's talents and furthering others' chosen ends. The respect it requires is owed to every person and cannot be lost through misdeeds.\n\nA further formula presents the will of every rational being as a will that gives universal law, so that the moral law is the law of one's own reason; this autonomy is why its authority binds us. The Formula of the Kingdom of Ends asks us to act as members legislating for a possible community of rational beings. Kant called the formulas equivalent without explaining in what sense, and interpreters still debate it. Critics have asked whether the framework leaves out people with severe cognitive disabilities, and Hegel objected that the universalizability test is an empty formalism that yields no determinate duties.",
    s: "Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), 4:421, 4:429 (Gregor trans.); Hegel, Philosophy of Right (1820), §135",
    p: ["kant", "hegel"], r: ["deontology", "autonomy"], br: ["ethics", "german-idealism"], k: ["categorical imperative", "kingdom of ends", "universal law"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Kant's Moral Philosophy”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Immanuel Kant”" }] },
  { id: "autonomy", t: "Autonomy", a: "ethics",
    d: "Self-legislation. In Kant, the will giving the moral law to itself; more broadly, self-government.",
    x: "Kant sets autonomy against heteronomy, in which the will is determined by something outside reason, such as desire or authority. In political thought autonomy means self-government, and in medical ethics a patient's right to decide about their own treatment. Feminist philosophers have developed relational accounts of autonomy, which stress that capacities for self-direction are formed in social relationships.",
    s: "Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), 4:440–41",
    p: ["rousseau", "kant"], r: ["categorical-imperative", "deontology", "general-will", "compatibilism", "personhood"], br: ["ethics", "political", "german-idealism"], k: ["autonomy", "heteronomy"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Immanuel Kant”" },
  { id: "social-contract", t: "Social contract", a: "ethics",
    d: "The view that political authority is legitimate because it rests on an agreement among the people subject to it.",
    x: "Hobbes imagines a state of nature so insecure that rational people would hand their right to all things to an absolute sovereign. Locke's contract protects natural rights to life, liberty and property, and permits resistance when government violates them. Rousseau seeks a form of association in which each, uniting with all, still obeys only himself, through the general will. Rawls revived the tradition with the original position, and Carole Pateman and Charles Mills argued that the classic contract rested on a sexual contract and a racial contract that excluded women and non-whites.",
    s: "Hobbes, Leviathan (1651); Locke, Second Treatise of Government (1689); Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762); Pateman, The Sexual Contract (1988); Mills, The Racial Contract (1997)",
    p: ["hobbes", "locke", "rousseau", "rawls", "pateman", "mills"], r: ["state-of-nature", "general-will", "veil-of-ignorance", "racial-contract", "sovereignty"], br: ["political"], k: ["social contract", "contractarian", "contractualism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hobbes's Moral and Political Philosophy”" },
  { id: "state-of-nature", t: "State of nature", a: "ethics",
    d: "The condition of human beings without political authority, used as a thought experiment to show why government is needed and what it may legitimately do.",
    x: "Hobbes describes it as a war of every man against every man, in which life is “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short”. Locke's state of nature is governed by natural law and is insecure mainly because everyone judges in their own case. Rousseau argued that his predecessors projected social vices back onto natural man, and traced inequality to property and society. Critics have noted that European writers often cast Indigenous peoples as living examples of the state of nature.",
    s: "Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), ch. 13; Locke, Second Treatise of Government (1689), ch. 2; Rousseau, Discourse on the Origin of Inequality (1755)",
    p: ["hobbes", "locke", "rousseau"], r: ["social-contract", "sovereignty", "general-will", "natural-law"], br: ["political"], k: ["state of nature"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/locke-political/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Locke's Political Philosophy”" },
  { id: "general-will", img: { file: "Social_contract_rousseau_page.jpg", alt: "Title page of Rousseau's Du contrat social", cap: "Title page of the first edition of Du contrat social (1762). Public domain, via" }, t: "General will", a: "ethics",
    d: "Rousseau: the will of the political body as a whole, aimed at the common good. It differs from the “will of all”, the sum of private interests.",
    x: "In the social contract each person places themselves under the supreme direction of the general will, and so in obeying the law obeys only themselves. Rousseau's remark that whoever refuses to obey the general will shall be “forced to be free” has drawn charges of totalitarianism, while defenders read it as protection against private domination. Kant's idea of autonomy and Hegel's rational state both draw on the concept.",
    s: "Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762), I.6–7, II.3",
    p: ["rousseau", "kant", "hegel"], r: ["social-contract", "autonomy", "sovereignty", "state-of-nature"], br: ["political"], k: ["general will", "volonté générale"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rousseau/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jean-Jacques Rousseau”" },
  { id: "veil-of-ignorance", t: "Original position and veil of ignorance", a: "ethics",
    d: "Rawls's device for choosing principles of justice: imagine choosing them without knowing your place in society, your talents, your conception of the good or even your generation.",
    x: "Rawls argued that people in the original position would choose two principles: equal basic liberties for all, and the difference principle, which allows social and economic inequalities only if they benefit the least advantaged and attach to positions open to all under fair equality of opportunity. Nozick replied that justice concerns how holdings came about, and Susan Moller Okin and Charles Mills asked what the idealized device leaves out about gender and race.",
    s: "Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), §§3–4, 24; Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974)",
    p: ["rawls", "nozick", "mills"], r: ["social-contract", "racial-contract", "consequentialism"], br: ["political", "ethics"], k: ["veil of ignorance", "original position"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rawls/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “John Rawls”" },
  { id: "sovereignty", img: { file: "Leviathan_frontispiece_cropped_British_Museum.jpg", alt: "Frontispiece of Leviathan: a crowned giant whose body is made of many small figures rises over a town", cap: "Frontispiece of Hobbes's Leviathan (1651), the sovereign made of the bodies of his subjects. Public domain, via" }, t: "Sovereignty", a: "ethics",
    d: "Supreme authority within a political order: the power that makes law and answers to no higher human authority within its territory.",
    x: "Bodin defined sovereignty as absolute and perpetual power, and Hobbes made the sovereign the artificial person who represents the commonwealth. Schmitt's Political Theology opens: “Sovereign is he who decides on the exception.” Foucault argued that modern power works largely below and around sovereignty, through discipline and biopower, and Agamben and Mbembe returned to sovereignty as power over life and death. Bataille used the word in a sense of his own, for moments of life freed from usefulness.",
    s: "Bodin, Six Books of the Commonwealth (1576); Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), chs. 17–18; Schmitt, Political Theology (1922), ch. 1; Bataille, The Accursed Share, vol. 3 (published 1976)",
    p: ["bodin", "hobbes", "schmitt", "foucault", "agamben", "mbembe", "bataille"], r: ["state-of-exception", "social-contract", "biopower", "necropolitics", "general-will"], br: ["political"], k: ["sovereignty", "sovereign"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/bodin/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jean Bodin”" },
  { id: "state-of-exception", t: "State of exception", a: "ethics",
    d: "The suspension of the normal legal order in an emergency, by an authority that the law empowers or that claims to stand above it.",
    x: "Schmitt argued that the exception reveals who is sovereign, since no norm can decide when it applies. Benjamin's eighth thesis on history answers that for the oppressed the “state of emergency” is the rule. Agamben's Homo Sacer and State of Exception argue that modern politics increasingly governs through the exception, reducing people to “bare life” exposed to violence without legal protection.",
    s: "Schmitt, Political Theology (1922); Benjamin, “On the Concept of History” (1940), Thesis VIII; Agamben, Homo Sacer (1995); State of Exception (2003)",
    p: ["schmitt", "benjamin", "agamben"], r: ["sovereignty", "necropolitics", "biopower"], br: ["political", "law"], k: ["state of exception", "state of emergency"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schmitt/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Carl Schmitt”" },
  { id: "recognition", t: "Recognition", a: "ethics",
    d: "Hegel: self-consciousness depends on being acknowledged by another self-consciousness. The section on lordship and bondage stages the struggle for it.",
    x: "In the Phenomenology, two self-consciousnesses each seek recognition and enter a life-and-death struggle; the one who prefers life to freedom becomes the bondsman, and through labor the bondsman shapes the world and gains a self-consciousness the lord lacks. Kojève's Paris lectures (1933–39) made this section central to French thought. Honneth and Charles Taylor built theories of recognition for love, rights, esteem and identity politics. Fanon objected that under colonial racism the master wants work from the slave and has no interest in recognition.",
    s: "Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit (1807), ¶¶178–96; Kojève, Introduction to the Reading of Hegel (1947); Fanon, Black Skin, White Masks (1952), ch. 7",
    p: ["hegel", "kojeve", "fanon", "honneth", "taylor"], r: ["dialectic", "alienation", "double-consciousness", "end-of-history", "personhood"], br: ["german-idealism", "political"], k: ["recognition", "lordship and bondage", "master and slave", "master-slave"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/recognition/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Recognition”" },
  { id: "personhood", t: "Personhood and community", a: "ethics",
    d: "A central debate in African philosophy about whether a person is constituted by their community. The Nguni saying umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu, “a person is a person through other persons”, expresses the ethic of ubuntu.",
    x: "Ifeanyi Menkiti argued that in African thought personhood “is the sort of thing which has to be attained”: it is earned through participation in communal life and the discharge of one's obligations. Kwame Gyekye counted individual liberty among the basic goods of a society, while holding that it should not conflict with the common good and that duties take precedence over rights. The debate bears on rights, duties and the relation between individual and society in general.",
    s: "Menkiti, “Person and Community in African Traditional Thought” (1984); Gyekye, An Essay on African Philosophical Thought (1987)",
    p: ["menkiti", "gyekye", "wiredu"], r: ["recognition", "autonomy", "ethnophilosophy", "ren"], br: ["african", "ethics"], k: ["ubuntu", "personhood", "communitarian", "communitarianism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-ethics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Ethics” (Kwame Gyekye)" },
  { id: "ren", t: "Ren and li", a: "ethics",
    d: "Two central Confucian virtues. Ren is humaneness or benevolence, the excellence of a person in relation to others; li is ritual propriety, the forms of conduct through which respect is expressed and character is shaped.",
    x: "In the Analects, Confucius says that ren is to love others, and that to restrain oneself and return to li is ren. Mencius held that human nature is good and contains sprouts of humaneness, righteousness, propriety and wisdom, which cultivation must nourish; Xunzi argued that human nature is bad and that ritual and teachers reshape it. Confucian role ethics is often compared with Aristotle's virtue ethics.",
    s: "Confucius, Analects 12.1, 12.22; Mencius 2A6; Xunzi, ch. 23",
    p: ["confucius", "mencius", "xunzi"], r: ["virtue-ethics", "dao", "personhood"], br: ["chinese", "ethics"], k: ["humaneness", "ritual propriety", "confucian", "confucianism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mencius/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Mencius”" },
  { id: "will-to-power", t: "Will to power", a: "ethics",
    d: "Nietzsche's name for the basic drive of living things to expand, overcome resistance and discharge their strength. Self-preservation, he says, is only one of its indirect results.",
    x: "Zarathustra says that wherever he found the living he found will to power, and that life is what must always overcome itself. Nietzsche uses the idea to interpret morality, knowledge and philosophy as expressions of drives, which grounds his genealogical method. The Will to Power (1901), compiled after his death by his sister and Heinrich Köselitz from unpublished notes, presented as a system what he never finished, and Heidegger read will to power as the culmination of Western metaphysics.",
    s: "Nietzsche, Thus Spoke Zarathustra II, “On Self-Overcoming” (1883); Beyond Good and Evil (1886), §§13, 36",
    p: ["nietzsche", "heidegger"], r: ["conatus", "ressentiment", "genealogy", "eternal-recurrence"], br: ["ethics", "metaphysics"], k: ["will to power"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nietzsche/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Friedrich Nietzsche”" },
  { id: "eternal-recurrence", t: "Eternal recurrence", a: "ethics",
    d: "Nietzsche's thought that one's life, and everything in it, will recur endlessly in exactly the same way. He offers it as a test: could you affirm your life if you had to live it again innumerable times?",
    x: "The Gay Science §341 introduces it through a demon who whispers it to you in your loneliest loneliness. In Zarathustra it is the “most abysmal thought”, which the hero must learn to will. Scholars debate whether Nietzsche held it as a cosmological claim or as an ethical thought experiment; most of his published uses are of the second kind. It belongs with amor fati, the love of one's fate.",
    s: "Nietzsche, The Gay Science (1882), §§276, 341; Thus Spoke Zarathustra III (1884)",
    p: ["nietzsche"], r: ["will-to-power", "ressentiment"], br: ["ethics", "metaphysics"], k: ["eternal recurrence", "eternal return", "amor fati"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nietzsche/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Friedrich Nietzsche”" },
  { id: "ressentiment", t: "Ressentiment", a: "ethics",
    d: "Nietzsche: the reactive hostility of the weak toward the strong which, when it becomes creative, produces values that condemn strength as evil and call weakness good.",
    x: "In the First Essay of the Genealogy, the “slave revolt in morality” begins when ressentiment itself becomes creative: those denied the reaction of deeds take an imaginary revenge by calling the powerful “evil” and themselves “good”. Nietzsche sets this reactive valuation against the noble mode, which affirms itself first and calls the low “bad” only as an afterthought. Max Scheler wrote a study of ressentiment in 1912, and the concept remains in use in political analysis of grievance.",
    s: "Nietzsche, On the Genealogy of Morality (1887), First Essay, §§7–11",
    p: ["nietzsche"], r: ["will-to-power", "genealogy", "eternal-recurrence"], br: ["ethics"], k: ["ressentiment", "slave morality", "slave revolt"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nietzsche/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Friedrich Nietzsche”" },
  { id: "natural-law", t: "Natural law", a: "ethics",
    d: "The theory that there are moral standards knowable by reason and grounded in human nature, which human law must respect. On the strong version, an unjust law lacks the full authority of law.",
    x: "The Stoics and Cicero spoke of a law of nature common to all rational beings. Aquinas defined law as an ordinance of reason for the common good, and held that the first precept of natural law is that good is to be done and evil avoided. Early modern theories of natural rights (Grotius, Locke) grew from this tradition. In the twentieth century Lon Fuller and John Finnis defended natural law against legal positivism.",
    s: "Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I–II, qq. 90–94; Cicero, On the Republic III",
    p: ["aquinas", "locke"], r: ["social-contract", "state-of-nature", "deontology"], vs: ["legal-positivism"], br: ["law", "ethics"], k: ["natural law", "natural rights"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Thomas Aquinas”" },
  { id: "legal-positivism", t: "Legal positivism", a: "ethics",
    d: "The view that what the law is depends on social facts (what has been enacted, recognized or practiced), and that a law's existence is a separate question from its moral merit.",
    x: "Bentham and John Austin defined law as the command of a sovereign backed by sanctions. Hart replaced this with a system of primary rules that govern conduct and secondary rules, including a “rule of recognition” by which officials identify valid law. Kelsen grounded a legal order in a presupposed basic norm. Dworkin objected that judges rely on moral principles that are themselves part of the law.",
    s: "Austin, The Province of Jurisprudence Determined (1832); Kelsen, Pure Theory of Law (1934); Hart, The Concept of Law (1961)",
    p: ["bentham", "johnaustin", "kelsen", "hart", "dworkin"], r: ["sovereignty", "state-of-exception"], vs: ["natural-law"], br: ["law"], k: ["legal positivism", "rule of recognition"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lawphil-nature/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “The Nature of Law”" },
  // Aesthetics & religion
  { id: "aura", t: "Aura", a: "aes",
    d: "Benjamin: the unique presence of a work of art in time and space, its here and now, which technical reproduction withers.",
    x: "Benjamin defines aura, by analogy with natural objects, as “the unique apparition of a distance, however near it may be”. Photography and film detach the work from tradition and ritual and bring it closer to the masses, which he saw as both a loss and a political opening: the function of art shifts from ritual toward politics. Adorno, in a 1936 letter to Benjamin, doubted that mass reproduction was as emancipatory as Benjamin hoped.",
    s: "Benjamin, “The Work of Art in the Age of Its Technological Reproducibility” (1935–36)",
    p: ["benjamin", "adorno"], r: ["culture-industry", "commodity-fetishism", "sublime"], br: ["aesthetics", "critical-theory"], k: ["aura", "mechanical reproduction", "technological reproducibility"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/benjamin/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Walter Benjamin”" },
  { id: "sublime", t: "The sublime", a: "aes",
    d: "The experience of something overwhelmingly vast or powerful (a storm, the night sky, a mountain range) that exceeds our capacity to take it in, mixing pain with a kind of elevation.",
    x: "Longinus wrote on sublimity in style. Burke separated the sublime, rooted in terror and astonishment, from the beautiful, rooted in love and pleasure. Kant distinguished the mathematical sublime (magnitude) from the dynamical sublime (power), and argued that the feeling discloses our rational vocation, which nature's might cannot touch. Lyotard made the sublime central to his account of the avant-garde and the postmodern.",
    s: "Burke, A Philosophical Enquiry into the Origin of Our Ideas of the Sublime and Beautiful (1757); Kant, Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), §§23–29; Lyotard, “The Sublime and the Avant-Garde” (1984)",
    img: { file: "Caspar_David_Friedrich_-_Wanderer_above_the_Sea_of_Fog.jpeg", alt: "Painting of a man seen from behind, standing on a rocky peak above a sea of fog", cap: "Caspar David Friedrich, Wanderer above the Sea of Fog, about 1818 (Hamburger Kunsthalle). Public domain, via" },
    p: ["burke", "kant", "lyotard"], r: ["aura", "transcendence", "transgression"], br: ["aesthetics"], k: ["sublime", "sublimity"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/burke/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Edmund Burke”" },
  { id: "ontological-argument", t: "Ontological argument", a: "aes",
    d: "An argument for God's existence from the concept of God alone. Anselm: God is that than which nothing greater can be conceived; a being that exists in reality is greater than one that exists only in the mind; so God exists in reality.",
    x: "The monk Gaunilo replied that the same reasoning would prove the existence of a perfect island. Descartes gave a version from the idea of a supremely perfect being, and Leibniz added that God's possibility must first be shown. Kant objected that existence is no real predicate: it adds nothing to the concept of a thing. Plantinga's modal version revived the debate in the twentieth century.",
    s: "Anselm, Proslogion, chs. 2–3; Descartes, Meditations V (1641); Kant, Critique of Pure Reason, A592–602/B620–30",
    p: ["anselm", "descartes", "leibniz", "kant"], r: ["essence-existence", "problem-of-evil", "a-priori"], br: ["religion"], k: ["ontological argument"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anselm/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Anselm of Canterbury”" },
  { id: "problem-of-evil", img: { file: "1755_Lisbon_earthquake.jpg", alt: "Engraving of Lisbon in ruins and flames, with ships wrecked in the harbour", cap: "Copper engraving of the Lisbon earthquake of 1755, the disaster behind Voltaire's Poem on the Lisbon Disaster and Candide. Public domain, via" }, t: "Problem of evil", a: "aes",
    d: "The question how an all-powerful, all-knowing and wholly good God could permit evil and suffering. The logical version claims these attributes are inconsistent with evil; the evidential version claims evil makes God's existence improbable.",
    x: "Hume's Philo repeats “Epicurus's old questions”: is God willing to prevent evil, but unable, or able, but unwilling? Theodicies answer that evil serves greater goods: free will (Augustine), the making of souls (Irenaeus, revived by John Hick) or the best possible world as a whole (Leibniz). Voltaire's Candide mocked Leibnizian optimism after the Lisbon earthquake of 1755.\n\nJ. L. Mackie's “Evil and Omnipotence” (1955) pressed the logical version. Alvin Plantinga's free will defense (1974) replied that a defense need only show that God and evil are consistent: it is possible that every free creature God could create would do some wrong, and many philosophers judge that the logical version fails. William Rowe's evidential argument (1979) points to intense suffering that seems pointless, such as a fawn dying slowly after a forest fire. Skeptical theists answer that our failure to see a reason for some evil is weak evidence that there is none.",
    s: "Hume, Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779), Part X; Leibniz, Theodicy (1710)",
    p: ["augustine", "leibniz", "hume"], r: ["ontological-argument", "determinism", "compatibilism"], br: ["religion"], k: ["problem of evil", "theodicy"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-religion/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Philosophy of Religion”" },
  { id: "transgression", t: "Transgression", a: "aes",
    d: "Bataille: the crossing of a taboo that suspends it while leaving it in force. Taboo and transgression belong together; festival, eroticism and sacrifice transgress the prohibitions that organize the world of work.",
    x: "For Bataille the sacred depends on the violation of a prohibition whose general observance makes us human, and sacrifice reaffirms the law it breaks: the law is the other side of transgression, which therefore depends on the limit it crosses. Foucault wrote “A Preface to Transgression” (1963) in homage to Bataille, who had died the year before.",
    s: "Bataille, Erotism: Death and Sensuality (1957); Foucault, “A Preface to Transgression” (1963)",
    p: ["bataille", "foucault"], r: ["expenditure", "general-economy", "sovereignty", "sublime"], br: ["religion"], k: ["transgression", "taboo"], src: "https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/opth-2024-0016/html", srcName: "“The Remedy for a World Without Transcendence”: Georges Bataille on Sacrifice and the Theology of Transgression, Open Theology 10 (2024) (Luka Trebežnik)" },
  // Phenomenology & hermeneutics
  { id: "phenomenology", t: "Phenomenology", a: "phen",
    d: "The study of the structures of experience as lived from the first-person point of view. Husserl's call was to go “back to the things themselves”: describe what appears, as it appears, before theorizing about it.",
    x: "Husserl developed phenomenology as a rigorous science of consciousness, using the epoché and the analysis of intentionality. Heidegger turned it toward the question of being and the structures of everyday existence; Merleau-Ponty toward the lived body and perception; Sartre and Beauvoir toward freedom and situation. Fanon's account of racialized embodiment and recent critical phenomenology study how social structures shape experience itself.",
    s: "Husserl, Logical Investigations (1900–01); Ideas I (1913); Merleau-Ponty, Phenomenology of Perception (1945)",
    p: ["husserl", "heidegger", "merleauponty", "sartre", "beauvoir", "fanon"], r: ["intentionality", "epoche", "lifeworld", "being-in-the-world", "existentialism"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["phenomenology", "phenomenological"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/phenomenology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Phenomenology”" },
  { id: "intentionality", t: "Intentionality", a: "phen",
    d: "The directedness of consciousness toward an object: every perceiving is a perceiving of something, every judging a judging of something.",
    x: "Intentionality is the power of minds and mental states to be about, to represent or to stand for things, properties and states of affairs: a belief is about the weather, a fear is of the dog, a hope is that the train will come. Words, pictures and other symbols have it too when people use them to convey what they think. The term comes from the Latin tendere, to aim or stretch, through the medieval intentio, and it differs from intention in the everyday sense and from intensionality, a logical feature of sentences.\n\nFranz Brentano revived the scholastic term in Psychology from an Empirical Standpoint (1874), where he wrote that every mental phenomenon is characterized by the “intentional inexistence” of an object. Pierre Jacob's Stanford Encyclopedia article separates three theses in Brentano: mental states are directed toward something other than themselves; the objects they are directed at have a special, intentional kind of existence; and intentionality is the mark of the mental, so that all mental states, and only they, have it. That third thesis shaped much of twentieth-century philosophy of mind.\n\nBrentano's student Edmund Husserl made intentionality the central theme of phenomenology. He analyzed the act of consciousness (noesis) and its intended content (noema), and used the epoché, the bracketing of questions about whether objects really exist, to study directedness as such. Sartre took the idea further and made it the basis of his claim that consciousness has no contents of its own: it is nothing but its directedness toward the world.\n\nThought can be directed at what does not exist, such as the fountain of youth or a golden mountain, and this raises a puzzle. Alexius Meinong held that such objects have properties without existing. Bertrand Russell rejected a world populated with round squares, and his theory of descriptions in “On Denoting” (1905) let sentences such as “Pegasus does not exist” be true without being about anything. Terence Parsons (1980) and Edward Zalta (1988) later developed more careful theories of nonexistent objects.\n\nSentences that report mental states behave oddly in logic. A woman can believe that Hesperus is shining without believing that Phosphorus is shining, though they are the same planet, and one can seek a unicorn though none exists. Roderick Chisholm (1957) proposed such failures of substitution and existential inference as a test for intentional language. Jacob argues that the test fits imperfectly: statements about necessity, laws or causation are intensional in this sense without describing anything mental.\n\nW. V. Quine (1960) agreed that intentional idioms cannot be reduced to physical ones and posed a dilemma: either accept them as indispensable and give up physicalism, or keep physicalism and deny that they are meaningful. Others tried to show how intentionality arises in nature: Fred Dretske built content from natural information, Ruth Millikan from biological functions, and Jerry Fodor posited a language of thought. Searle, Fodor and John Haugeland distinguish the original intentionality of minds from the derived intentionality of words and artifacts, a distinction Daniel Dennett rejects; his intentional stance treats talk of beliefs and desires as a predictive strategy. Following Anscombe's shopping-list example (1957), Searle (1983) distinguished beliefs, which aim to fit the world, from desires and intentions, which aim to make the world fit them.\n\nWhether every mental state is intentional remains open. Pains, itches and moods seem to have a felt character without obviously being about anything. Ned Block (1995) distinguished access consciousness from phenomenal consciousness. Intentionalists such as Dretske and Michael Tye (both 1995) argue that what experiences are like consists in their representational content, and John McDowell (1994) holds that perceptual experience already has conceptual content. In analytic philosophy of mind, the deeper question is how physical states can be about anything at all.",
    s: "Brentano, Psychology from an Empirical Standpoint (1874); Husserl, Ideas I (1913)",
    p: ["brentano", "husserl", "sartre"], r: ["phenomenology", "epoche", "transcendence"], br: ["phenomenology", "mind"], k: ["intentionality", "intentional object", "noema"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intentionality/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Intentionality”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/brentano/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Franz Brentano”" }] },
  { id: "epoche", t: "Epoché", a: "phen",
    d: "Husserl's bracketing: suspending the natural attitude's belief in the existence of the world, so that experience can be described just as it is given.",
    x: "The epoché leaves the world as it was and puts the belief in its existence out of play, so that attention can turn to how things appear. Husserl pairs it with the phenomenological and transcendental reductions, which lead back to the consciousness for which the world has meaning. The Greek word comes from the Pyrrhonian skeptics, who used it for suspension of judgment. Merleau-Ponty remarked that the most important lesson of the reduction is that a complete reduction is impossible.",
    s: "Husserl, Ideas I (1913), §§31–32; Merleau-Ponty, Phenomenology of Perception (1945), Preface",
    p: ["sextus", "husserl", "merleauponty"], r: ["phenomenology", "skepticism", "intentionality", "transcendental"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["epoché", "epoche", "bracketing", "phenomenological reduction"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/husserl/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Edmund Husserl”" },
  { id: "dasein", t: "Dasein", a: "phen",
    d: "Heidegger's term for the kind of being that humans are: the entity for which its own being is an issue.",
    x: "The German word means existence, literally “being-there”. Heidegger uses it to avoid the inherited language of subject, consciousness and soul. Dasein always already has some understanding of being, exists as being-in-the-world, is thrown into a situation it did not choose, and can own its existence authentically by facing its being-toward-death.\n\nBeing and Time analyzes Dasein's structures as existentials: being-in-the-world, being-with others, thrownness, understanding, discourse and falling into the anonymous “they.” Their unity is care, which turns out to be grounded in temporality. Heidegger insisted that the analysis concerns the being of the questioner who asks about being, and that it serves the larger question of the meaning of being, which the published book never reached.",
    s: "Heidegger, Being and Time (1927), §§4, 9",
    p: ["heidegger"], r: ["being-in-the-world", "phenomenology", "existentialism", "ontology"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["dasein", "being-toward-death", "thrownness"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heidegger/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Martin Heidegger”" },
  { id: "being-in-the-world", t: "Being-in-the-world", a: "phen",
    d: "Heidegger's term for Dasein's basic state: a unified whole in which self and world are given together. We are first of all engaged with things in practical concern, using them as equipment.",
    x: "A hammer in use is ready-to-hand and withdraws from attention; it becomes present-at-hand, an object with properties, when it breaks or when we stop to theorize about it. Heidegger uses this to criticize the Cartesian picture of a subject that must prove the existence of an external world. Merleau-Ponty developed the idea through the body's practical grasp of its surroundings, and Hubert Dreyfus drew on it in his critique of artificial intelligence.",
    s: "Heidegger, Being and Time (1927), §§12–18",
    p: ["heidegger", "merleauponty"], r: ["dasein", "lifeworld", "intentionality"], vs: ["dualism"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["being-in-the-world", "ready-to-hand", "present-at-hand"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heidegger/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Martin Heidegger”" },
  { id: "lifeworld", t: "Lifeworld", a: "phen",
    d: "Husserl's Lebenswelt: the everyday world of lived experience, taken for granted as the background of all our activities, including science.",
    x: "In The Crisis of European Sciences, Husserl argued that modern science, mathematizing nature since Galileo, forgot its own roots in the lifeworld. Alfred Schutz brought the concept into sociology. Habermas sets the lifeworld, reproduced through communication, against systems such as markets and bureaucracies, and warns of the colonization of the lifeworld by their imperatives.\n\nHusserl's charge was that Galileo dressed the lifeworld in a “garb of ideas”, mathematical idealizations that later scientists took for reality itself. The lifeworld is pre-given: it is always already there as the horizon of experience, and science is one of the activities that take place within it. Schutz analyzed its structures of typification and relevance, work completed by Thomas Luckmann in The Structures of the Life-World (1973), and Peter Berger and Luckmann's The Social Construction of Reality (1966) built on it. Merleau-Ponty's phenomenology of perception also starts from the lived world prior to scientific description.",
    s: "Husserl, The Crisis of European Sciences and Transcendental Phenomenology (1936); Habermas, The Theory of Communicative Action, vol. 2 (1981)",
    p: ["husserl", "schutz", "habermas"], r: ["phenomenology", "communicative-action", "being-in-the-world", "social-construction"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["lifeworld", "lebenswelt"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/husserl/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Edmund Husserl”" },
  { id: "hermeneutics", t: "Hermeneutics", a: "phen",
    d: "The theory and practice of interpretation. It began with the interpretation of scripture and law and grew into a general theory of understanding.",
    x: "Hermeneutics is the study of interpretation. Outside philosophy it serves as an auxiliary discipline in theology, especially biblical studies, in law, in medicine and in parts of the social sciences and humanities. Within philosophy it names both a field and the historical movement that developed it, and it treats interpretation itself as its subject: its nature, its scope, the validity of its results and its place in human life. Theodore George's Stanford Encyclopedia article describes understanding as the success of interpretation, a success judged by standards of its own.\n\nA recurring theme is the hermeneutic circle. In its older form, traced back to antiquity and taken up by Schleiermacher and Dilthey, it is the relation between a text as a whole and its parts: the parts can be understood only in light of the whole, and the whole only through the parts. Hermeneutics accepts this circularity and opposes the attempt to ground knowledge on a single secure foundation from which everything else is derived.\n\nFriedrich Schleiermacher, writing in the early nineteenth century and close to German romanticism, made hermeneutics a general art of understanding any discourse, written or spoken, sacred or secular. He distinguished a grammatical side of interpretation, concerned with the shared language, from a psychological side, concerned with the author's individual mind and style, and held that each informs the other. The aim, he said, is to understand an author as well as, or better than, the author did, a task that can never be completed.\n\nWilhelm Dilthey placed hermeneutics at the center of an unfinished critique of historical reason. He wanted to give the human sciences, such as history, philology and law, foundations independent of the natural sciences. Their method is understanding (Verstehen), the grasp of lived experience (Erlebnis) and its expressions, distinct from the causal explanation of external events in the natural sciences. In “The Rise of Hermeneutics” (1900) he argued that hermeneutics secures the validity of interpretation and so the certainty of historical knowledge.\n\nMartin Heidegger transformed the field in Being and Time (1927). His inquiry into human existence is itself interpretation, a hermeneutics of facticity: understanding is a basic way of being, and human beings interpret themselves and their world before they ever theorize. Understanding is shaped by fore-structures and moods, we are thrown into situations we did not choose, and everyday life tends to cover over its own structures by absorbing us into the anonymous “they” (das Man).\n\nHans-Georg Gadamer's Truth and Method (1960) developed a philosophical hermeneutics in which understanding is an experience of truth irreducible to scientific method. Understanding is conditioned by tradition and by prejudices, prejudgments that can be productive as well as distorting, and Gadamer criticized the Enlightenment's prejudice against prejudice. Because history keeps working on us (Wirkungsgeschichte), self-knowledge is never complete, and understanding is a fusion of the interpreter's horizon with that of the text, reached in something like a conversation; “being that can be understood is language”. Jürgen Habermas objected that so much trust in tradition leaves too little room to criticize ideology and distorted power, and Gadamer replied that every critique itself stands within a tradition.\n\nHeidegger and Gadamer made interpretation a basic feature of human existence; Paul Ricoeur preferred a “long route” to self-understanding, through the interpretation of symbols, texts and the findings of other disciplines. He set a hermeneutics of faith beside a hermeneutics of suspicion, which he found in Marx, Nietzsche and Freud, and later studied metaphor and narrative. In his encounter with Gadamer in 1981, Jacques Derrida argued that meaning is always deferred, and later writers such as Gianni Vattimo and Richard Rorty took hermeneutics in postmodern and pragmatist directions.",
    s: "Schleiermacher, Hermeneutics and Criticism (1838); Gadamer, Truth and Method (1960); Ricoeur, Freud and Philosophy (1965)",
    p: ["schleiermacher", "dilthey", "heidegger", "gadamer", "ricoeur"], r: ["hermeneutic-circle", "phenomenology", "deconstruction", "genealogy"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["hermeneutics", "hermeneutic"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hermeneutics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hermeneutics”" },
  { id: "hermeneutic-circle", fig: "hermeneutic-circle", t: "Hermeneutic circle", a: "phen",
    d: "Understanding the parts of a text requires a grasp of the whole, and grasping the whole requires understanding the parts.",
    x: "Heidegger argued that the circle is unavoidable and productive: interpretation always starts from a fore-understanding, and the task is to work that fore-understanding out from the things themselves. Gadamer rehabilitated “prejudice” in its sense of pre-judgment, and described understanding as a fusion of the interpreter's horizon with the horizon of the text.\n\nThe idea began in philology. Friedrich Ast and Friedrich Schleiermacher noted that a word is understood from its sentence, a sentence from the whole work, and the work from its author's life and its genre, each in turn clarifying the others; Schleiermacher paired grammatical interpretation of the language with psychological interpretation of the author. Wilhelm Dilthey extended the circle to the human sciences generally, since a life too is understood from its parts and its parts from the whole. E. D. Hirsch, in Validity in Interpretation (1967), argued against Gadamer that the author's intended meaning sets the standard for a correct reading.",
    s: "Heidegger, Being and Time (1927), §32; Gadamer, Truth and Method (1960), Part II",
    p: ["schleiermacher", "heidegger", "gadamer"], r: ["hermeneutics", "coherentism"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["hermeneutic circle", "fusion of horizons"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hermeneutics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hermeneutics”" },
  { id: "existentialism", t: "Existentialism", a: "phen",
    d: "Philosophy centered on concrete human existence, freedom and responsibility. Sartre's formula: existence precedes essence.",
    x: "Kierkegaard insisted on the single individual and the leap of faith against Hegel's system, and Nietzsche on the creation of values after the death of God. Sartre argued that human beings have no fixed nature and are condemned to be free, responsible for what they make of themselves. Beauvoir's The Second Sex analyzed how woman is made the Other, and Camus explored the absurd. The label was mostly applied by others, and Heidegger rejected it.",
    s: "Kierkegaard, Fear and Trembling (1843); Sartre, Existentialism Is a Humanism (1946); Beauvoir, The Second Sex (1949)",
    p: ["kierkegaard", "nietzsche", "heidegger", "sartre", "beauvoir", "camus"], r: ["bad-faith", "essence", "dasein", "phenomenology", "essence-existence"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["existentialism", "existentialist", "existence precedes essence"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Existentialism”" },
  { id: "bad-faith", t: "Bad faith", a: "phen",
    d: "Sartre: self-deception by which a person flees their own freedom, for example by treating themselves as fixed by a role or by circumstance.",
    x: "Sartre's café waiter plays at being a waiter, as if his role fixed what he is. Bad faith exploits the double nature of human reality: we are both facticity (body, past, situation) and transcendence (the freedom to surpass them). Beauvoir extends the analysis to women who flee freedom into roles defined by men, and Lewis Gordon applies it to antiblack racism.\n\nSartre's other examples include a woman on a first date who lets her companion take her hand and leaves it there as though it were a thing, neither consenting nor refusing, and the “champion of sincerity” who demands that a friend admit what he “is”, as though a person were fixed like an object. Bad faith differs from lying, since the liar knows the truth he hides from someone else, while in bad faith the deceiver and the deceived are one person. That raises the puzzle of how one can hide something from oneself, and Sartre rejects Freud's unconscious as a solution, arguing that the censor would have to know what it represses.",
    s: "Sartre, Being and Nothingness (1943), Part I, ch. 2; Gordon, Bad Faith and Antiblack Racism (1995)",
    p: ["sartre", "beauvoir", "lewisgordon"], r: ["existentialism", "naturalization", "double-consciousness"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["bad faith", "mauvaise foi"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sartre/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jean-Paul Sartre”" },
  // Critical theory: Hegel, Marx and the Frankfurt School
  { id: "dialectic", t: "Dialectic", a: "crit",
    d: "In Hegel, the movement by which a concept or form of life produces its own contradictions and passes into a richer form that preserves what it overcomes. The formula “thesis, antithesis, synthesis” comes from Fichte; Hegel never used it to describe his own philosophy.",
    x: "Plato's dialectic is the art of question and answer that rises toward the Forms; Kant's transcendental dialectic exposes the illusions reason falls into when it passes the limits of experience. Hegel's dialectic is the self-movement of concepts through determinate negation. Marx and Engels gave it a materialist form, and Adorno's negative dialectics refuses the final reconciliation that Hegel's system promised.",
    s: "Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit (1807), Preface; Science of Logic (1812–16); Adorno, Negative Dialectics (1966)",
    p: ["socrates", "plato", "kant", "fichte", "hegel", "marx", "adorno"], r: ["aufhebung", "immanent-critique", "historical-materialism", "recognition", "tautology"], br: ["german-idealism", "marxism"], k: ["dialectic", "dialectics", "dialectical", "negative dialectics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hegel/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel”" },
  { id: "aufhebung", t: "Aufhebung (sublation)", a: "crit",
    d: "Hegel's term for a kind of overcoming that at once cancels, preserves and lifts up what it overcomes.",
    x: "Hegel delights in the fact that the German verb aufheben means both to cancel and to preserve, and notes that what is sublated survives as a moment of a richer whole. English translators have used “sublation”, “supersession” and “overcoming”. The term marks how Hegel's dialectic keeps what it criticizes.\n\nHegel's Science of Logic opens with a classic example. Pure being, with no determination at all, turns out to be indistinguishable from nothing; the truth of both is becoming, the passing of each into the other, in which being and nothing are each cancelled as independent and kept as moments. Each later category of the Logic arises the same way, from the instability of the one before it.",
    s: "Hegel, Science of Logic (1812), Remark on the expression “sublation”",
    p: ["hegel"], r: ["dialectic", "immanent-critique"], br: ["german-idealism"], k: ["aufhebung", "sublation", "sublate"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hegel/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel”" },
  { id: "immanent-critique", t: "Immanent critique", a: "crit",
    d: "Criticism that measures a position or a society by its own standards, and exposes the contradictions within it.",
    x: "Hegel's Phenomenology proceeds this way, letting each form of consciousness fail by its own criterion. Marx criticized bourgeois society by its own promises of freedom and equality. Horkheimer and Adorno made it the method of Frankfurt critical theory, which claims no standpoint outside society and works from within its contradictions.\n\nAdorno held that late capitalist society is so integrated that no standpoint outside it remains from which to criticize it, so criticism must work from within, confronting concepts and institutions with their own promises. Negative Dialectics turns this method on philosophy itself, criticizing Kant and Hegel by their own standards, and Habermas later sought firmer normative ground for critique in the presuppositions of communication.",
    s: "Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit (1807), Introduction; Adorno, Negative Dialectics (1966)",
    p: ["hegel", "marx", "horkheimer", "adorno"], r: ["dialectic", "critical-theory", "immanence", "ideology"], br: ["critical-theory"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/adorno/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Theodor W. Adorno”" },
  { id: "alienation", t: "Alienation", a: "crit",
    d: "Marx: under capitalism workers are estranged from the product of their labor, from their own activity, from their species-being and from one another.",
    x: "Alienation names a social or psychological ill in which a subject is separated, in a problematic way, from something that properly belongs with it. David Leopold's Stanford Encyclopedia article identifies three elements: a subject, an individual or a group; an object, which may be a thing, another person or oneself; and a separation that frustrates some baseline of harmony or connection. Unhealthy entanglement differs from alienation: someone overly absorbed in her family has a troubled relation to it without being estranged from it.\n\nThe English word was in use by the early fifteenth century, in senses including estrangement from God, the legal transfer of property and mental derangement. Hegel usually used Entäusserung for transferring property and Entfremdung for estrangement. Leopold calls Rousseau's Second Discourse (1754–55) perhaps the first sophisticated philosophical treatment: in civilized society inflamed self-love divides people from one another and from their own nature.\n\nHegel and Feuerbach had used alienation for the estrangement of spirit, or of humanity, from its own products, and for Hegel the overcoming of alienation meant being at home in the world. Feuerbach read God as humanity's alienated essence: people project their own best qualities onto an ideal being, which then rules over them. The young Marx took up this pattern of a human creation that escapes and dominates its creators and applied it to economic life.\n\nMarx made alienation economic. In the manuscripts of 1844 he described workers under capitalism as estranged from the product of their labor, from the activity of laboring, from their human species-being and from other people. The worker's product confronts them as an alien power that belongs to capital. In the Communist Manifesto (1848) modern bourgeois society is a sorcerer who can no longer control the powers he has summoned, and in Capital (1867) the domination of producers by their products returns in the analysis of commodity fetishism, which Leopold treats as one form alienation can take.\n\nLeopold distinguishes subjective alienation, a matter of feeling estranged, meaningless or not at home, from objective alienation, the frustration of essential human capacities whether or not anyone feels it. The two can come apart. On his reading, Hegel's modern world shows subjective alienation without the objective kind, Herbert Marcuse's One-Dimensional Man (1964) describes people who are objectively alienated without feeling it, and Marx saw both at once in capitalism.\n\nThe concept needs a standard of what belongs together, and that standard has traditionally been human nature: separations count as problematic when they frustrate human flourishing. Critics worry that this ties the concept to a contestable theory of human nature. Rahel Jaeggi's Alienation (2005; English 2014) offers a thinner standard, appropriation, the ability to make one's life and actions one's own. Alienation is a failure of appropriation, and she describes four forms: powerlessness, loss of authenticity, inner division and indifference.\n\nThe idea has traveled widely. Existentialists such as Sartre and Camus described a social world that remains other to us, Lukács's account of reification and Marcuse's critique of consumer society carried Marx's version forward, and sociologists have used it for powerlessness and meaninglessness at work, measured through proxies such as job satisfaction. In ethics, Peter Railton (1984) argued that an impartial morality can alienate people from their own attachments and projects.",
    s: "Marx, Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844; Feuerbach, The Essence of Christianity (1841)",
    p: ["hegel", "feuerbach", "marx", "lukacs", "marcuse"], r: ["species-being", "reification", "commodity-fetishism", "surplus-value"], br: ["marxism"], k: ["alienation", "estrangement"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/alienation/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Alienation”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx”" }] },
  { id: "species-being", t: "Species-being", a: "crit",
    d: "Marx's term, taken from Feuerbach, for what is distinctive of human beings: free, conscious, productive activity through which they shape nature and recognize themselves in a world they have made.",
    x: "In the 1844 Manuscripts, alienated labor estranges workers from their species-being, turning their life activity into a mere means of staying alive. The concept shows the humanist, Hegelian side of the young Marx. Althusser argued that the mature Marx broke with this humanism, a reading that remains disputed.\n\nThe German Gattungswesen comes from Feuerbach, for whom human beings are distinguished by consciousness of their species, of humanity as a whole, which religion projects onto God. Marx gave the idea a practical turn: humans affirm their species-being by transforming nature through free, conscious labor and recognizing themselves in what they make, which is why, on his account, alienated labor estranges workers from their own humanity.",
    s: "Marx, Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844; Feuerbach, The Essence of Christianity (1841); Althusser, For Marx (1965)",
    p: ["feuerbach", "marx", "althusser"], r: ["alienation", "recognition"], br: ["marxism"], k: ["species-being", "species being", "gattungswesen"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx”" },
  { id: "commodity-fetishism", t: "Commodity fetishism", a: "crit",
    d: "Marx: the social relations between producers appear as relations between things, the commodities they exchange.",
    x: "Marx compares it to religion, in which the products of the human brain appear as independent beings. Because producers meet only through the exchange of their products, the social character of their labor appears as a property of things: value. Lukács developed the idea into reification, and Benjamin and Adorno traced it through mass culture.\n\nThe section on fetishism ends the first chapter of Capital. Marx argued that the categories of political economy, value, price, capital, appear natural and eternal because the social relations behind them are hidden in the movement of things. Only by comparing other forms of production, a medieval manor or a free association of producers, can one see that value is a historically specific social form.",
    s: "Marx, Capital, vol. 1 (1867), ch. 1, sec. 4",
    p: ["marx", "lukacs", "benjamin", "adorno"], r: ["reification", "use-exchange-value", "alienation", "culture-industry", "aura"], br: ["marxism", "critical-theory"], k: ["commodity fetishism", "fetishism of commodities", "fetish-character"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx”" },
  { id: "reification", t: "Reification", a: "crit",
    d: "Lukács: relations between people take on the character of a thing and acquire a “phantom objectivity”.",
    x: "In “Reification and the Consciousness of the Proletariat”, Lukács argued that the commodity form shapes all of modern life, from the calculating rationality of bureaucracy and science to the contemplative stance of bourgeois philosophy. Only the proletariat, he argued, can grasp society as a historical totality. The concept shaped the Frankfurt School, and Honneth later reinterpreted reification as a forgetting of recognition.",
    s: "Lukács, History and Class Consciousness (1923); Honneth, Reification (2005)",
    p: ["lukacs", "adorno", "honneth"], r: ["commodity-fetishism", "class-consciousness", "alienation", "instrumental-reason"], br: ["marxism", "critical-theory"], k: ["reification", "reified", "phantom objectivity"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lukacs/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Georg Lukács”" },
  { id: "class-consciousness", t: "Class consciousness", a: "crit",
    d: "A class's awareness of its shared position and interests within the relations of production, and of itself as a collective agent. Marxists distinguish a class “in itself”, defined by its objective position, from a class “for itself”, organized and conscious.",
    x: "The distinction grows out of Marx's Poverty of Philosophy. Lukács argued that the proletariat's position allows it to grasp society as a totality, and separated the consciousness that can be imputed to a class, what it would think if it understood its situation, from the actual beliefs of workers. Engels used the phrase “false consciousness” for beliefs whose real motives remain hidden from the believer. Debates over how class consciousness forms run through Gramsci, E. P. Thompson's The Making of the English Working Class (1963) and standpoint theory.",
    s: "Marx, The Poverty of Philosophy (1847); Lukács, History and Class Consciousness (1923); Engels, letter to Franz Mehring (1893)",
    p: ["marx", "engels", "lukacs", "gramsci"], r: ["reification", "ideology", "standpoint", "hegemony"], br: ["marxism"], k: ["class consciousness", "false consciousness"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lukacs/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Georg Lukács”" },
  { id: "base-superstructure", fig: "base-superstructure", t: "Base and superstructure", a: "crit",
    d: "Marx: the economic structure of society, its base, conditions the legal, political and ideological forms that rise on it, its superstructure.",
    x: "In the 1859 Preface Marx writes that social being determines consciousness. Engels later warned against reading this as strict economic determinism, in his letter to Joseph Bloch (1890). Gramsci's hegemony, Althusser's relative autonomy of ideology and Raymond Williams's cultural materialism are attempts to think the relation more flexibly.\n\nIn the 1859 Preface Marx described the economic structure as the real foundation on which a legal and political superstructure rises, corresponding to definite forms of social consciousness. G. A. Cohen's analytical reconstruction read the relation functionally: the superstructure takes the form it does because that form stabilizes the economic structure, just as the economic structure is explained by its fitness to develop the productive forces.",
    s: "Marx, Preface to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy (1859); Engels, letter to Joseph Bloch (1890)",
    p: ["marx", "engels", "gramsci", "althusser", "williams"], r: ["historical-materialism", "ideology", "hegemony"], br: ["marxism"], k: ["base and superstructure", "superstructure"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx”" },
  { id: "historical-materialism", t: "Historical materialism", a: "crit",
    d: "Marx and Engels' account of history as driven by the development of material production and by class struggle. Changes in the forces of production (technology, skills) come into conflict with the relations of production (property, class) and drive social transformation.",
    x: "Marx and Engels developed it against the Young Hegelians, for whom ideas drive history. The Communist Manifesto opens with the claim that the history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles. Debates continue over technological determinism, defended in G. A. Cohen's reading, and over the roles of politics, culture and contingency.\n\nThe classic statement is the 1859 Preface. In producing their lives, people enter relations of production that answer to a given stage of their productive forces; together these relations form the economic structure, the real foundation on which a legal and political superstructure arises, and social existence determines consciousness. When the forces outgrow the relations, the relations become fetters and an era of social revolution begins. Marx named the Asiatic, ancient, feudal and modern bourgeois modes of production as progressive epochs. In an 1890 letter to Joseph Bloch, Engels insisted that production is the determining element only “in the last instance”, and that politics, law and ideas act back on it.",
    s: "Marx and Engels, The German Ideology (1845–46); The Communist Manifesto (1848); Marx, Preface to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy (1859)",
    p: ["marx", "engels"], r: ["base-superstructure", "dialectic", "primitive-accumulation", "materialism"], br: ["marxism", "history"], k: ["historical materialism", "class struggle"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx”" },
  { id: "use-exchange-value", t: "Use value and exchange value", a: "crit",
    d: "Marx: a commodity's use value is its usefulness, the need it meets. Its exchange value is the proportion in which it exchanges for other commodities, which expresses the value it contains.",
    x: "Aristotle had distinguished using a shoe from exchanging it, and Marx cites him. Capital begins with this twofold character of the commodity and traces it to the twofold character of labor: concrete labor produces use values, abstract labor produces value. Money is the commodity in which exchange value becomes independent, and the tension between use and exchange runs through later critiques of commodification.",
    s: "Marx, Capital, vol. 1 (1867), ch. 1; Aristotle, Politics I.9",
    p: ["aristotle", "marx"], r: ["commodity-fetishism", "surplus-value"], br: ["marxism"], k: ["use value", "use-value", "exchange value", "exchange-value", "abstract labor", "abstract labour"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx”" },
  { id: "surplus-value", img: { file: "Zentralbibliothek_Zürich_Das_Kapital_Marx_1867.jpg", alt: "Title page of the first volume of Das Kapital", cap: "Title page of the first edition of Das Kapital, volume one (1867; Zentralbibliothek Zürich). Public domain, via" }, t: "Surplus value", a: "crit",
    d: "Marx: the difference between the value that labor-power creates in production and the value of labor-power itself, paid as wages. The capitalist appropriates it, and it is the source of profit.",
    x: "Workers sell their capacity to work for a day, and in that day they produce more value than their wages. Capital raises surplus value absolutely, by lengthening the working day, and relatively, by raising productivity. Marx calls the ratio of surplus value to wages the rate of exploitation.\n\nMarx distinguished constant capital, spent on means of production, which only transfers its existing value to the product, from variable capital, spent on labor power, which alone creates new value. The labor theory of value on which the account rests faces technical objections, such as the transformation problem of relating labor values to market prices, though some philosophers argue that the concept of exploitation can be defended without it.",
    s: "Marx, Capital, vol. 1 (1867), parts 3–5",
    p: ["marx"], r: ["use-exchange-value", "primitive-accumulation", "alienation", "social-reproduction"], br: ["marxism"], k: ["surplus value", "surplus-value", "labor-power", "labour-power"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx”" },
  { id: "primitive-accumulation", t: "Primitive accumulation", a: "crit",
    d: "Marx: the historical process, through enclosure, expropriation and colonial plunder, that separated producers from their means of production and made capitalist relations possible.",
    x: "Marx mocked the political economists' story of a frugal elite and an idle majority, and documented how the English peasantry was driven off the land by enclosure and by “bloody legislation against the expropriated”. Federici argues that the witch hunts and the disciplining of women's bodies belong to the same process, which redefined the sexual division of labor. David Harvey's “accumulation by dispossession” holds that such processes continue today through privatization and debt.",
    s: "Marx, Capital, vol. 1 (1867), part 8; Federici, Caliban and the Witch (2004); Harvey, The New Imperialism (2003)",
    p: ["marx", "federici"], r: ["racial-capitalism", "social-reproduction", "surplus-value", "historical-materialism"], br: ["marxism", "history"], k: ["primitive accumulation", "original accumulation", "accumulation by dispossession"], src: "https://rcientificas.uninorte.edu.co/index.php/investigacion/article/view/11930", srcName: "Review of Federici, Caliban and the Witch, Investigación y Desarrollo 27, no. 1 (2019) (Bárbara Monteiro de Barros da Gama, Universidade Federal de Minas Gerais)" },
  { id: "ideology", t: "Ideology", a: "crit",
    d: "Ideas and representations that express and legitimate existing social relations. For Marx and Engels, the ruling ideas of each epoch are the ideas of its ruling class.",
    x: "Destutt de Tracy coined the word in the 1790s for a science of ideas. Marx and Engels used it for distorted consciousness that hides social contradictions. Later Marxists broadened it: Gramsci tied it to hegemony, Althusser to the practices and apparatuses that make individuals into subjects. Outside Marxism, “ideology” can mean any comprehensive political worldview.\n\nThe German Ideology compares ideology to a camera obscura, in which people and their circumstances appear upside down. Engels, in an 1893 letter to Franz Mehring, spoke of “false consciousness”, a thinker's ignorance of the real forces moving his thought. Karl Mannheim's Ideology and Utopia (1929) turned the concept into a sociology of knowledge, distinguishing a particular conception, which unmasks an opponent's specific claims, from a total conception, which traces a whole worldview to its social position. The Frankfurt School located ideology in the products of the culture industry, and Stuart Hall studied how media encode and audiences decode it.",
    s: "Marx and Engels, The German Ideology (1845–46); Althusser, “Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses” (1970)",
    p: ["marx", "engels", "gramsci", "althusser"], r: ["hegemony", "interpellation", "naturalization", "base-superstructure"], br: ["marxism", "political"], k: ["ideology", "ideological"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/althusser/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Louis Althusser”" },
  { id: "hegemony", t: "Hegemony", a: "crit",
    d: "Gramsci: a class rules through consent as well as coercion, by making its view of the world into society's common sense.",
    x: "The concept is central to the Prison Notebooks (1929–35). Gramsci contrasted Russia, where state power was strong and civil society weak, with modern states, where capitalist rule rests on consent generated across a sturdy civil society, through schools, churches, associations, newspapers and intellectuals, as well as on coercion. Countering it calls for a “war of position”, the gradual winning of strongholds, in place of a “war of manoeuvre”. Stuart Hall drew on hegemony in the 1980s debates over Thatcherism, and Laclau and Mouffe (1985) recast it as the basis of a strategy of “radical democracy”.",
    s: "Gramsci, Prison Notebooks (1929–35); Laclau and Mouffe, Hegemony and Socialist Strategy (1985)",
    p: ["gramsci", "hall"], r: ["ideology", "subaltern", "interpellation", "base-superstructure"], br: ["marxism", "political"], k: ["hegemony", "hegemonic", "war of position"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/gramsci/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Antonio Gramsci” (James Martin)" },
  { id: "interpellation", t: "Interpellation", a: "crit",
    d: "Althusser: ideology “hails” individuals and in doing so constitutes them as subjects.",
    x: "Althusser's example is a police officer calling “Hey, you there!”: the person who turns around recognizes themselves as the one addressed and so becomes a subject. Ideology, he argues, exists in material practices and in Ideological State Apparatuses such as school, family, church and media. Butler revisits the scene in her account of subjection and performativity.\n\nFor Althusser, ideology represents the imaginary relationship of individuals to their real conditions of existence. He distinguishes the Repressive State Apparatus (government, police, courts, army and prisons), which works chiefly by force, from the many Ideological State Apparatuses, which work chiefly by ideology, and he argues that in mature capitalism the school has replaced the church as the dominant one. Individuals are “always-already” subjects: even before birth a child is expected, named and placed in a family. The hailing scene is a way of presenting in sequence what happens all at once.",
    s: "Althusser, “Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses” (1970); Butler, The Psychic Life of Power (1997)",
    p: ["althusser", "butler"], r: ["subjectivation", "ideology", "performativity", "hegemony"], br: ["marxism", "poststructuralism"], k: ["interpellation", "interpellate", "interpellated", "interpellates", "ideological state apparatus"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/althusser/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Louis Althusser”" },
  { id: "critical-theory", t: "Critical theory", a: "crit",
    d: "In the narrow sense, the Frankfurt School's program of social theory aimed at emancipation. In a broader sense, a family of critical approaches that includes critical race theory and feminist theory.",
    x: "Critical theory is a family of theories that aim to criticize and transform society by joining normative perspectives with empirically informed analysis of its conflicts and tendencies. Jeffrey Flynn's Stanford Encyclopedia article distinguishes a narrow sense, the work of several generations of thinkers in the Western Marxist tradition known as the Frankfurt School, from a broad sense that includes feminist theory, critical race theory, queer theory and postcolonial and decolonial theory, each reflecting on the emancipatory aims of social movements. The Frankfurt School itself was less a unified school than a loosely connected tradition of argument.\n\nIts institutional home was the Institute for Social Research, founded in Frankfurt in 1923. Max Horkheimer became its director in 1930 and set out its program in an inaugural lecture of 1931, joining philosophy and social theory with psychology, political economy and cultural analysis. After the Nazis closed the Institute it moved by way of Geneva to Columbia University in New York in 1934, and in 1941 Horkheimer, Adorno and Friedrich Pollock took it to Los Angeles. After the war it returned to Frankfurt, directed by Horkheimer from 1951 to 1958 and by Adorno from 1958 to 1969; Habermas took Horkheimer's chair in 1964, and Axel Honneth directed the Institute from 2001 to 2018.\n\nHorkheimer's essay “Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937) remains the standard reference point. Traditional theory, including a positivist social science modeled on the natural sciences, takes the existing social order as given and so tends to reinforce it. Critical theory reflects on its own place in society and aims to unite theory with practice. It “never aims simply at an increase of knowledge as such”: its interest is emancipation, the abolition of social injustice and the creation of reasonable conditions of life, a rational society free of domination.\n\nThe first generation, with Horkheimer, Theodor W. Adorno and Herbert Marcuse at its center and Walter Benjamin, Erich Fromm, Leo Löwenthal, Franz Neumann, Otto Kirchheimer and Siegfried Kracauer in a wider circle, joined Marx with Freud and Weber. They studied authority and its psychological roots in Studies in Authority and the Family (1936), edited by Horkheimer, and The Authoritarian Personality (1950), led by Adorno. Benjamin's essay on the work of art in the age of its technological reproducibility (1936) saw emancipatory possibilities in mass media, while Adorno regarded mass culture mainly as a vehicle of conformity.\n\nHorkheimer and Adorno's Dialectic of Enlightenment, written between 1939 and 1944, first circulated in 1944 and published in 1947, argued that enlightenment reason, shrunk to instrumental rationality, had become an instrument of domination, and introduced the idea of the culture industry. Marcuse's One-Dimensional Man (1964) carried this diagnosis to postwar America, and Adorno developed his own response in Negative Dialectics (1966) and the posthumous Aesthetic Theory (1970).\n\nHabermas reoriented the program toward communication and democracy. The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere (1962) traced the rise and decline of a reasoning public; Knowledge and Human Interests (1968) grounded social criticism in an interest in emancipation; The Theory of Communicative Action (1981) found rational and normative resources in everyday communication and diagnosed a colonization of the lifeworld by money and power; and Between Facts and Norms (1992) gave a discourse theory of law and democracy. Honneth's The Struggle for Recognition (1992) reoriented it again toward recognition, and Nancy Fraser, Seyla Benhabib, Rainer Forst and Rahel Jaeggi have extended it in different directions.\n\nIn the broad sense, critical race theory began in American legal scholarship in the 1970s and 1980s with Derrick Bell, Kimberlé Crenshaw and others, and feminist, queer and postcolonial theorists have developed their own critical programs. Flynn notes that critical theory increasingly understands itself as a critical practice, methodologically plural and closely tied to social struggles, with globalization and transnational democracy among its newer themes.",
    s: "Horkheimer, “Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937)",
    p: ["horkheimer", "adorno", "marcuse", "habermas", "honneth", "bell"], r: ["immanent-critique", "culture-industry", "instrumental-reason", "communicative-action", "intersectionality"], br: ["critical-theory"], k: ["critical theory", "frankfurt school", "critical race theory"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/critical-theory/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Critical Theory”" },
  { id: "culture-industry", t: "Culture industry", a: "crit",
    d: "Horkheimer and Adorno's term for the mass production of culture (film, radio, magazines, popular music) as standardized commodities that train their audiences in conformity.",
    x: "In Dialectic of Enlightenment they argue that the culture industry offers pseudo-individuality: products differ in detail and follow the same formulas, and leisure becomes an extension of work. Amusement promises escape and delivers adaptation. Critics charge the analysis with elitism; cultural studies, from Stuart Hall and the Birmingham school onward, stressed how audiences actively decode what they consume.",
    s: "Horkheimer and Adorno, Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/1947), “The Culture Industry: Enlightenment as Mass Deception”",
    p: ["horkheimer", "adorno", "hall"], r: ["aura", "commodity-fetishism", "instrumental-reason", "reification", "hegemony"], br: ["critical-theory", "aesthetics"], k: ["culture industry", "mass culture"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/adorno/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Theodor W. Adorno”" },
  { id: "instrumental-reason", t: "Instrumental reason", a: "crit",
    d: "Reason reduced to calculating the most efficient means to given ends, with the ends themselves left outside rational discussion.",
    x: "Horkheimer's Eclipse of Reason sets this subjective reason against an older objective reason, which asked what ends are worth pursuing. With Adorno he argued that enlightenment's drive to master nature turns into the domination of human beings. The theme draws on Weber's account of rationalization and the “iron cage”, and Habermas answered it with a theory of communicative reason.",
    s: "Horkheimer, Eclipse of Reason (1947); Horkheimer and Adorno, Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/1947); Weber, The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism (1905)",
    p: ["weber", "horkheimer", "adorno", "marcuse"], r: ["reification", "culture-industry", "communicative-action", "critical-theory"], br: ["critical-theory"], k: ["instrumental reason", "instrumental rationality", "rationalization", "iron cage"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/horkheimer/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Max Horkheimer”" },
  { id: "communicative-action", t: "Communicative action", a: "crit",
    d: "Habermas: action oriented to reaching understanding, in which speakers coordinate by raising validity claims (to truth, rightness and sincerity) that others can accept or contest with reasons.",
    x: "Habermas sets it against strategic action, which aims at success by influencing others. In discourse, participants test contested claims under conditions approaching an “ideal speech situation”, where only the force of the better argument counts. This grounds his discourse ethics, his theory of deliberative democracy, and his worry that markets and bureaucracies colonize the lifeworld.",
    s: "Habermas, The Theory of Communicative Action (1981); Between Facts and Norms (1992)",
    p: ["habermas"], r: ["lifeworld", "instrumental-reason", "critical-theory", "discourse"], br: ["critical-theory", "political"], k: ["communicative action", "communicative rationality", "ideal speech situation", "public sphere"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/habermas/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jürgen Habermas”" },
  // Critical theory: structuralism, post-structuralism and Foucault
  { id: "structuralism", t: "Structuralism", a: "crit",
    d: "The view that meaning and identity arise from differential relations within a system. Elements mean nothing in isolation; they mean by their differences from other elements.",
    x: "Saussure's Course in General Linguistics (1916) gave the model: the link between signifier and signified is set by a network of differential relations within language itself. Lévi-Strauss built his structural anthropology on Saussure's differential conception of the symbolic order, Lacan's rereading of Freud took the work of Saussure and Jakobson as its precondition, and Roland Barthes's semiotics also descends from Saussure.\n\nGary Aylesworth's Stanford Encyclopedia article on postmodernism explains that the French thinkers it covers, among them Foucault, Deleuze and Derrida, are often called poststructuralists because they work with concepts developed “during the structuralist revolution in Paris in the 1950s and early 1960s”.",
    s: "Saussure, Course in General Linguistics (1916); Lévi-Strauss, Structural Anthropology (1958)",
    p: ["saussure", "levistrauss", "lacan", "barthes"], r: ["signifier", "post-structuralism", "differance"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["structuralism", "structuralist"], src: "https://cahiers.kingston.ac.uk/concepts/linguistics.html", srcName: "Cahiers pour l'Analyse (Kingston University), “Linguistics”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2014/entries/postmodernism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Postmodernism” (Gary Aylesworth), Spring 2014 edition" }] },
  { id: "signifier", fig: "signifier", t: "Signifier and signified", a: "crit",
    d: "Saussure: a sign unites a sound-image, the signifier, with a concept, the signified. The bond between them is arbitrary.",
    x: "Arbitrary here means unmotivated: nothing in the sound “tree” resembles a tree. In language, Saussure says, there are only differences without positive terms. Lacan built on Saussure's account in his return to Freud, and Derrida's différance names a play of differences in which no meaning is ever simply present.\n\nSaussure's model has two levels. Within a sign, the bond between signifier and signified is arbitrary. Within the language system, each sign's value comes from its differences from the other signs around it, so that what “sheep” means in English is partly fixed by the existence of “mutton,” a distinction French draws differently. This model of meaning as difference became the starting point of structuralism.",
    s: "Saussure, Course in General Linguistics (1916)",
    p: ["saussure", "barthes", "lacan", "derrida"], r: ["structuralism", "differance", "deconstruction"], br: ["poststructuralism", "language"], k: ["signifier", "signified", "semiotics", "semiology"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lacan/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jacques Lacan” (Adrian Johnston)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/postmodernism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Postmodernism” (Gary Aylesworth)" }] },
  { id: "differance", t: "Différance", a: "crit",
    d: "Derrida's coinage from the French différer, which means both “to differ” and “to defer”: a sign means by its differences from other signs, and its meaning is always deferred along a chain of further signs, never fully present.",
    x: "The silent “a” can be seen in writing and cannot be heard in speech, which suits Derrida's argument against the priority of speech over writing. He first used the word in a 1963 paper and set it out in the 1968 lecture “Différance”. Derrida insisted that it is neither a word nor a concept in the ordinary sense, since it names the condition of any concept's identity.\n\nDerrida builds on Saussure's claim that in language there are only differences without positive terms: a sign has its value through its contrasts with the other signs in the system. Différance names the movement that produces these differences and keeps meaning from ever coming to rest. Every element carries the trace of the others it is defined against, so no term is simply present to itself. Derrida uses the idea against what he calls the metaphysics of presence, the long tradition that grounds meaning and truth in something immediately present, such as a speaker's voice, an intention or an origin.",
    s: "Derrida, “Différance” (1968), in Margins of Philosophy (1972)",
    p: ["saussure", "derrida"], r: ["deconstruction", "signifier", "structuralism", "post-structuralism"], br: ["poststructuralism", "language"], k: ["différance", "differance"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/derrida/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jacques Derrida”" },
  { id: "post-structuralism", t: "Post-structuralism", a: "crit",
    d: "A name for French thinkers, among them Derrida, Foucault and Deleuze, who worked with concepts from the structuralist revolution of the 1950s and early 1960s while questioning the stability of structures, origins and the unified subject.",
    x: "They share a suspicion of fixed structures and of origins (Foucault's genealogy opposes itself to the search for origins), attention to how meaning is deferred and how power operates through discourse, and a critique of the self-transparent subject. Major works appeared from the late 1960s, including Derrida's Of Grammatology (1967), Kristeva's Revolution in Poetic Language (1974), Foucault's Discipline and Punish (1975) and Lyotard's The Postmodern Condition (1979).",
    p: ["barthes", "derrida", "foucault", "kristeva", "lyotard"], r: ["structuralism", "deconstruction", "discourse", "differance", "genealogy"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["post-structuralism", "poststructuralism", "post-structuralist", "postmodernism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/postmodernism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Postmodernism” (Gary Aylesworth)" },
  { id: "deconstruction", t: "Deconstruction", a: "crit",
    d: "Derrida's practice of reading, which shows how a text's hierarchical oppositions (speech and writing, presence and absence) depend on the term they subordinate.",
    x: "Derrida criticized the “metaphysics of presence”, the privileging of speech, consciousness and origin as immediately present. Deconstruction first reverses a hierarchy and then displaces it, showing the subordinated term at work inside the dominant one. It spread to literary studies through Paul de Man and the Yale critics and to law, architecture and political theory; Derrida insisted it is neither a method nor a purely negative critique.",
    s: "Derrida, Of Grammatology (1967); “Différance” (1968)",
    p: ["derrida"], r: ["differance", "signifier", "post-structuralism", "hermeneutics"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["deconstruction", "deconstruct", "deconstructive", "metaphysics of presence"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/derrida/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jacques Derrida”" },
  { id: "discourse", t: "Discourse", a: "crit",
    d: "In Foucault, a regulated system of statements and practices that forms the objects it speaks about and decides who may speak about them with authority.",
    x: "Foucault's archaeology studies discursive formations: the rules that govern which statements can be made, by whom, about which objects. In his later work discourse is bound up with power, as in the medical, psychiatric and penal knowledges that classify people. Others use the word differently: for Habermas, discourse is argumentation aimed at justified agreement, and in linguistics it simply means language above the level of the sentence.",
    s: "Foucault, The Archaeology of Knowledge (1969); “The Order of Discourse” (1970)",
    p: ["foucault", "habermas"], r: ["power-knowledge", "episteme", "genealogy", "naturalization", "orientalism"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["discourse", "discursive"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/foucault/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Michel Foucault”" },
  { id: "power-knowledge", t: "Power/knowledge", a: "crit",
    d: "Foucault: power and knowledge directly imply one another. Power relations constitute fields of knowledge, and knowledge in turn sustains relations of power.",
    x: "Foucault rejects the picture of knowledge as neutral and of power as purely repressive: “power produces; it produces reality; it produces domains of objects and rituals of truth”. The human sciences arise together with disciplinary institutions, such as prisons, clinics and schools, that observe, examine and normalize individuals.\n\nDiscipline and Punish gives the clearest example. The examination, in school, hospital or barracks, combines the observing gaze with normalizing judgment: it makes each individual a case, documented and compared with a norm, and so produces knowledge of individuals that serves their control. Foucault's point is that the human sciences, psychiatry, criminology, pedagogy, emerged from such practices and continue to support them.",
    s: "Foucault, Discipline and Punish (1975), Part 3; Power/Knowledge (interviews, 1980)",
    p: ["foucault"], r: ["discourse", "panopticism", "biopower", "episteme", "orientalism"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["power/knowledge", "power-knowledge"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/foucault/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Michel Foucault”" },
  { id: "episteme", t: "Episteme", a: "crit",
    d: "Foucault: the underlying order that, in a given period, determines what can count as knowledge and how things can be known. He also calls it a “historical a priori”.",
    x: "The Order of Things describes three epistemes: the Renaissance, organized by resemblance; the Classical age, by representation and ordered tables; and the modern, in which “man” appears as both object and subject of knowledge. The book ends by wagering that man may be erased “like a face drawn in sand at the edge of the sea”. Foucault later moved from archaeology to genealogy and the study of power.",
    s: "Foucault, The Order of Things (1966)",
    p: ["foucault"], r: ["discourse", "paradigm", "power-knowledge", "a-priori"], br: ["poststructuralism", "epistemology"], k: ["episteme", "epistemes", "historical a priori"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/foucault/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Michel Foucault”" },
  { id: "genealogy", t: "Genealogy", a: "crit",
    d: "A historical method that traces how values, concepts or institutions came to be, exposing their contingent and often conflictual origins.",
    x: "Nietzsche's Genealogy traces good and evil, guilt and the ascetic ideal to struggles between forces, and asks what value our values have. Foucault took up the method to show how practices of punishment, sexuality and madness came to seem natural, attending to descent (Herkunft) and emergence (Entstehung) and treating the search for a pure origin with suspicion. Exposing a belief's history can weaken its claim to be natural or necessary, which makes genealogy a tool of critique.",
    s: "Nietzsche, On the Genealogy of Morality (1887), Preface §6; Foucault, “Nietzsche, Genealogy, History” (1971)",
    p: ["nietzsche", "foucault"], r: ["naturalization", "discourse", "power-knowledge", "ressentiment", "will-to-power"], br: ["poststructuralism", "history"], k: ["genealogy", "genealogical"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/foucault/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Michel Foucault”" },
  { id: "biopower", t: "Biopower", a: "crit",
    d: "Foucault: power that takes life itself as its object, both by disciplining individual bodies and by regulating whole populations.",
    x: "Foucault describes a shift from the sovereign's ancient right “to take life or let live” to a power “to foster life or disallow it to the point of death”. Biopower has two poles: an anatomo-politics of the body (discipline) and a biopolitics of the population (birth rates, health, longevity). In the 1976 lectures he argues that racism lets biopower divide the population and justify killing. Agamben and Mbembe develop the concept toward bare life and necropolitics.",
    s: "Foucault, The History of Sexuality, vol. 1 (1976), Part 5; “Society Must Be Defended” (lectures 1975–76)",
    p: ["foucault", "agamben", "mbembe"], r: ["governmentality", "necropolitics", "sovereignty", "panopticism", "state-of-exception"], br: ["poststructuralism", "political"], k: ["biopower", "biopolitics", "biopolitical"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/foucault/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Michel Foucault”" },
  { id: "governmentality", t: "Governmentality", a: "crit",
    d: "Foucault: the art of governing understood as the “conduct of conduct”, the ways institutions, experts and individuals themselves shape how people behave.",
    x: "Foucault traces the arts of government from Christian pastoral power through reason of state and police to liberalism, which governs through the freedom of individuals and markets. His lectures The Birth of Biopolitics analyze neoliberalism as the extension of market rationality to every area of life. Governmentality studies apply the concept to welfare, risk, health and the self-management of individuals.",
    s: "Foucault, Security, Territory, Population (lectures 1977–78); The Birth of Biopolitics (lectures 1978–79)",
    p: ["foucault"], r: ["biopower", "power-knowledge", "panopticism", "subjectivation"], br: ["poststructuralism", "political"], k: ["governmentality", "conduct of conduct", "neoliberalism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/foucault/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Michel Foucault”" },
  { id: "panopticism", t: "Panopticism", a: "crit",
    d: "Foucault's name for disciplinary power modeled on Bentham's Panopticon, a prison in which inmates can always be seen and never know when they are watched, so that they come to watch themselves.",
    x: "Bentham published the Panopticon design in 1791: a central tower surrounded by a ring of cells. For Foucault it is a diagram of power that can be applied in schools, hospitals, factories and barracks: visibility is a trap, and the permanent possibility of observation produces self-discipline. Later writers describe digital surveillance as working through data collection, prediction and sorting, as in Shoshana Zuboff's account of surveillance capitalism.",
    s: "Bentham, Panopticon; or, The Inspection-House (1791); Foucault, Discipline and Punish (1975), Part 3, ch. 3",
    img: { file: "Panopticon_Willey_Reveley_1791.png", alt: "Plan and section of Bentham's circular panopticon prison", cap: "Elevation, section and plan of Bentham's panopticon, drawn by Willey Reveley, 1791. Public domain, via", wide: true },
    p: ["bentham", "foucault"], r: ["power-knowledge", "governmentality", "biopower"], br: ["poststructuralism", "political"], k: ["panopticism", "panopticon", "panoptic", "surveillance"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/bentham/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jeremy Bentham”" },
  { id: "subjectivation", t: "Subjectivation", a: "crit",
    d: "The process by which individuals are formed as subjects. The French assujettissement also means subjection.",
    x: "For Althusser, ideology interpellates individuals as subjects. Foucault studies both the ways disciplines and sciences make people into subjects and the practices of the self through which people shape themselves. Butler joins these accounts with psychoanalysis: the subject is formed through subordination to power, which also makes agency possible.\n\nIn Foucault's late lectures and in the second and third volumes of The History of Sexuality, the emphasis shifts from subjection to self-formation: the technologies of the self, such as self-examination, diet and the regulation of pleasures, through which ancient Greeks and Romans made themselves into ethical subjects. Foucault called the result an ethics as an aesthetics of existence.",
    s: "Foucault, “The Subject and Power” (1982); Butler, The Psychic Life of Power (1997)",
    p: ["althusser", "foucault", "butler"], r: ["interpellation", "performativity", "power-knowledge", "habitus"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["subjectivation", "subjectification", "assujettissement", "subjection"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/foucault/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Michel Foucault”" },
  { id: "naturalization", t: "Naturalization", a: "crit",
    d: "Presenting a historically contingent arrangement as natural, necessary or self-evident. Barthes analyzes myth as the conversion of history into nature.",
    x: "Barthes's example is a Paris-Match cover on which a young Black soldier in French uniform salutes the flag, an image that presents French empire as natural and good. Marx attacked the economists for presenting the relations of bourgeois production as eternal categories. Feminist, queer and critical race theorists analyze how gender, sexuality and race are naturalized.",
    s: "Barthes, Mythologies (1957), “Myth Today”; Marx, The Poverty of Philosophy (1847), ch. 2",
    p: ["marx", "barthes", "foucault", "butler"], r: ["genealogy", "ideology", "essentialism", "social-construction", "racial-formation"], br: ["poststructuralism", "political"], k: ["naturalization", "naturalize", "naturalizes", "naturalized", "naturalise", "naturalised"] },
  { id: "essentialism", t: "Essentialism", a: "crit",
    d: "The view that a kind of thing, or a social group, has a fixed essence that defines its members.",
    x: "In metaphysics, essentialism holds that things have some of their properties necessarily. In social theory the term is usually critical: it names claims that women, races or nations have fixed natures. Spivak's “strategic essentialism” lets marginalized groups mobilize around shared identities while knowing those identities are constructed.\n\nPhilosophers distinguish essentialism about natural kinds, which Kripke and Putnam revived with claims such as that water is necessarily H2O, from essentialism about social groups. Critics of the second, such as Sally Haslanger, analyze gender as a social position of systematic subordination, while Iris Marion Young and Natalie Stoljar account for women as a group without any shared essence: as a series unified by the practical realities its members live with, or as a resemblance class.",
    s: "Spivak, “Subaltern Studies: Deconstructing Historiography” (1985)",
    p: ["aristotle", "spivak", "butler"], r: ["essence", "naturalization", "negritude"], vs: ["social-construction"], br: ["metaphysics", "feminist"], k: ["essentialism", "essentialist", "anti-essentialism", "strategic essentialism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/essential-accidental/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Essential vs. Accidental Properties” (Philip Atkins, Fabrice Correia and Teresa Robertson Ishii)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-gender/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Feminist Perspectives on Sex and Gender” (Mari Mikkola)" }] },
  { id: "social-construction", t: "Social construction", a: "crit",
    d: "The thesis that a category or fact is produced and sustained by social practices and agreements.",
    x: "Berger and Luckmann described how human activity is externalized, hardens into institutions and is internalized as reality. Hacking asked what exactly is said to be constructed (ideas, objects or kinds), and described a “looping effect of human kinds”: a way of classifying people shapes how they are treated and how they behave, making new ways to be a person. Sally Haslanger analyzes gender as a social position of systematic subordination, and holds that race is real though not grounded in biology.",
    s: "Berger and Luckmann, The Social Construction of Reality (1966); Hacking, The Social Construction of What? (1999); Haslanger, Resisting Reality (2012)",
    p: ["berger", "luckmann", "hacking", "haslanger"], r: ["naturalization", "racial-formation", "performativity", "paradigm"], vs: ["essentialism"], br: ["political", "feminist"], k: ["social construction", "socially constructed", "constructionism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/social-construction-naturalistic/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Naturalistic Approaches to Social Construction” (Ron Mallon)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-gender/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Feminist Perspectives on Sex and Gender” (Mari Mikkola)" }] },
  { id: "habitus", t: "Habitus", a: "crit",
    d: "Bourdieu: durable, transposable dispositions, acquired through a person's social position and history, that generate their practices, perceptions and tastes.",
    x: "Bourdieu used habitus to explain how practices are coordinated “without being the product of the organizing activity of a conductor”: it is the unchosen principle of all choices. Distinction showed how it gives a rough coherence to tastes in music, sport, food and furniture, the distinctive lifestyles of people in the same class. The word goes back to Aristotle's hexis, an acquired yet entrenched state of character, and Mauss used it in “Techniques of the Body” (1934).",
    s: "Bourdieu, Outline of a Theory of Practice (1972); Distinction (1979); Mauss, “Techniques of the Body” (1934)",
    p: ["aristotle", "mauss", "bourdieu"], r: ["subjectivation", "social-construction", "ideology"], br: ["political"], k: ["habitus"], src: "https://loicwacquant.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/lw-2016-a-concise-genealogy-and-anatomy-of-habitus.pdf", srcName: "“A Concise Genealogy and Anatomy of Habitus”, The Sociological Review 64 (2016) (Loïc Wacquant)" },
  // Critical theory: race, gender, colonialism
  { id: "double-consciousness", t: "Double consciousness", a: "crit",
    d: "Du Bois's term for the divided self-awareness of Black Americans: “this sense of always looking at one's self through the eyes of others”.",
    x: "Du Bois describes the veil that separates Black and white America, and a “two-ness” of being both an American and a Negro: “two souls, two thoughts, two unreconciled strivings”. He treated it as a burden and also as a kind of second sight. The idea shaped Black studies and later work on racialized self-perception, including Fanon's account of being fixed by the white gaze.\n\nThe chapter first appeared in 1897 in the Atlantic Monthly as “Strivings of the Negro People”. Du Bois describes the Negro as born “with a veil, and gifted with second-sight in this American world”, and says the history of the American Negro is the longing to merge this double self into a better and truer self while losing neither of the older selves. The phrase had earlier uses, in Emerson and in the psychology of the time. Paul Gilroy's The Black Atlantic (1993) extended double consciousness to the culture of the African diaspora across the Atlantic.",
    s: "Du Bois, The Souls of Black Folk (1903), ch. 1",
    p: ["dubois", "fanon"], r: ["racial-formation", "recognition", "bad-faith", "standpoint", "negritude"], br: ["race"], k: ["double consciousness", "double-consciousness", "two-ness", "the veil"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dubois/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “W. E. B. Du Bois”" },
  { id: "racial-formation", t: "Racial formation", a: "crit",
    d: "Omi and Winant: the social and historical process by which racial categories are created, inhabited, transformed and destroyed.",
    x: "Omi and Winant call race a concept that signifies and symbolizes social conflicts by referring to different types of human bodies. Racial projects link representations of race with the distribution of resources, and the state is a central site of racial politics. The theory rejects both biological essentialism and the claim that race is merely an illusion.\n\nA racial project, in their definition, is at once an interpretation or representation of race and an effort to organize and distribute resources along racial lines, so that culture and structure are always joined. They criticize theories that reduce race to ethnicity, to class or to nation, and they trace how the United States moved from racial dictatorship toward racial hegemony, in which rule works more through consent, and how the movements of the 1960s were met by a conservative rearticulation of racial meanings in the 1970s and 1980s. The book appeared in new editions in 1994 and 2015.",
    s: "Omi and Winant, Racial Formation in the United States (1986; 2nd ed. 1994; 3rd ed. 2015)",
    p: ["omi", "winant"], r: ["racial-capitalism", "social-construction", "naturalization", "racial-contract"], br: ["race"], k: ["racial formation", "racialization", "racialized", "racial project"] },
  { id: "racial-capitalism", t: "Racial capitalism", a: "crit",
    d: "Cedric Robinson's thesis that capitalism emerged from a European social order already structured by racialism, so that race has organized capitalism from its beginnings.",
    x: "Robinson argued that capitalism emerged within the feudal order, in a Western civilization already infused with racialism: the first European proletarians, Irish, Jews, Roma and Slavs among them, were already racial subjects. The system that grew from it depended on slavery, violence, imperialism and genocide, and labor and goods markets have been organized by race since the conquest of the Americas. The last part of Black Marxism traces a Black radical tradition through Du Bois, C. L. R. James and Richard Wright. Movements against state violence and mass incarceration now call for an end to “racial capitalism”.",
    s: "Robinson, Black Marxism: The Making of the Black Radical Tradition (1983)",
    p: ["dubois", "robinson"], r: ["racial-formation", "primitive-accumulation", "surplus-value", "racial-contract"], br: ["race", "marxism"], k: ["racial capitalism", "black radical tradition"], src: "https://bostonreview.net/race/robin-d-g-kelley-what-did-cedric-robinson-mean-racial-capitalism", srcName: "“What Did Cedric Robinson Mean by Racial Capitalism?”, Boston Review (2017) (Robin D. G. Kelley)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/critical-phil-race/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Critical Philosophy of Race” (Linda Alcoff)" }] },
  { id: "racial-contract", t: "Racial contract", a: "crit",
    d: "Charles Mills's argument that the social contract of modern political theory was in practice a racial contract among whites, which established a polity that excluded and exploited non-whites.",
    x: "The contract requires an “epistemology of ignorance”, in Mills's words “a particular pattern of localized and global cognitive dysfunctions”, so that whites will in general be unable to understand the world they themselves have made. He drew on Carole Pateman's The Sexual Contract and later criticized Rawlsian ideal theory for its silence about racial injustice.\n\nMills argued that the racial contract has three dimensions: a political agreement that establishes white supremacy, a moral one that sets non-whites outside full moral standing, and an epistemological one that prescribes the misperceptions he called white ignorance. In Black Rights/White Wrongs (2017) he held that liberalism need not be abandoned, but must be rebuilt as a Black radical liberalism devoted to corrective justice.",
    s: "Mills, The Racial Contract (1997); Pateman, The Sexual Contract (1988)",
    p: ["pateman", "mills", "rawls"], r: ["social-contract", "veil-of-ignorance", "racial-formation", "epistemic-injustice"], br: ["race", "political"], k: ["racial contract", "sexual contract", "epistemology of ignorance"], src: "https://learninglink.oup.com/access/content/ramgotra-choat1e-resources/ramgotra-choat1e-key-thinker-charles-mills", srcName: "Oxford University Press, Rethinking Political Thinkers, “Key thinker: Charles W. Mills (1951–2021)” (Manjeet Ramgotra and Simon Choat, eds.)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/critical-phil-race/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Critical Philosophy of Race” (Linda Alcoff)" }] },
  { id: "intersectionality", t: "Intersectionality", a: "crit",
    d: "Kimberlé Crenshaw's term for the way race, gender and other axes of power combine, producing forms of discrimination that single-axis analysis misses.",
    x: "Crenshaw's examples were legal cases, such as DeGraffenreid v. General Motors, in which courts allowed a claim of race or of sex discrimination but not of the two combined, so that claims by Black women as such fell through the gap. The Combahee River Collective, a Boston Black feminist group, had already described “interlocking” systems of oppression in its Statement of 1977, and Patricia Hill Collins developed the idea of a “matrix of domination”. The concept has spread across many disciplines as a method and framework, along with criticism that it relies on fixed identity categories.",
    s: "Crenshaw, “Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex” (1989); Collins, Black Feminist Thought (1990)",
    p: ["crenshaw", "collins"], r: ["standpoint", "racial-formation", "social-reproduction", "epistemic-injustice"], br: ["race", "feminist"], k: ["intersectionality", "intersectional"], src: "https://keywords.keywords2.hosting.nyu.edu/american-cultural-studies/essay/intersectionality/", srcName: "Keywords for American Cultural Studies (NYU Press), “Intersectionality”" },
  { id: "standpoint", t: "Standpoint theory", a: "crit",
    d: "Feminist epistemology holding that social position shapes knowledge, and that the standpoints of marginalized groups can yield less distorted knowledge of society.",
    x: "Nancy Hartsock adapted Marx's idea that the proletariat's position reveals the workings of capitalism. Sandra Harding argued that starting research from marginalized lives yields “strong objectivity”. Patricia Hill Collins develops a Black feminist standpoint, and Dorothy Smith's sociology begins from women's everyday experience. Critics ask whether a group has a single standpoint, and how social position becomes epistemic advantage.",
    s: "Hartsock, “The Feminist Standpoint” (1983); Harding, Whose Science? Whose Knowledge? (1991); Smith, The Everyday World as Problematic (1987)",
    p: ["hartsock", "harding", "collins", "dorothysmith"], r: ["situated-knowledges", "epistemic-injustice", "intersectionality", "class-consciousness"], br: ["epistemology", "feminist"], k: ["standpoint", "strong objectivity"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminist-philosophy/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Feminist Philosophy”" },
  { id: "epistemic-injustice", t: "Epistemic injustice", a: "know",
    d: "Fricker: a wrong done to someone in their capacity as a knower. In testimonial injustice, prejudice deflates the credibility given to a speaker. In hermeneutical injustice, a gap in shared concepts leaves someone unable to make sense of their own experience.",
    x: "Fricker's examples include testimony dismissed because of the speaker's gender or race, and women's experience of sexual harassment before the concept existed, when victims lacked words for what was happening to them. Kristie Dotson describes forms of “epistemic oppression” and persistent epistemic exclusion, and José Medina develops an “epistemology of resistance”; work on actively produced ignorance draws largely on Charles Mills.\n\nFricker's central literary example of testimonial injustice is the trial of Tom Robinson in Harper Lee's To Kill a Mockingbird, in which an all-white jury disbelieves a Black man's true testimony. Such injustice, she argued, wrongs a person in a capacity essential to human value, as a giver of knowledge, and she proposed corrective virtues of testimonial and hermeneutical justice for hearers. ",
    s: "Fricker, Epistemic Injustice: Power and the Ethics of Knowing (2007)",
    p: ["fricker", "collins"], r: ["standpoint", "situated-knowledges", "intersectionality", "racial-contract"], br: ["epistemology", "feminist"], k: ["epistemic injustice", "testimonial injustice", "hermeneutical injustice"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminist-social-epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Feminist Social Epistemology” (Heidi Grasswick)" },
  { id: "performativity", t: "Performativity", a: "crit",
    d: "Austin: some utterances perform the act they name (“I promise”). Butler: gender is constituted through the repetition of performative acts.",
    x: "Austin distinguished constatives, which describe, from performatives, which act, and later concluded that every utterance does something. Derrida stressed that performatives work through iterability, the citation of conventions. Butler argues that gender has no inner essence behind its expressions; the appearance of a stable gender is produced by stylized repetition, which also opens possibilities of subversion.",
    s: "Austin, How to Do Things with Words (1962); Butler, Gender Trouble (1990); Bodies That Matter (1993)",
    p: ["austin", "derrida", "butler"], r: ["subjectivation", "interpellation", "social-construction", "hyperstition"], vs: ["essentialism"], br: ["language", "feminist"], k: ["performativity", "performative"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/austin-jl/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “John Langshaw Austin”" },
  { id: "social-reproduction", t: "Social reproduction", a: "crit",
    d: "The work, much of it unpaid and done by women, that produces and maintains people and so the workforce: care, domestic labor, education. Social reproduction theory treats it as part of the analysis of capitalism.",
    x: "Lise Vogel's Marxism and the Oppression of Women (1983) set out the framework. In the 1970s Mariarosa Dalla Costa, Selma James and Silvia Federici made the analysis of domestic labor the basis of a demand for wages for housework. Federici's Caliban and the Witch argues that capital's need to control working-class reproduction and secure unpaid reproductive work helped fuel the European witch hunts.\n\nMarx noted in Capital that the maintenance and reproduction of the working class is a necessary condition of capital, and that the capitalist leaves it to the workers' own drives for self-preservation and propagation. Vogel proposed a unitary theory, placing women's oppression within the reproduction of labor power, against the dual-systems theory, associated with Heidi Hartmann, that treats patriarchy and capitalism as two systems; on Hartmann's account, a bargain struck in the nineteenth century between capitalists and working-class men produced the family wage. Tithi Bhattacharya's collection extends the framework to schools, hospitals, migration and race, and Nancy Fraser describes a crisis of care, as capital depletes the social capacities it depends on.",
    s: "Vogel, Marxism and the Oppression of Women (1983); Federici, Caliban and the Witch (2004); Bhattacharya, ed., Social Reproduction Theory (2017); Fraser, “Contradictions of Capital and Care”, New Left Review 100 (2016)",
    p: ["vogel", "federici", "bhattacharya"], r: ["primitive-accumulation", "surplus-value", "intersectionality", "alienation"], br: ["feminist", "marxism"], k: ["social reproduction", "reproductive labor", "reproductive labour", "wages for housework"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-class/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Feminist Perspectives on Class and Work” (Ann Ferguson, Rosemary Hennessy and Mechthild Nagel)" },
  { id: "cyborg", t: "Cyborg", a: "crit",
    d: "Haraway's figure, in “A Cyborg Manifesto”, for a hybrid of organism and machine that breaks down the boundaries between human and animal, organism and machine, and physical and non-physical.",
    x: "Haraway offers the cyborg as an ironic political myth for socialist feminism, one that refuses origin stories of a lost unity and identities based on natural kinship. She analyzes the “informatics of domination” and the place of women in the integrated circuit of high-tech capitalism.\n\nThe word was coined in 1960 by Manfred Clynes and Nathan Kline, who proposed “cybernetic organisms” adapted to survive in space. Haraway's essay first appeared in Socialist Review in 1985. Its closing line, “I would rather be a cyborg than a goddess”, sets it against the goddess imagery of some feminism of the time. Women of color, in her account, already live the cyborg's split and joined identities, and she draws on Chela Sandoval's idea of oppositional consciousness. Haraway later turned from the cyborg to dogs and other “companion species”, in The Companion Species Manifesto (2003).",
    s: "Haraway, “A Manifesto for Cyborgs” (1985), revised in Simians, Cyborgs, and Women (1991)",
    p: ["haraway"], r: ["situated-knowledges", "essentialism", "biopower", "social-construction"], br: ["feminist", "science"], k: ["cyborg", "cyborgs", "posthuman", "posthumanism"] },
  { id: "orientalism", img: { file: "Jean-Léon_Gérôme_-_Le_charmeur_de_serpents.jpg", alt: "Gérôme's painting of a naked boy charming a snake before seated men in a tiled hall", cap: "Jean-Léon Gérôme, The Snake Charmer, about 1879 (Clark Art Institute); a detail of it was on the cover of the first edition of Said's Orientalism. Public domain, via" }, t: "Orientalism", a: "crit",
    d: "Said: the Western discourse that produced “the Orient” as an object of knowledge, exotic, static and inferior, and so helped justify colonial rule.",
    x: "Said drew on Foucault's account of discourse and Gramsci's hegemony to show how scholarship, literature and colonial administration worked together from the late eighteenth century onward. The book founded postcolonial studies and provoked debates about whether Western knowledge of other cultures is compromised as a whole. Later work extends the analysis to representations in media and policy.",
    s: "Said, Orientalism (1978); Culture and Imperialism (1993)",
    p: ["said", "foucault", "gramsci"], r: ["discourse", "power-knowledge", "subaltern", "hegemony", "ethnophilosophy"], br: ["postcolonial"], k: ["orientalism", "orientalist", "the orient"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/colonialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Colonialism”" },
  { id: "subaltern", t: "Subaltern", a: "crit",
    d: "Groups excluded from the structures of political and cultural power. Gramsci wrote of “subaltern classes”; Spivak asks whether the subaltern can be heard within the discourses that claim to represent them.",
    x: "The Subaltern Studies group, founded by Ranajit Guha in the early 1980s, rewrote South Asian history from the perspective of peasants and other groups ignored by colonial and nationalist elites. Spivak questioned whether such voices can be recovered without being spoken for: experience is itself constituted through representation, and representation “has not withered away”. Her case is widow sacrifice, which British colonizers and Indian men debated over the heads of the women concerned.",
    s: "Gramsci, Prison Notebooks, Notebook 25; Guha, Elementary Aspects of Peasant Insurgency in Colonial India (1983); Spivak, “Can the Subaltern Speak?” (1988)",
    p: ["gramsci", "guha", "spivak"], r: ["hegemony", "orientalism", "epistemic-injustice", "essentialism"], br: ["postcolonial", "marxism"], k: ["subaltern", "subalterns", "subalternity"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/colonialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Colonialism” (Margaret Kohn and Kavita Reddy)" },
  { id: "necropolitics", t: "Necropolitics", a: "crit",
    d: "Mbembe: sovereignty exercised as the power to decide who may live and who must die, and to subject populations to conditions of social death.",
    x: "Mbembe starts from Foucault's biopower and argues that in the colony, the plantation and occupied territory, sovereignty appears above all as the right to kill. He draws on Bataille's linking of sovereignty with death and expenditure, and on Agamben's state of exception.\n\nMbembe speaks of “death-worlds”, forms of social existence in which large populations are subjected to conditions that give them the status of the living dead. His examples run from the slave on the plantation, kept alive in a state of injury, through apartheid, to the late-modern colonial occupation of Palestine, where sovereignty works through checkpoints, walls and the control of movement. He developed the essay into a book, published in French in 2016 and in English as Necropolitics (2019). Jasbir Puar took up the concept in Terrorist Assemblages (2007) and The Right to Maim (2017).",
    s: "Mbembe, “Necropolitics”, Public Culture 15, no. 1 (2003)",
    p: ["mbembe", "foucault", "bataille", "agamben"], r: ["biopower", "sovereignty", "state-of-exception", "expenditure"], br: ["postcolonial", "political"], k: ["necropolitics", "necropower", "necropolitical"] },
  { id: "negritude", t: "Négritude", a: "crit",
    d: "A literary and philosophical movement of Black francophone writers, begun in Paris in the 1930s by Aimé Césaire, Léopold Sédar Senghor, Léon-Gontran Damas and others, that affirmed Black African identity and culture against French colonial assimilation.",
    x: "Césaire coined the word when the student journal L'Étudiant noir was founded in 1934–35, and used it in his Notebook of a Return to the Native Land (1939). Senghor gave it a philosophical form: “European reason is analytical by utilization, Negro reason is intuitive by participation”. Critics charged that this accepted the colonial ethnology of a “primitive mentality”. Sartre's “Black Orpheus” (1948) called négritude an “anti-racist racism” and the minor term of a dialectical progression, and Fanon answered Sartre in Black Skin, White Masks (1952).",
    s: "Césaire, Notebook of a Return to the Native Land (1939); Sartre, “Black Orpheus” (1948); Fanon, Black Skin, White Masks (1952), ch. 5",
    p: ["cesaire", "senghor", "sartre", "fanon"], r: ["double-consciousness", "ethnophilosophy", "recognition", "essentialism"], br: ["african", "postcolonial", "race"], k: ["négritude", "negritude"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/negritude/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Négritude” (Souleymane Bachir Diagne)" },
  // Critical theory: on your shelf (Bataille, Land, Jäger)
  { id: "expenditure", t: "Expenditure (dépense)", a: "crit",
    d: "Bataille: unproductive loss, spending without return (luxury, mourning, war, cults, monuments, games, spectacles, the arts, non-reproductive sexuality), which he argued is as basic to social life as production and acquisition.",
    x: "In “The Notion of Expenditure” (1933) Bataille drew on Mauss's study of potlatch, in which rivals gain rank by giving away or destroying wealth. He criticized the principle of utility that judges everything by production and conservation. The idea grows into the general economy of The Accursed Share, and Mbembe later draws on Bataille's linking of sovereignty with death and expenditure.",
    s: "Bataille, “The Notion of Expenditure” (1933), in Visions of Excess (1985); Mauss, The Gift (1925)",
    p: ["mauss", "bataille"], r: ["general-economy", "transgression", "sovereignty", "necropolitics"], br: [], k: ["expenditure", "dépense", "potlatch"], src: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/bataille-georges-1897-1962", srcName: "Encyclopedia of Philosophy, 2nd ed. (Macmillan, 2005), “Bataille, Georges” (Allan Stoekl)", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/opth-2024-0016/html", srcName: "“The Remedy for a World Without Transcendence”: Georges Bataille on Sacrifice and the Theology of Transgression, Open Theology 10 (2024) (Luka Trebežnik)" }] },
  { id: "general-economy", t: "General economy", a: "crit",
    d: "Bataille's economics of the whole: it starts from the excess energy that living systems receive from the sun, a surplus greater than maintenance and growth can absorb, which must be spent “willingly or not, gloriously or catastrophically”.",
    x: "Restricted economy, the economics of a household, a firm or a nation, is the logic of scarcity; general economy is the logic of surplus and unreserved spending. Bataille argues that the decisive question for a society is how it spends its excess, its “accursed share”, and his examples include Aztec sacrifice, early Islamic conquest, Tibetan monasticism, capitalist reinvestment and modern war. Volume one, Consumption, appeared in 1949; the volumes on eroticism and sovereignty were published posthumously in 1976.",
    s: "Bataille, The Accursed Share, vol. 1: Consumption (1949)",
    p: ["bataille"], r: ["expenditure", "transgression", "sovereignty"], br: [], k: ["general economy", "restricted economy", "accursed share"], src: "https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/opth-2024-0016/html", srcName: "“The Remedy for a World Without Transcendence”: Georges Bataille on Sacrifice and the Theology of Transgression, Open Theology 10 (2024) (Luka Trebežnik)", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/bataille-georges-1897-1962", srcName: "Encyclopedia of Philosophy, 2nd ed. (Macmillan, 2005), “Bataille, Georges” (Allan Stoekl)" }] },
  { id: "accelerationism", t: "Accelerationism", a: "crit",
    d: "The view that the processes driving capitalism, technology and modernization should be intensified, either to push through and past capitalism (left accelerationism) or to free them from human and political control (Nick Land's version).",
    x: "Benjamin Noys coined the term, in a critical register, in The Persistence of the Negative (2010), for a tendency, including Anti-Oedipus, that answers Marx by turning capitalism against itself, on the principle of “the worse the better”. Land's essays of the 1990s, written in and around the Cybernetic Culture Research Unit at Warwick, pitch runaway positive feedback against the stabilizing effect of negative feedback. Nick Srnicek and Alex Williams's “#Accelerate: Manifesto for an Accelerationist Politics” (2013) proposed repurposing capitalist technology for post-capitalist ends.",
    s: "Noys, The Persistence of the Negative (2010); Land, Fanged Noumena: Collected Writings 1987–2007 (2011); Srnicek and Williams, “#Accelerate” (2013); Land, “A Quick-and-Dirty Introduction to Accelerationism” (2017)",
    p: ["land", "noys", "srnicek", "alexwilliams"], r: ["hyperstition", "noumenon"], br: ["political"], k: ["accelerationism", "accelerationist"], srcs: [{ src: "https://www.e-flux.com/journal/46/60063/escape-velocities", srcName: "“Escape Velocities”, e-flux Journal 46 (2013) (Alex Williams)" }], src: "https://eprints-gro.gold.ac.uk/19773/1/Hyperstition_Myth-Science_Cyclops.docx", srcName: "“Accelerationism, Hyperstition and Myth-Science”, Goldsmiths Research Online (Simon O'Sullivan, Goldsmiths, University of London)" },
  { id: "hyperstition", t: "Hyperstition", a: "crit",
    d: "Nick Land's term, from the Cybernetic Culture Research Unit (CCRU) of the 1990s, for an idea or fiction that brings about its own reality: in his words, “a positive feedback circuit including culture as a component”.",
    x: "The CCRU's own list described hyperstition as an “element of effective culture that makes itself real”, a “fictional quantity functional as a time-traveling device”, a “coincidence intensifier” and a “call to the Old Ones”. Land called it “the experimental (techno-)science of self-fulfilling prophecies”: superstitions are merely false beliefs, but hyperstitions, by their very existence as ideas, function causally to bring about their own reality.\n\nThe CCRU was founded at the University of Warwick in 1995 by Sadie Plant, with Land, who led it after she left academia. Its texts mix theory and fiction: Land's “Circuitries” runs a science-fiction narrative beside its theory, and “Meat” is both philosophy and cyberpunk fiction. Positive feedback, which Simon O'Sullivan calls another name for hyperstition, runs through these essays, and the group's numerological system, the Numogram, often appears beside it. Fanged Noumena (2011) and #Accelerate: The Accelerationist Reader (2014) carried the idea into accelerationist debate.",
    s: "Land, interviewed by Delphi Carstens, “Hyperstition: An Introduction” (2009); CCRU, Writings 1997–2003 (2015)",
    p: ["land"], r: ["accelerationism", "performativity", "ideology"], br: [], k: ["hyperstition", "hyperstitional", "ccru"], src: "https://eprints-gro.gold.ac.uk/19773/1/Hyperstition_Myth-Science_Cyclops.docx", srcName: "“Accelerationism, Hyperstition and Myth-Science”, Goldsmiths Research Online (Simon O'Sullivan, Goldsmiths, University of London)", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.orphandriftarchive.com/articles/hyperstition-an-introduction/", srcName: "“Hyperstition: An Introduction”, Nick Land interviewed by Delphi Carstens (2009) (Land's own words)" }] },
  { id: "hyperpolitics", t: "Hyperpolitics", a: "crit",
    d: "Anton Jäger's term for the present phase of politics: intense, constant politicization, much of it online, with few lasting consequences, because the mass organizations that once turned passion into power have hollowed out.",
    x: "Jäger places it in a sequence: mass politics, with high politicization and strong institutions (parties, unions, churches); post-politics in the 1990s and early 2000s, with low politicization and weak institutions; anti-politics after 2008, protest against the institutions that remained; and hyperpolitics, high politicization with low institutionalization. He describes it as an attempt to break with neoliberalism without the tools to do so.",
    s: "Jäger, Hyperpolitik (2023); English edition, Hyperpolitics: Extreme Politicization without Political Consequences",
    p: ["jager"], r: ["end-of-history", "hegemony", "class-consciousness"], br: ["political"], k: ["hyperpolitics", "hyperpolitik", "post-politics", "anti-politics"], src: "https://publicseminar.org/2026/06/review-hyperpolitics/", srcName: "Public Seminar, review of Hyperpolitics (2026)" },
  { id: "end-of-history", t: "End of history", a: "crit",
    d: "The thesis that history, understood as the development of political and social forms through conflict, reaches a final stage. Kojève read Hegel this way, and Francis Fukuyama argued in 1989 that liberal democracy might be that final form.",
    x: "Hegel saw world history as “the progress of the consciousness of freedom”. Kojève's Paris lectures on the Phenomenology (1933–39), attended by Bataille, Lacan, Merleau-Ponty and others, held that history had already ended with the Revolution and Napoleon, which made possible a secular, universal and homogeneous state in which every human being is equally recognized. Fukuyama's essay “The End of History?” (1989) and his book (1992) draw substantially on Kojève; Boris Groys argues that they miss his point, since for Kojève that state remained a project still to be realized.",
    s: "Hegel, Lectures on the Philosophy of History; Kojève, Introduction to the Reading of Hegel (1947); Fukuyama, “The End of History?” (1989)",
    p: ["hegel", "kojeve", "fukuyama"], r: ["recognition", "hyperpolitics", "dialectic", "historical-materialism"], br: ["history", "political"], k: ["end of history"], src: "https://archive2.bakonline.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/Alexandre-Kojeve-by-Boris-Groys.pdf", srcName: "“The Photographer as the Sage”, in After History: Alexandre Kojève as a Photographer (BAK, 2012) (Boris Groys)" },
  // Deleuze and Guattari: from their books, with chapter and page, and scholarship on them
  { id: "desiring-production", t: "Desiring-production", a: "crit",
    d: "Deleuze and Guattari's name, in Anti-Oedipus (1972), for desire as a process of production: an unconscious made of machines that couple flows and cut them, at work directly in natural and social production.",
    x: "Anti-Oedipus opens with machines everywhere: “The breast is a machine that produces milk, and the mouth a machine coupled to it.” The Stanford Encyclopedia article by Daniel Smith, John Protevi and Daniela Voss calls desiring-production the book's key term, a “universal primary process” beneath the natural, social and psychological realms, and stresses that no subject stands behind it and that this desire is purely positive, with no lack to make up.\n\nDesiring-machines are binary, a flow and a break in it, and they link in series under the formula “and then”. They “work only when they break down, and by continually breaking down”, which sets them apart from technical machines, which need to be in order to work.\n\nThe fourth section, “A Materialist Psychiatry”, sets this against the logic of desire as lack, which the authors trace to Plato's choice between production and acquisition. What is missing in desire, on their account, is a fixed subject; lack is produced and organized by social production itself, and needs derive from desire.\n\nSocial production, on this account, is desiring-production under determinate conditions, and libido invests production with no need of mediation. Hence what the authors call the fundamental problem of political philosophy, a question Spinoza asked and Wilhelm Reich took up again: why people fight to stay in servitude.\n\nDesiring-production works through three syntheses, connective, disjunctive and conjunctive, whose energies Protevi's notes give as libido, numen and voluptas. The Stanford article likens desiring-production to Spinoza's natura naturans and Nietzsche's will to power.\n\nThe book begins with a schizophrenic's walk, Lenz's stroll as Büchner reconstructed it, and with nature lived as a process of production, in which man and nature “are not like two opposite terms confronting each other”. Clinical schizophrenia, on the Stanford article's reading, results when the process of desiring-production is interrupted or blocked. Book-length guides include Eugene Holland's Deleuze and Guattari's Anti-Oedipus: Introduction to Schizoanalysis (1999) and Ian Buchanan's Deleuze and Guattari's Anti-Oedipus: A Reader's Guide (2008).",
    s: "Anti-Oedipus (1972), trans. Robert Hurley, Mark Seem and Helen R. Lane (1977), ch. 1, sec. 1, “Desiring-Production” (pp. 1–8) and sec. 4, “A Materialist Psychiatry” (pp. 22–35)",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari", "nietzsche", "spinoza"], r: ["body-without-organs", "syntheses-unconscious", "schizoanalysis", "coding-decoding"], br: ["poststructuralism", "critical-theory"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.protevi.com/john/DG/AO1.html", srcName: "John Protevi, notes on Anti-Oedipus, part one" }, { src: "https://www.versobooks.com/blogs/news/3844-why-do-people-fight-for-their-servitude-as-if-it-were-their-salvation", srcName: "Asad Haider, “Why do people fight for their servitude as if it were their salvation?”, Verso blog (2018)" }] },
  { id: "body-without-organs", t: "Body without organs", a: "crit",
    d: "A phrase Deleuze and Guattari take from Antonin Artaud: in Anti-Oedipus the unproductive surface on which desiring-production is recorded; in A Thousand Plateaus a field of intensities beneath the organism's organization.",
    x: "The phrase comes from Antonin Artaud's To Have Done with the Judgment of God, a radio work made for French radio in late 1947 whose broadcast was cancelled before it aired. In The Logic of Sense (1969), the Stanford Encyclopedia article notes, Deleuze drew on Artaud to name a life of intensities in motion the body without organs. Anti-Oedipus gives it a section of its first chapter.\n\nThere it is the third moment of the connective synthesis: production stops, and “an enormous undifferentiated object” forms, “the unproductive, the sterile, the unengendered, the unconsumable”. Its name, the authors write, is the death instinct, and its model is the catatonic body; it experiences the desiring-machines as “an over-all persecution apparatus”, and it is constantly put back into production as antiproduction.\n\nBy repulsion it pushes the machines away, which the authors call the paranoiac machine; by attraction it draws them to itself, so that production seems to come from it, which they call the miraculating machine. It becomes the surface on which production is recorded, the site of the disjunctive synthesis, and a celibate machine joins attraction and repulsion, producing a subject as a residue beside the machines.\n\nSociety has a full body too, the socius, on which production is recorded and from which it seems to come: the earth, the despot, capital. “Capital is indeed the body without organs of the capitalist, or rather of the capitalist being.”\n\nThe body without organs is zero intensity, and the states a subject passes through on it are bands of intensity, thresholds and gradients; the authors call it an egg.\n\nA Thousand Plateaus titles its sixth plateau “November 28, 1947: How Do You Make Yourself a Body without Organs?”. There the body without organs is the enemy of the organism, a stratum that organizes the organs, and the Stanford article glosses it as “the reservoir of potentials for different patterns of bodily affect”. The authors treat it as a practice and an experiment, and warn that wild destratification leaves emptied bodies or totalitarian and fascist ones.",
    s: "Anti-Oedipus (1972), trans. Robert Hurley, Mark Seem and Helen R. Lane (1977), ch. 1, sec. 2, “The Body without Organs” (pp. 9–16); A Thousand Plateaus (1980), trans. Brian Massumi (1987), plateau 6, “November 28, 1947: How Do You Make Yourself a Body without Organs?”",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari"], r: ["desiring-production", "syntheses-unconscious", "deterritorialization"], br: ["poststructuralism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.protevi.com/john/DG/AO1.html", srcName: "John Protevi, notes on Anti-Oedipus, part one" }, { src: "https://www.blackbird.vcu.edu/v10n1/gallery/porter_j/radio_page.shtml", srcName: "“Lost and Found Radio”, Blackbird 10.1 (2011)" }] },
  { id: "syntheses-unconscious", t: "Syntheses of the unconscious", a: "crit",
    d: "The three processes that make up desiring-production in Anti-Oedipus, connective, disjunctive and conjunctive, each with an immanent, legitimate use and a transcendent, illegitimate use that produces Oedipus.",
    x: "Deleuze and Guattari borrow the terms of Kant's critique: each synthesis of desire has an immanent use and a transcendent one. The Stanford Encyclopedia article describes psychoanalysis as the transcendent use, which posits a subject behind the syntheses, and schizoanalysis as the study of their immanent use; subjects are among the products of the syntheses.\n\nThe connective synthesis is the production of production, with the formula “and then”, and its energy is libido. Used legitimately it is partial and nonspecific; used illegitimately it is global and specific, referring partial objects to complete objects and to the parents, which the authors call the paralogism of extrapolation.\n\nThe disjunctive synthesis is the production of recording on the body without organs, with the formula “either … or … or”, and its energy is numen. Used inclusively it keeps its terms open; used exclusively and restrictively it forces a choice between fixed terms, the double bind, a term the authors take from Gregory Bateson.\n\nThe conjunctive synthesis is the production of consumption, with the formula “so it's …”, and its energy is voluptas; in it a subject is born as a residue beside the machines. Its legitimate use is nomadic and polyvocal and its illegitimate use segregative and biunivocal, setting up one-to-one relations between the figures of the social field and the parents, which the authors call the paralogism of application.\n\nTwo further paralogisms follow: displacement, in the section on social and psychic repression, by which repression offers desire a falsified image of what it represses, and a fifth, in the section on neurosis and psychosis, which treats actual factors as after-effects of family problems.\n\nIn A Thousand Plateaus, the Stanford article explains, the results of these paralogisms become strata: the organism totalizes the connective synthesis of production, signifiance flattens the disjunctive synthesis of recording, and the subject reifies the conjunctive synthesis of consummation.",
    s: "Anti-Oedipus (1972), trans. Robert Hurley, Mark Seem and Helen R. Lane (1977), ch. 2, secs. 3–8 (pp. 68–130)",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari", "kant"], r: ["desiring-production", "body-without-organs", "schizoanalysis", "transcendental"], br: ["poststructuralism"], src: "https://www.protevi.com/john/DG/PDF/Anti-Oedipus_Part_2.pdf", srcName: "John Protevi, notes on Anti-Oedipus, part two", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)" }, { src: "https://www.protevi.com/john/DG/AO1.html", srcName: "John Protevi, notes on Anti-Oedipus, part one" }] },
  { id: "schizoanalysis", t: "Schizoanalysis", a: "crit",
    d: "The critical practice proposed in Anti-Oedipus: a materialist psychiatry that traces how desire invests the social field directly, set against the Oedipal reductions of psychoanalysis.",
    x: "Anti-Oedipus calls its practice schizoanalysis, and the Stanford Encyclopedia article describes it as a “materialist psychoanalysis” that studies the immanent use of the syntheses of desire. Its target is what the article calls the “familial or ‘Oedipal’ patterning of desire” in psychoanalysis, which posits an original investment in family figures.\n\nThe schizophrenic process, on the article's reading, is desiring-production itself, and the clinical schizophrenic is what results when that process is interrupted or blocked.\n\nThe critique of psychoanalysis runs through the second chapter, “Psychoanalysis and Familialism: The Holy Family”. The great discovery of psychoanalysis, the authors write, was the production of desire, and Protevi's notes trace how Freud then moved from the factory to the classical theater as the model of the unconscious. Social repression delegates psychic repression to the family, and Wilhelm Reich, whom the authors call the true father of materialist psychiatry, is credited with showing how psychic repression depends on social repression, then faulted for two consequences of his approach.\n\nThe fourth chapter, “Introduction to Schizoanalysis”, closes with a set of theses, which Garin Dowd summarizes: every investment is “molar and social”, the libidinal investment of the social field comes before familial investments, and a schizophrenic, revolutionary pole challenges the paranoid one.\n\nMichel Foucault's preface to the English translation calls the book “an Introduction to the Non-Fascist Life” and names three adversaries: the political ascetics, among them the bureaucrats of the revolution, the technicians of desire, and fascism, including the fascism in all of us. He also warns against reading it as “the new theoretical reference”. Guattari's working notes for the book were later published as The Anti-Oedipus Papers.",
    s: "Anti-Oedipus (1972), trans. Robert Hurley, Mark Seem and Helen R. Lane (1977), Michel Foucault's preface, ch. 2 (pp. 51–137) and ch. 4, “Introduction to Schizoanalysis”",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari", "foucault"], r: ["desiring-production", "syntheses-unconscious", "coding-decoding", "deterritorialization"], br: ["poststructuralism", "critical-theory"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.protevi.com/john/DG/PDF/Anti-Oedipus_Part_2.pdf", srcName: "John Protevi, notes on Anti-Oedipus, part two" }, { src: "https://uwl-9.eprints-hosting.org/id/eprint/1110/2/Dowd_Deleuze_and_Schizoanalysis_EXTRACT.pdf", srcName: "Garin Dowd, “Is critique et clinique schizoanalytic?”, in Deleuze and the Schizoanalysis of Literature (2015)" }, { src: "https://revistareflexoes.com.br/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/5.1-Helton-Adverse-modelo-aaa.pdf", srcName: "Helton Adverse, “Foucault, o fascismo e a ‘vida não fascista’”, Revista Reflexões 19 (2021)" }] },
  { id: "coding-decoding", t: "Coding, overcoding, decoding", a: "crit",
    d: "In Anti-Oedipus, the ways social machines register flows of desire: territorial societies code them, despotic states overcode those codes, and capitalism decodes them and binds them with an axiomatic.",
    x: "The third chapter of Anti-Oedipus, “Savages, Barbarians, Civilized Men”, is a history of social machines. The Stanford Encyclopedia article distinguishes three: the territorial machine of tribal societies, the despotic machine of empires and the capitalist machine, each with a full body, the socius, on which production is recorded and which seems to be its cause: the earth, the body of the despot, capital.\n\nThe territorial machine traces material flows onto the earth and codes them by marking bodies in initiation ceremonies, in what the article calls a theater of cruelty. Its signs, the article notes, do not map onto a voice and are not yet signifiers.\n\nEmpires overcode these codes and trace all production back to the despot, and the signifier appears through a “flattening” or “bi-univocalization” of the codes.\n\nCapitalism, in the article's words, is “the radical decoding and deterritorialization of the material flows”: it brings together freed flows of labor and capital and puts an axiomatic in the place of codes. Its decoded flows are reterritorialized on persons, that is, on members of the family.\n\nA Thousand Plateaus continues this history: the Stanford article describes the state as an “apparatus of capture” “living vampirically off of labor”, a sign of the authors' basically Marxist perspective, and the war machine as the form that “reterritorializes on deterritorialization itself”.",
    s: "Anti-Oedipus (1972), trans. Robert Hurley, Mark Seem and Helen R. Lane (1977), ch. 3, “Savages, Barbarians, Civilized Men”",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari", "marx"], r: ["desiring-production", "deterritorialization", "primitive-accumulation", "surplus-value", "accelerationism"], br: ["marxism", "poststructuralism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.protevi.com/john/DG/AO1.html", srcName: "John Protevi, notes on Anti-Oedipus, part one" }] },
  { id: "deterritorialization", t: "Deterritorialization and reterritorialization", a: "crit",
    d: "Deleuze and Guattari's terms for the movement by which something leaves a territory, a code or a habit, and the movement by which it forms a new territory elsewhere.",
    x: "In Anti-Oedipus flows are territorialized when they are traced onto the earth, and the Stanford Encyclopedia article describes capitalism as their radical decoding and deterritorialization. For A Thousand Plateaus the article glosses deterritorialization as the breaking of habits and reterritorialization as their formation.\n\nEmpires trace production back to the despot, and capitalism reterritorializes its decoded flows on persons, that is, on members of the family.\n\nLines of flight, in Martin Müller and Carolin Schurr's account, take an assemblage apart. The eleventh plateau treats the refrain, or rhythm, as a way of escaping from territories and forming new ones, and the Stanford article notes that continual deterritorialization is what the authors call consistency.\n\nLanguage can be deterritorialized too: in Kafka: Toward a Minor Literature (1975) the German of Prague's Jewish writers has a strong coefficient of deterritorialization. Sara De Balsi sets out the two paths the authors see: a symbolic reterritorialization, which they find in Max Brod, and an absolute deterritorialization, the path of minor literature, Kafka's use of Prague German in its very poverty.\n\nThe Stanford article classes deterritorialization among philosophy's concepts, which the authors keep apart in kind from the functions of science, and notes that they repeatedly insist such concepts are no metaphors.\n\nIn Anti-Oedipus the authors ask which path is revolutionary and suggest going further in the movement of the market, to “accelerate the process”; Alex Williams notes that Nick Land later answered this question in the affirmative, while Deleuze and Guattari counseled caution.",
    s: "Anti-Oedipus (1972), ch. 3; Kafka: Toward a Minor Literature (1975), ch. 3; A Thousand Plateaus (1980), trans. Brian Massumi (1987), plateau 11, “1837: Of the Refrain”",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari"], r: ["coding-decoding", "assemblage", "minor-literature", "rhizome", "accelerationism"], br: ["poststructuralism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.e-flux.com/journal/46/60063/escape-velocities", srcName: "“Escape Velocities”, e-flux Journal 46 (2013) (Alex Williams)" }, { src: "https://cielam.univ-amu.fr/print/pdf/node/3814", srcName: "Sara De Balsi, “Écriture translingue et littérature mineure”, Revue Malice" }, { src: "https://research.birmingham.ac.uk/files/26874611/Mu_ller_Schurr_2016_Assemblage_thinking_and_actor_network_theory_Transactions_of_the_Institute_of_British_Geographers.pdf", srcName: "Martin Müller and Carolin Schurr, “Assemblage thinking and actor-network theory”, Transactions of the Institute of British Geographers (2016)" }] },
  { id: "rhizome", t: "Rhizome", a: "crit",
    d: "Deleuze and Guattari's model of thought and organization: a network in which any point can connect to any other, with no root, center or fixed order, set out in the introduction to A Thousand Plateaus.",
    x: "In botany a rhizome is an underground stem, and the authors' examples include bulbs, tubers and couchgrass. Kafka: Toward a Minor Literature (1975) already calls Kafka's work a rhizome, a burrow with many entrances, none of them privileged.\n\nAgainst the rhizome stands the tree, the arborescent model of thought: the tree, in the authors' words, is filiation, and the rhizome “uniquely alliance”, its fabric the conjunction “and . . . and . . . and . . .”.\n\nThe introduction lists “certain approximate characteristics of the rhizome”. By the principles of connection and heterogeneity, “any point of a rhizome can be connected to anything other, and must be”; by the principle of multiplicity, a multiplicity has no relation to the One as subject or object; and by the principle of asignifying rupture, a rhizome shattered at one spot starts up again on its old lines or on new ones. The orchid, they write, “forms a map with the wasp, in a rhizome”.\n\nBy the principles of cartography and decalcomania a rhizome is a map, and the authors treat genetic axes and deep structures as principles of tracing, which they find in linguistics as in psychoanalysis.\n\nThe book follows suit: its Author's Note lets the plateaus be taken in any order, keeping only the conclusion for last, and the Stanford Encyclopedia article describes them as planes of intensity, a rhizome allowing immediate connections between any of its points.\n\nGuides include Brent Adkins's Deleuze and Guattari's A Thousand Plateaus: A Critical Introduction and Guide (2015) and The Deleuze and Guattari Dictionary (2013) by Eugene Young, Gary Genosko and Janell Watson.",
    s: "A Thousand Plateaus (1980), trans. Brian Massumi (1987), Author's Note and “Introduction: Rhizome”; Kafka: Toward a Minor Literature (1975)",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari"], r: ["assemblage", "deterritorialization", "body-without-organs"], br: ["poststructuralism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iberian-connections.yale.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/minorliterature.pdf", srcName: "Deleuze and Guattari, “What Is a Minor Literature?”, trans. Robert Brinkley, Mississippi Review 11.3 (1983)" }] },
  { id: "assemblage", t: "Assemblage (agencement)", a: "crit",
    d: "Deleuze and Guattari's term for an arrangement of heterogeneous elements that holds together for a time and produces effects; developed into a social ontology by Manuel DeLanda.",
    x: "“Assemblage” is the usual English rendering of the French agencement, a translation the sociologist John Law calls awkward. Martin Müller and Carolin Schurr describe assemblages as relations among heterogeneous entities working together for a time, and note that Deleuze and Guattari's vocabulary of contagions and epidemics hints at their fluidity and ephemerality.\n\nThe Stanford Encyclopedia article describes the assemblage in A Thousand Plateaus as “an emergent unity joining together heterogeneous bodies in a ‘consistency’”: coding orders matter as it enters a body, stratification makes hierarchies of bodies, and territorialization orders bodies into assemblages. Lines of flight, in Müller and Schurr's account, take an assemblage apart.\n\nManuel DeLanda developed the concept into a social ontology in A New Philosophy of Society (2006) and Assemblage Theory (2016), after reconstructing Deleuze's ontology in Intensive Science and Virtual Philosophy (2002). He takes “relations of exteriority as the defining characteristic of assemblages”: their components are never fully determined by their relations, which lets them plug into other assemblages. Müller and Schurr find his account “strongly wedded to discrete notions of scales”.\n\nActor-network theory, associated with Bruno Latour, John Law and Michel Callon, is the neighboring program, and Jane Bennett's Vibrant Matter (2010), in Müller and Schurr's words, “segues between Latour, Deleuze and Guattari”. Jasbir Puar, who argued in Terrorist Assemblages (2007) that intersectionality must be supplemented by a notion of assemblage, wrote in 2011 that the relation between the two is “frictional”, and that what interests her is what assemblages do.",
    s: "A Thousand Plateaus (1980), trans. Brian Massumi (1987); Manuel DeLanda, A New Philosophy of Society (2006) and Assemblage Theory (2016)",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari"], r: ["rhizome", "deterritorialization", "intersectionality", "body-without-organs"], br: ["poststructuralism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://research.birmingham.ac.uk/files/26874611/Mu_ller_Schurr_2016_Assemblage_thinking_and_actor_network_theory_Transactions_of_the_Institute_of_British_Geographers.pdf", srcName: "Martin Müller and Carolin Schurr, “Assemblage thinking and actor-network theory”, Transactions of the Institute of British Geographers (2016)" }, { src: "https://www.transversal.at/pdf/journal-text/541/", srcName: "Jasbir Puar, “‘I would rather be a cyborg than a goddess’: Intersectionality, Assemblage, and Affective Politics”, transversal (2011)" }] },
  { id: "plane-of-immanence", t: "Plane of immanence", a: "crit",
    d: "In What Is Philosophy? (1991), the field on which a philosophy creates its concepts; each philosopher, Deleuze and Guattari hold, must lay out a plane of their own.",
    x: "What Is Philosophy? (1991), the final collaboration between Deleuze and Guattari, calls philosophy “the art of forming, inventing, and fabricating concepts”; science creates functions on a plane of reference, and art creates compounds of percepts and affects, blocs of sensation. “Thought, in all its modes,” the Stanford article adds, “struggles with chaos against opinion.”\n\nA concept, on the Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy's account, is an assemblage of components that must stay coherent with one another, a multiplicity, distinct from a proposition, and it “has no relationship to truth, which is an external determination”. Concepts exist on the plane of immanence, and each philosopher must create such a plane. The question of what thought presupposes, the IEP notes, is the one Difference and Repetition had treated under the image of thought.\n\nConceptual personae are “the figures of thought that give concepts their specific force”, and the IEP names the Idiot as Descartes's implicit persona. “A conceptual persona is not a subject,” the Stanford article explains, “for thinking is not subjective.”\n\nThe plane of immanence is opposed to transcendence, and the IEP explains that insisting that immanence be immanent to something is what brings transcendence back and arrests thought. For Deleuze, the Stanford article notes, Spinoza was the “prince” or even the “Christ” of philosophers, and Deleuze's essay “Immanence: A Life” returns to the theme.\n\nIn its account of What Is Philosophy?, the Stanford article says philosophy gives consistency to the virtual, mapping the forces of a system as pure potentials, while science tracks how the virtual is actualized and explains why one road was taken.",
    s: "What Is Philosophy? (1991), trans. Hugh Tomlinson and Graham Burchell (1994), chs. 1–3; “Immanence: A Life” (1995)",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari", "spinoza"], r: ["immanence", "transcendence", "difference-in-itself", "virtual-actual"], br: ["poststructuralism", "metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/deleuze/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze”" }] },
  { id: "minor-literature", t: "Minor literature", a: "crit",
    d: "In Kafka: Toward a Minor Literature (1975), the literature a minority makes within a major language, marked by the deterritorialization of language, an immediate political charge and a collective value.",
    x: "Deleuze and Guattari define a minor literature as “the literature a minority makes in a major language”, and their case is Franz Kafka, a Jew in Prague writing in German.\n\nThey give it three characteristics: its language has a strong coefficient of deterritorialization; everything in it is political, since in its narrow space every individual matter is plugged straight into politics; and everything in it has a collective value. Together these, they write, describe the revolutionary conditions of any literature within the great or established one.\n\nKafka's own word, Sara De Balsi notes, is small (petites littératures in French), and the adjective mineure comes from Marthe Robert's 1954 translation of his diaries.\n\nThe authors see two responses to a deterritorialized language: a symbolic reterritorialization, which they find in the Prague school around Max Brod, whose politics ends in the dream of Zion, and an absolute deterritorialization, the path of minor literature, which takes Prague German as it is, in its very poverty. Joyce, in De Balsi's account of their reading, works by exuberance and overdetermination.\n\nA minor use of language makes a writer a stranger in their own language. De Balsi holds that only some writers in a second language make a minor literature, and treats it as one possible path for translingual writing.",
    s: "Kafka: Toward a Minor Literature (1975), trans. Dana Polan (1986), ch. 3, “What Is a Minor Literature?”",
    p: ["deleuze", "guattari"], r: ["deterritorialization", "assemblage", "rhizome"], br: ["aesthetics", "language", "poststructuralism"], src: "https://cielam.univ-amu.fr/print/pdf/node/3814", srcName: "Sara De Balsi, “Écriture translingue et littérature mineure”, Revue Malice", srcs: [{ src: "https://iberian-connections.yale.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/minorliterature.pdf", srcName: "Deleuze and Guattari, “What Is a Minor Literature?”, trans. Robert Brinkley, Mississippi Review 11.3 (1983)" }] },
  { id: "difference-in-itself", t: "Difference in itself", a: "meta",
    d: "Deleuze's project in Difference and Repetition (1968): to think difference before and apart from identity, freeing it from its subordination to representation.",
    x: "Difference and Repetition (1968) was Deleuze's primary thesis for the doctorat d'État. The Stanford Encyclopedia article describes his reversal of the usual order: identity is produced by a prior relation between differentials, and difference is the sufficient reason of empirical diversity, as in the phenomenon of lightning.\n\nRepresentation, the article explains, subordinates difference in four ways: identity in the concept, opposition of predicates, analogy in judgment and resemblance in perception. The article approaches it through Aristotle and Porphyry, for whom animal is a genus divided by the specific differences rational and irrational.\n\nDeleuze rejects Hegel's treatment of contradiction, which, the Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy notes, is the greatest difference only in relation to the identical, and against Plato's models and copies he sets the simulacrum, calling for an overturning of Platonism.\n\nThe third chapter sets out eight postulates of the dogmatic image of thought, among them the good nature of thought, common sense and recognition. Thought begins, on Deleuze's account, with a chance encounter with something that forces it to think, which the Stanford article compares to the Kantian sublime, a discordant accord of the faculties that leaves a fractured self.\n\nRepetition, Deleuze argues, belongs to another order from generality, which is the order of laws, and he calls it difference without a concept. Repetition for itself, following his reading of the eternal return, is the return of what differs.\n\nIntensive magnitudes such as temperature or pressure cannot be divided without changing in nature, and the Stanford article's example of a process they drive is convection. Guides include Joe Hughes's Deleuze's Difference and Repetition: A Reader's Guide (2009), James Williams's critical introduction and guide (2003) and Henry Somers-Hall's Edinburgh guide (2013).",
    s: "Difference and Repetition (1968), trans. Paul Patton (1994), Introduction, ch. 1, “Difference in Itself”, and ch. 3, “The Image of Thought”",
    p: ["deleuze", "aristotle", "plato", "hegel", "nietzsche"], r: ["virtual-actual", "syntheses-of-time", "univocity", "plane-of-immanence", "differance"], br: ["metaphysics", "poststructuralism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/deleuze/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze”" }, { src: "https://protevi.com/DG/DR.pdf", srcName: "John Protevi, outline of Difference and Repetition (2006)" }] },
  { id: "syntheses-of-time", t: "Syntheses of time", a: "meta",
    d: "The three passive syntheses of time in chapter 2 of Difference and Repetition: habit, which founds the living present; memory, which grounds it in the pure past; and the eternal return, the empty form of time and the future.",
    x: "The second chapter of Difference and Repetition, “Repetition for Itself”, describes three passive syntheses of time. The Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy ties the first to habit and a continually living present, the second to memory and the pure past, and the third to the eternal return, which it calls the time of the future.\n\nThe first synthesis starts from Hume's observation that repetition changes something only in the mind that contemplates it, and contracts successive instants into a living present. These contractions are the passive syntheses of contemplative souls, and the selves they form are larval subjects. Habit is the foundation of time, and memory its ground.\n\nThe second synthesis is memory, a pure past that was never present, which Deleuze develops from Bergson's paradoxes of memory and pairs with Eros and Mnemosyne. Proust's novels are, for the IEP, “the most profound development of memory as the pure past”.\n\nThe third synthesis is the pure and empty form of time, in which time is “out of joint”: a caesura divides it into a before and an after, and the I is fractured, split between active and passive. Its content is the eternal return, in which only what differs returns.\n\nThe Stanford Encyclopedia article describes the syntheses of habit as bodily, desiring and unconscious contractions, and subjects as “the patterns of these multiple and serial syntheses which fold in on themselves producing a site of self-awareness”.\n\nIn the same chapter Deleuze turns to Freud: excitations must be bound for pleasure to be possible, Eros draws objects from the pure past, and libido flowing back onto the ego becomes a desexualized energy that forms the death instinct, the empty form of time. James Williams's Gilles Deleuze's Philosophy of Time (2011) is a full study of these syntheses.",
    s: "Difference and Repetition (1968), trans. Paul Patton (1994), ch. 2, “Repetition for Itself”",
    p: ["deleuze", "hume", "nietzsche"], r: ["difference-in-itself", "eternal-recurrence", "virtual-actual", "syntheses-unconscious"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/deleuze/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze”", srcs: [{ src: "https://protevi.com/DG/DR.pdf", srcName: "John Protevi, outline of Difference and Repetition (2006)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)" }] },
  { id: "virtual-actual", t: "Virtual and actual", a: "meta",
    d: "Deleuze's distinction, drawn from Bergson, between the virtual, real conditions of genesis structured as differential multiplicities, and the actual things and states of affairs that emerge from them.",
    x: "Deleuze develops the ideas of virtuality and multiplicity in Bergsonism (1966), the Stanford Encyclopedia article notes, and for him the virtual is fully real: what happens in genesis is that it is actualized. Since the ground cannot resemble what it grounds, the virtual is differential through and through.\n\nMultiplicity, in the article's account, designates the multiple as a substantive, and a typology of multiplicities replaces the dialectic of the one and the many. An Idea, Deleuze's name for a virtual structure, is “an n-dimensional, continuous, defined multiplicity”, determined step by step through differential elements, differential relations and singularities; the differential elements of a language are its phonemes.\n\nDeleuze distinguishes differentiation, the varieties of relations and singular points in an Idea, from differenciation, actualization as the constitution of solutions, and joins them in the notion of different/ciation. The Stanford article pairs differentiation with inclusive disjunction and differenciation with exclusive disjunction.\n\nBetween virtual and actual lies the intensive: the Stanford article describes impersonal individuations beneath actual things and pre-individual singularities marking the thresholds of systems, and takes Gilbert Simondon's theory of individuation, in which a supersaturated solution is metastable before it crystallizes, as a simple model.\n\nIn A Thousand Plateaus, the article says, the virtual is called the Earth, the intensive consistency and the actual the system of the strata, and “Nothing ever instantiates the sheer frozen stasis of the actual nor the sheer differential dispersion of the virtual”, which makes Deleuze and Guattari process philosophers.\n\nThe Stanford article names Brian Massumi and Manuel DeLanda, author of Intensive Science and Virtual Philosophy (2002), among those who bring Deleuze's epistemology and ontology together with dynamical systems theory, popularly called chaos and complexity theory.",
    s: "Difference and Repetition (1968), trans. Paul Patton (1994), ch. 4, “Ideas and the Synthesis of Difference”, and ch. 5, “Asymmetrical Synthesis of the Sensible”; Bergsonism (1966)",
    p: ["deleuze"], r: ["difference-in-itself", "syntheses-of-time", "plane-of-immanence", "potentiality"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze” (Smith, Protevi and Voss)", srcs: [{ src: "https://protevi.com/DG/DR.pdf", srcName: "John Protevi, outline of Difference and Repetition (2006)" }] },
  { id: "univocity", t: "Univocity of being", a: "meta",
    d: "The thesis that being is said in one and the same sense of everything of which it is said, which Deleuze takes from Duns Scotus, Spinoza and Nietzsche as the basis of a philosophy of difference.",
    x: "Medieval philosophers debated how terms apply to God and to creatures: Thomas Aquinas held that we can predicate only analogically, and John Duns Scotus that certain predicates apply univocally, with exactly the same meaning, to both. Since all our concepts come from creatures, Scotus argued, without univocity we could not talk about God at all. Deleuze declares that ontology has only ever had a single proposition, the univocity of being.\n\nFor Deleuze being is “said in a single and same sense of everything of which it is said”, and it is said of difference itself, so that difference can be thought within being, and the Stanford Encyclopedia article calls univocity the key to a philosophy of difference.\n\nHe tells its history in three moments, as John Protevi's outline sets them out: Scotus neutralized univocal being into an abstract concept, Spinoza made it an object of pure affirmation, and Nietzsche realized it in the repetition of the eternal return.\n\nDeleuze's secondary thesis was Spinoza and the Problem of Expression (1968), and the Stanford article describes the result of his reading as “a Spinozism minus substance, a purely modal or differential universe”, in which difference is difference in degrees of power. The article defines puissance as immanent power, the power to act, and links pouvoir to transcendent power.\n\nCeleste Ramírez reads univocity against the vertical, transcendent hierarchy of genera and species.\n\nAlain Badiou's Deleuze: The Clamor of Being (1997) argued, as the Stanford article reports, that Deleuze is at bottom a philosopher of the One, and criticized his vitalism.",
    s: "Difference and Repetition (1968), trans. Paul Patton (1994), ch. 1, “Difference in Itself”; Expressionism in Philosophy: Spinoza (1968), trans. Martin Joughin (1990)",
    p: ["deleuze", "spinoza", "nietzsche", "aristotle"], r: ["difference-in-itself", "immanence", "substance", "mode", "plane-of-immanence"], br: ["metaphysics"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze”" },
  // Added from sources checked in October 2026
  { id: "language-game", t: "Language-game", a: "logic",
    d: "Wittgenstein: a whole made of language and the activities into which it is woven. Speaking a language is part of a form of life, and the meaning of a word is, in most cases, its use in the language.",
    x: "The Philosophical Investigations opens with a simple builders' language of four words, “block”, “pillar”, “slab”, “beam”, used to order stones, and goes on to list the many things people do with words: giving orders, describing, reporting, telling jokes, thanking, praying. The term was aimed at the Tractatus's own picture of language as a single system for stating facts. Lyotard later borrowed it for his account of incommensurable discourses in The Postmodern Condition.",
    s: "Wittgenstein, Philosophical Investigations (1953), §§1–43",
    p: ["wittgenstein", "lyotard"], r: ["family-resemblance", "private-language", "speech-act"], br: ["language", "analytic"], k: ["language-game", "language game", "form of life", "meaning as use"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ludwig Wittgenstein”" },
  { id: "family-resemblance", t: "Family resemblance", a: "logic",
    d: "Wittgenstein: the things that fall under one concept may share no single common feature, only a network of overlapping and criss-crossing similarities, like the resemblances among members of a family.",
    x: "Wittgenstein's example is “game”: board games, card games, ball games and ring-a-ring-a-roses share various features, competition, skill, luck, amusement, but no feature is common to all. “Don't think, but look!” The idea challenges the classical demand that a concept be defined by necessary and sufficient conditions, and has been used in debates about the definition of art, religion and gender.",
    s: "Wittgenstein, Philosophical Investigations (1953), §§65–71",
    p: ["wittgenstein"], r: ["language-game", "conditions", "essentialism"], br: ["language", "analytic"], k: ["family resemblance", "family resemblances"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ludwig Wittgenstein”" },
  { id: "private-language", t: "Private language argument", a: "logic",
    d: "Wittgenstein's argument that there could not be a language whose words refer to what only the speaker can know, such as his immediate private sensations, because such a language would have no criterion of correctness.",
    x: "In Philosophical Investigations (1953), §243, Wittgenstein asks whether there could be a language whose words refer to what only the speaker can know, his immediate private sensations, so that no one else could understand it. A personal code or a diary in an invented script would not qualify, since others could in principle learn it; the target is a language that could be understood only by its originator. The main discussion runs through §§244–271, with related remarks to §315. Wittgenstein never used the phrase “private language argument”, and some commentators doubt that the passages form a single argument.\n\nThe preliminary sections distinguish two senses in which sensations might be private, owned by one person alone or knowable by one person alone, and argue that sensations as ordinary language describes them are private in neither sense. The private language Wittgenstein examines is one that a philosopher's picture of the mind seems to require, such as the inner life of a Cartesian soul. His conclusion is that such a language is impossible, because its supposed speaker could never give its signs a meaning; Gordon Baker argued that the idea is nonsense posing as a possibility.\n\nIn §258, suppose I write “S” in a diary whenever I have a certain sensation. Wittgenstein argued that the diarist would have no way to tell remembering the sensation correctly from merely seeming to: “whatever is going to seem correct to me is correct”, which means that here we cannot speak of “correct”. The idea of getting it right would have no application, and with it the sign would say nothing.\n\nEarly commentators, among them Anthony Kenny and Robert Fogelin, read this as skepticism about memory. The Stanford Encyclopedia article by Stewart Candlish and George Wrisley rejects that reading: the problem is that the private definition establishes no meaning for memory to preserve. Checking one private impression against another, Wittgenstein remarks, is like buying several copies of the morning paper to assure oneself that what it says is true.\n\nIf “S” is linked to something public, such as a rise in blood pressure measured on a manometer (§§270–271), the sign acquires a use, but then its meaning is public and the private object plays no role. The beetle in the box (§293) makes the same point: if everyone had a box containing something called a beetle that no one else could see, the thing in the box would drop out of the language-game entirely. Meaning requires public practices in which mistakes can be identified.\n\nThe argument targets the Cartesian and empiricist picture of the mind as a private inner theater. The article finds that picture in Locke's account of communication as translating private ideas into public words and back, later embraced by Jerry Fodor, in Descartes' confidence that he can identify his experiences by inner inspection, in the problem of other minds as Mill posed it, and in Russell's view that a name applies only to a particular with which the speaker is acquainted.\n\nInterpreters have long disputed whether the argument shows that language is essentially social. Norman Malcolm's influential review of 1954 supported a community view, while Baker and P. M. S. Hacker opposed it, and critics asked whether the argument rules out a Robinson Crusoe who devises a language in isolation. Saul Kripke's Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language (1982) linked the argument to a skeptical paradox about rule-following drawn from §201, with a skeptical solution grounded in agreement within a community. The article judges that Kripke's reading depends on assumptions Wittgenstein rejected, though it revived the whole debate.",
    s: "Wittgenstein, Philosophical Investigations (1953), §§243–315; Kripke, Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language (1982)",
    p: ["wittgenstein", "kripke"], r: ["language-game", "dualism", "skepticism"], br: ["language", "mind"], k: ["private language", "beetle in a box", "rule-following"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/private-language/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Private Language”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ludwig Wittgenstein”" }] },
  { id: "sense-reference", t: "Sense and reference", a: "logic",
    d: "Frege's distinction between what an expression stands for, its reference (Bedeutung), and the way that thing is presented to us, its sense (Sinn).",
    x: "“The morning star” and “the evening star” refer to the same planet, Venus, but present it differently, which is why “the morning star is the evening star” can be an astronomical discovery while “the morning star is the morning star” is trivial. Sense determines reference. In reports of belief, Frege argued, expressions refer to their ordinary sense, so substituting co-referring terms can change a sentence's truth. The distinction became a foundation of analytic philosophy of language.",
    s: "Frege, “On Sense and Reference” (1892)",
    p: ["frege", "russell", "kripke"], r: ["theory-of-descriptions", "rigid-designation", "signifier"], br: ["language", "analytic"], k: ["sense and reference", "sense", "reference", "Sinn", "Bedeutung"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/frege/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gottlob Frege”" },
  { id: "theory-of-descriptions", t: "Theory of descriptions", a: "logic",
    d: "Russell's analysis of phrases of the form “the so-and-so” as disguised claims about existence and uniqueness, so that they need not name anything to be meaningful.",
    x: "The theory of descriptions is Bertrand Russell's analysis of phrases of the form “the F” (definite descriptions) and “an F” (indefinite descriptions), set out in “On Denoting”, published in Mind in 1905. On Russell's account such phrases do not name anything. “An F is G” says that there is something that is both F and G. “The F is G” combines three claims: that there is an F, that there is at most one F, and that whatever is F is G.\n\n“The present King of France is bald” says that there is exactly one present King of France and that whoever is King of France is bald. Since there is no such king, the sentence is false, without any need for a nonexistent king for it to be about. Its negation is ambiguous: “The present King of France is not bald” can deny that there is exactly one king who is bald, which is true, or say of the one king that he lacks hair, which is false. Russell explained such ambiguities as differences of scope.\n\nThe analysis solved several puzzles at once. Negative existentials such as “The present King of France does not exist” come out true without any strange object to deny. Substitution in reports of thought is explained: George IV wished to know whether Scott was the author of Waverley, but he did not wish to know whether Scott was Scott, and the theory shows why replacing “the author of Waverley” with “Scott” changes the claim. The law of excluded middle is preserved, since one of “the King of France is bald” and its properly scoped negation is true.\n\n“On Denoting” became a model of philosophical analysis, showing that a sentence's grammatical form can mislead about its logical form. It also served Russell's epistemology: in “Knowledge by Acquaintance and Knowledge by Description” (1910–11) he argued that much of what we know about things we know only under descriptions, since we are directly acquainted with very little.\n\nPeter Strawson objected in “On Referring” (1950) that such sentences are neither true nor false but presuppose that the thing exists; on his view, when there is no king, the question of truth or falsity simply does not arise. Russell replied in 1957 that the sentence is simply false, and Stephen Neale's Descriptions (1990) defended the Russellian view at length. More recent work by Kai von Fintel, Stephen Yablo and Anders Schoubye suggests that our judgments vary with context.\n\nKeith Donnellan (1966) distinguished attributive uses of descriptions, about whoever uniquely fits them, from referential uses, which pick out a particular person: “Smith's murderer is insane” can mean whoever killed Smith, or the man in the dock. Kripke replied in 1977 that the Russellian analysis can handle both, by distinguishing what the speaker refers to from what the words themselves refer to. Incomplete descriptions such as “the table is covered with books” raise a further problem, since there is obviously more than one table in the world; Jason Stanley and Zoltán Szabó (2000) appeal to restrictions on the domain of quantification.\n\nLinguists and philosophers have since proposed alternatives in which “the” signals familiar or given information, among them Hans Kamp (1981) and Irene Heim (1982), who treat indefinites as variables, and accounts in which uniqueness is presupposed. Russell's analysis remains the reference point against which these rivals are measured.",
    s: "Russell, “On Denoting” (1905); Strawson, “On Referring” (1950)",
    p: ["russell", "frege", "moore"], r: ["sense-reference", "rigid-designation", "validity"], br: ["language", "analytic", "logic"], k: ["theory of descriptions", "definite descriptions", "On Denoting", "King of France"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descriptions/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Descriptions”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Bertrand Russell”" }] },
  { id: "rigid-designation", t: "Rigid designator", a: "logic",
    d: "Kripke: an expression that refers to the same thing in every possible world in which that thing exists. Proper names are rigid; most descriptions, such as “the teacher of Alexander”, are not.",
    x: "A rigid designator is an expression that designates the same object in every possible world in which that object exists, and designates nothing else. Saul Kripke introduced the term in “Identity and Necessity” (1971) and in Naming and Necessity, lectures given at Princeton in 1970 and published as a book in 1980. Joseph LaPorte's Stanford Encyclopedia article notes two ways of making the idea precise, depending on whether a rigid designator refers to its object even in worlds where the object does not exist, and suggests that little may hang on the choice.\n\nAristotle might not have taught Alexander, but in any possible situation we describe, “Aristotle” still names Aristotle. A description such as “the teacher of Alexander” could have picked out someone else. Kripke used this difference against the descriptivist theory of names, on which a name abbreviates a description: if “Aristotle” meant “the teacher of Alexander”, then “Aristotle might not have taught Alexander” would mean that the teacher of Alexander might not have taught Alexander. Descriptivists such as Michael Dummett replied that the descriptions take wide scope, and the debate over these modal arguments continues.\n\nKripke proposed that a name's reference is fixed by an initial baptism and passed along a causal chain of uses from speaker to speaker. Fixing reference differs from giving a meaning. His example is the standard meter: if “one meter” is introduced as the length of a particular stick S at a time t0, then “one meter is the length of S at t0” is known a priori, yet it is only contingently true, because “one meter” is rigid and “the length of S” is not; the stick could have been longer.\n\nRigid designation also explains necessary a posteriori truths. Since “Hesperus” and “Phosphorus” are both rigid, and both in fact name Venus, the identity “Hesperus is Phosphorus” is true in every possible world, though astronomers had to discover it. By contrast, “Hesperus is the brightest body in the evening sky apart from the Moon” is true, but only contingently, since the description could have applied to something else.\n\nKripke distinguished designators that are rigid de jure, because of how they are introduced and used, from those that are rigid de facto, because of how the world is. “The successor of 2” is rigid de facto, since it picks out 3 in every world because of a mathematical necessity. Proper names are rigid de jure: we use them to talk about their bearers in every possible situation, whatever descriptions those bearers satisfy. A description tied to the actual world, such as “the person who actually taught Alexander”, is also rigid.\n\nNathan Salmon (1981) called a designator that refers to its object in every world, whether or not the object exists there, obstinately rigid. Some philosophers, including Alvin Plantinga, argue that claims such as “Socrates might not have existed” make sense only if the name refers even in worlds without Socrates. Kripke left the question unsettled, remarking in 1980 that in a counterfactual situation in which Hitler never existed, “Hitler” still refers to the man who in that situation would not exist.\n\nKripke and Hilary Putnam extended rigidity to natural kind terms such as “water” and “tiger”, arguing that “water is H₂O” is necessary though discovered by science; many philosophers dispute the extension, and it is unclear how a general term can be rigid without positing abstract kinds. Counterpart theorists such as David Lewis and Allan Gibbard reject some of the necessities rigidity seems to deliver.",
    s: "Kripke, Naming and Necessity (lectures 1970; book 1980)",
    p: ["kripke", "frege", "russell"], r: ["sense-reference", "necessity", "a-posteriori"], br: ["language", "metaphysics", "analytic"], k: ["rigid designator", "rigid designation", "causal theory of reference"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rigid-designators/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Rigid Designators”" },
  { id: "speech-act", t: "Speech act", a: "logic",
    d: "An utterance considered as the performance of an action. Austin distinguished the locutionary act of saying something meaningful, the illocutionary act done in saying it, such as promising or warning, and the perlocutionary act achieved by saying it, such as persuading.",
    x: "Austin began from performatives, such as “I name this ship”, which do something and can misfire, and concluded that all utterances perform acts. Searle systematized the theory, and Grice's account of implicature explained how speakers convey more than they literally say. Derrida argued that every performative depends on the citation of conventions, and Butler extended performativity to the formation of gender.",
    s: "Austin, How to Do Things with Words (1962); Searle, Speech Acts (1969)",
    p: ["austin", "derrida", "butler"], r: ["performativity", "language-game"], br: ["language", "analytic"], k: ["speech act", "speech acts", "illocutionary", "perlocutionary", "locutionary"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/austin-jl/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “John Langshaw Austin”" },
  { id: "myth-of-the-given", t: "Myth of the given", a: "know",
    d: "Sellars's name for the idea that some states, such as raw sense impressions, can both be independent of all other knowledge and serve as its foundation.",
    x: "In “Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind” (1956) Sellars argued that to know even that something looks red is to place it in the logical space of reasons, justifying and being able to justify what one says, which requires a network of concepts and other knowledge. No item can be both given, outside that space, and a reason. Empirical knowledge is rational because it is a self-correcting enterprise that can put any claim in jeopardy, though not all at once. The argument is a central attack on foundationalism.",
    s: "Sellars, “Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind” (1956)",
    p: ["sellars", "quine"], r: ["foundationalism", "coherentism", "empiricism"], br: ["epistemology", "analytic"], k: ["myth of the given", "the given", "space of reasons"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sellars/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Wilfrid Sellars”" },
  { id: "indeterminacy-of-translation", t: "Indeterminacy of translation", a: "logic",
    d: "Quine's thesis that the totality of evidence about speakers' behavior is compatible with incompatible translation manuals, so that there is no fact of the matter about which translation is correct.",
    x: "In Word and Object (1960) Quine imagined a linguist translating a wholly unknown language. A native says “gavagai” when a rabbit runs past; the linguist may translate “rabbit”, but “undetached rabbit part” or “temporal stage of a rabbit” would fit all the same evidence, provided adjustments are made elsewhere. Quine concluded that meaning is not a determinate thing in the head or in the world, a conclusion tied to his rejection of the analytic-synthetic distinction.",
    s: "Quine, Word and Object (1960), ch. 2",
    p: ["quine"], r: ["analytic-synthetic", "coherentism", "language-game"], br: ["language", "analytic"], k: ["indeterminacy of translation", "radical translation", "gavagai"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/quine/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Willard Van Orman Quine”" },
  { id: "naturalistic-fallacy", t: "Naturalistic fallacy", a: "ethics",
    d: "Moore's name for the attempt to define goodness in terms of a natural property, such as pleasure or what we desire. Good, he held, is simple and indefinable.",
    x: "The naturalistic fallacy is G. E. Moore's name, in Principia Ethica (1903), for the error of identifying goodness with some other property. His main targets were natural properties of the kind science studies, such as pleasure or being more evolved, and he found the error in hedonists such as Bentham and evolutionary ethicists such as Herbert Spencer. He also rejected identifying goodness with metaphysical properties of the kind invoked in idealist ethics, such as T. H. Green's. Thomas Hurka's Stanford Encyclopedia article notes that Henry Sidgwick had used a version of the argument in passing.\n\nAt the center of Moore's view is the claim that goodness is simple and cannot be analyzed, even in moral terms. Principia Ethica defined what we ought to do in terms of what is good; after criticism from Russell, Moore's Ethics (1912) treated “ought” as a separate moral concept, though he continued to regard good as simple. He insisted that his claim concerned the concept of goodness itself, the object of thought; questions about the word were secondary for him.\n\nMoore's open question argument holds that for any proposed natural definition, “X is good” remains an open, meaningful question: we can sensibly ask whether pleasure is good, which we could not if “good” meant “pleasant”, since then “pleasure is good” would say only that pleasure is pleasure. The same test applies to any proposal, such as “what we desire to desire”, and to other moral terms: one can always ask of an act that maximizes pleasure whether it is right.\n\nMoore concluded that goodness is a non-natural property and that basic truths about what is intrinsically good are known by intuition. Such propositions are self-evident: they cannot be proved from other truths, and the Preface to Principia Ethica treats any attempt to prove them as a sign of confusion. Intuition is fallible all the same, and Moore allowed that we can take a false proposition to be self-evident as easily as a true one.\n\nCritics questioned whether the argument works. William Frankena's “The Naturalistic Fallacy” (Mind, 1939) charged that it begs the question by assuming that “pleasure is good” is no analytic truth, which is exactly what the naturalist denies. C. H. Langford (1942) raised the paradox of analysis: a correct analysis would seem uninformative. Gilbert Harman (1977), David Brink (1989) and Nicholas Sturgeon (2003) argued that Moore confused concepts with properties, since informative identities such as “water is H₂O” can also seem open. Derek Parfit (2011) and others reply that goodness lacks the hidden structure that makes such scientific identities possible.\n\nMany philosophers accepted the autonomy of ethics that Moore defended while rejecting his realism. A. J. Ayer (1936) and C. L. Stevenson (1944) drew non-cognitivist conclusions, holding that moral judgments express attitudes, and R. M. Hare (1952) developed prescriptivism; non-cognitivists following Hume added that no natural definition could capture the action-guiding force of moral judgment. Late in life Moore conceded that Stevenson's view had as good a claim as his own to be true, then returned to his non-naturalism without being able to say why he had wavered.\n\nMoore's own ethics combined this account of goodness with ideal utilitarianism: the right act is the one that produces the most good, and the greatest goods are aesthetic appreciation and personal affection. His principle of organic unities holds that the value of a whole need not equal the sum of the values of its parts. His non-naturalism is enjoying a revival in the work of Thomas Nagel, T. M. Scanlon, Derek Parfit, David Enoch, Russ Shafer-Landau and Michael Huemer.",
    s: "Moore, Principia Ethica (1903), ch. 1",
    p: ["moore", "hume"], r: ["normative", "consequentialism"], br: ["ethics", "analytic"], k: ["naturalistic fallacy", "open question argument"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore-moral/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Moore's Moral Philosophy”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “George Edward Moore”" }] },
  { id: "harm-principle", t: "Harm principle", a: "ethics",
    d: "Mill's principle that the only purpose for which power can rightly be exercised over any member of a civilized community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others. A person's own good is not a sufficient warrant.",
    x: "On Liberty (1859) applies the principle to thought, speech and ways of life. Silencing an opinion is wrong whether it is true, false or partly true, since even false opinions keep truths from becoming dead dogma, and individuality, the development of one's own character, is part of well-being. Debate continues over what counts as harm, whether offense does, and whether paternalism is ever justified, as in Hart's argument against Devlin over enforcing morality by law.",
    s: "Mill, On Liberty (1859), ch. 1; Hart, Law, Liberty, and Morality (1963)",
    p: ["mill", "hart"], r: ["autonomy", "consequentialism", "natural-law"], br: ["political", "ethics", "law"], k: ["harm principle", "liberty principle", "On Liberty"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mill/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “John Stuart Mill”" },
  { id: "difference-principle", t: "Difference principle", a: "ethics",
    d: "Rawls's principle that social and economic inequalities are just only if they work to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged members of society.",
    x: "In A Theory of Justice (1971) the difference principle forms part of the second principle of justice, together with fair equality of opportunity, and is ranked below the first principle of equal basic liberties. Rawls argued that parties behind the veil of ignorance would choose it, since no one knows whether they will be among the worst off. Nozick objected that it imposes a pattern on distribution that free exchanges continually upset; G. A. Cohen objected that it allows too much inequality.",
    s: "Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), §§13, 26; Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974)",
    p: ["rawls", "nozick"], r: ["veil-of-ignorance", "social-contract"], br: ["political", "ethics"], k: ["difference principle", "maximin", "least advantaged"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rawls/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “John Rawls”" },
  { id: "banality-of-evil", t: "Banality of evil", a: "ethics",
    d: "Arendt's phrase, from her report on the trial of Adolf Eichmann, for evil done on a vast scale by people who are neither demonic nor deeply ideological, but thoughtless.",
    x: "Eichmann in Jerusalem (1963) found the organizer of the deportations to the death camps to be an ambitious functionary who spoke in clichés and seemed unable to think from the standpoint of anyone else. Arendt did not mean that his deeds were banal. The book, and its remarks on the Jewish councils, provoked bitter controversy. In her later work she connected thinking, the silent dialogue with oneself, to the capacity to resist evil.",
    s: "Arendt, Eichmann in Jerusalem (1963); The Life of the Mind (1978)",
    p: ["arendt"], r: ["bad-faith", "instrumental-reason"], br: ["ethics", "political"], k: ["banality of evil", "Eichmann"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/arendt/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hannah Arendt”" },
  { id: "friend-enemy", t: "Friend–enemy distinction", a: "ethics",
    d: "Schmitt's claim that the specifically political distinction, to which political actions and motives can be reduced, is that between friend and enemy: a public enemy whose existence threatens a people's way of life.",
    x: "The Concept of the Political (1927, expanded 1932) argued that the political is the most intense of all oppositions, and that the enemy need not be morally evil, only existentially other. Liberalism, Schmitt held, tries to dissolve this distinction into competition and discussion and so cannot ground a political community. Thinkers on the left, notably Chantal Mouffe, have adapted the idea, while critics stress its link to Schmitt's support for National Socialism.",
    s: "Schmitt, The Concept of the Political (1932)",
    p: ["schmitt", "agamben"], r: ["sovereignty", "state-of-exception", "hegemony"], br: ["political"], k: ["friend-enemy distinction", "friend and enemy", "the political"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schmitt/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Carl Schmitt”" },
  { id: "metanarrative", t: "Metanarrative", a: "crit",
    d: "Lyotard: a grand story that legitimates knowledge and politics by appeal to a universal goal, such as the emancipation of humanity or the progress of reason. He defined the postmodern as incredulity toward metanarratives.",
    x: "The Postmodern Condition (1979), commissioned by the government of Quebec as a report on knowledge in developed societies, argued that the Enlightenment narrative of emancipation and the Hegelian narrative of spirit had lost their credibility. Knowledge is increasingly legitimated by performativity, its efficiency within a system. Lyotard favored a plurality of local language games and a science of instabilities. Habermas criticized the book as abandoning the unfinished project of modernity.",
    s: "Lyotard, The Postmodern Condition (1979), introduction",
    p: ["lyotard", "habermas", "hegel"], r: ["language-game", "differend", "post-structuralism"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["metanarrative", "grand narrative", "postmodern"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lyotard/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jean-François Lyotard”" },
  { id: "differend", t: "Differend", a: "crit",
    d: "Lyotard: a conflict between two parties that cannot be equitably resolved because no rule of judgment applies to both, so that judging in one party's idiom wrongs the other.",
    x: "In The Differend (1983) Lyotard analyzed language as a series of phrases governed by different regimens and genres, which have no common measure. A party who cannot express its injury in the language of the tribunal suffers a wrong that the tribunal cannot even recognize. His central example is the demand, by those who deny the Nazi gas chambers, for eyewitnesses who saw them work, when those witnesses were killed. Philosophy's task is to bear witness to differends.",
    s: "Lyotard, The Differend: Phrases in Dispute (1983)",
    p: ["lyotard"], r: ["metanarrative", "epistemic-injustice", "language-game"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["differend", "le différend"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lyotard/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jean-François Lyotard”" },
  { id: "bare-life", t: "Bare life", a: "crit",
    d: "Agamben: life exposed to death by sovereign power, the life of the homo sacer, who may be killed but not sacrificed.",
    x: "Homo Sacer (1995) builds on the Greek distinction between zoē, the simple fact of living, and bios, a qualified form of life. Western politics, Agamben argued, is founded on what it excludes from politics, in an “inclusive exclusion” of natural life, embodied in the homo sacer of Roman law, whose killer was not condemned for homicide, though he could not be sacrificed. The camp is “the space that is opened when the state of exception begins to become the rule”; Agamben's later examples include refugees and the detainees at Guantánamo.",
    s: "Agamben, Homo Sacer: Sovereign Power and Bare Life (1995)",
    p: ["agamben", "schmitt", "foucault", "benjamin"], r: ["state-of-exception", "biopower", "necropolitics", "sovereignty"], br: ["political", "poststructuralism"], k: ["bare life", "homo sacer", "zoē", "zoe"], src: "https://iep.utm.edu/agamben/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Giorgio Agamben” (Catherine Mills, University of New South Wales)" },
  { id: "abjection", t: "Abjection", a: "crit",
    d: "Kristeva: the reaction of horror and disgust before what threatens the boundaries of the self, such as bodily fluids, waste and the corpse, which the subject must expel in order to constitute itself.",
    x: "Powers of Horror (1980) traces abjection to the infant's separation from the maternal body, before a stable distinction between self and object exists. The abject is neither subject nor object; what causes abjection is what “disturbs identity, system, order”. Kristeva analyzed how religions organize it through rules of purity and defilement, and how literature, in Céline above all, confronts it.",
    s: "Kristeva, Powers of Horror: An Essay on Abjection (1980)",
    p: ["kristeva", "lacan", "bataille"], r: ["transgression", "subjectivation"], br: ["poststructuralism", "feminist"], k: ["abjection", "the abject", "abject"] },
  { id: "intertextuality", t: "Intertextuality", a: "crit",
    d: "Kristeva's term, developed from Bakhtin: “any text is constructed as a mosaic of quotations; any text is the absorption and transformation of another”.",
    x: "Kristeva introduced the term in essays of the 1960s collected in Séméiôtiké (1969), as part of the Tel Quel group's critique of the self-contained work and its author. Barthes took up the idea in “The Death of the Author” and S/Z: a text is a tissue of citations, and its unity lies in its reader.",
    s: "Kristeva, “Word, Dialogue and Novel” (1966), in Séméiôtiké (1969); Barthes, “The Death of the Author” (1967)",
    p: ["kristeva", "barthes"], r: ["death-of-the-author", "structuralism", "differance"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["intertextuality", "intertext"] },
  { id: "death-of-the-author", t: "Death of the author", a: "aes",
    d: "Barthes's thesis that a text's meaning is not fixed by its author's intentions or biography: the author gives way to a scriptor who recombines existing writing, and meaning is produced in reading.",
    x: "Barthes's essay (1967) ends: the birth of the reader must be at the cost of the death of the Author. It attacked criticism that explained works by their authors' lives and psychology. Foucault's lecture “What Is an Author?” (1969) asked instead what functions the author's name performs in a culture's discourses. S/Z distinguished readerly texts, offering fixed meanings, from writerly texts that make the reader a producer of meaning.",
    s: "Barthes, “The Death of the Author” (1967); Foucault, “What Is an Author?” (1969)",
    p: ["barthes", "foucault"], r: ["intertextuality", "hermeneutics", "deconstruction"], br: ["poststructuralism", "aesthetics"], k: ["death of the author", "scriptor", "readerly", "writerly"] },
  { id: "cultural-capital", t: "Cultural capital", a: "crit",
    d: "Bourdieu: knowledge, skills, tastes, credentials and manners that confer advantage, and that can be converted into economic and social advantage, alongside economic, social and symbolic capital.",
    x: "Bourdieu distinguished cultural capital in an embodied state, as dispositions of the habitus, in an objectified state, as books and artworks, and in an institutionalized state, as academic qualifications. In Reproduction (1970), with Passeron, he argued that schools reward the cultural capital children bring from home as though it were individual merit, and Distinction (1979) showed how taste marks and reproduces class. Competition within each field is a struggle over its specific forms of capital.",
    s: "Bourdieu and Passeron, Reproduction (1970); Bourdieu, Distinction (1979); “The Forms of Capital” (1986)",
    p: ["bourdieu"], r: ["habitus", "social-reproduction", "ideology"], br: ["political"], k: ["cultural capital", "social capital", "symbolic capital", "field", "symbolic violence"] },
  { id: "structure-of-feeling", t: "Structure of feeling", a: "crit",
    d: "Raymond Williams's term for the lived quality of experience in a particular period and generation, felt in its art and conventions before it is articulated as ideas or institutions.",
    x: "Williams introduced the phrase in the 1950s and developed it in The Long Revolution (1961) and Marxism and Literature (1977), where he also distinguished the dominant, residual and emergent elements of a culture. A structure of feeling is social, shared and changing, and literature is one of its best records. The concept was part of his cultural materialism, which rejected a mechanical model of base and superstructure.",
    s: "Williams, The Long Revolution (1961); Marxism and Literature (1977)",
    p: ["williams"], r: ["base-superstructure", "hegemony", "ideology"], br: ["marxism"], k: ["structure of feeling", "structures of feeling", "cultural materialism"] },
  { id: "encoding-decoding", t: "Encoding/decoding", a: "crit",
    d: "Stuart Hall's model of communication: media messages are encoded with preferred meanings by their producers and decoded by audiences from their own social positions, in dominant, negotiated or oppositional ways.",
    x: "Hall's paper of 1973 replaced the idea of passive audiences absorbing a message. A dominant reading accepts the preferred meaning, a negotiated reading accepts its general frame while adapting it to local circumstances, and an oppositional reading understands the message and rejects it, reading it within another framework.",
    s: "Hall, “Encoding and Decoding in the Television Discourse” (1973)",
    p: ["hall", "gramsci"], r: ["hegemony", "culture-industry", "ideology"], br: ["marxism", "critical-theory"], k: ["encoding/decoding", "encoding decoding", "preferred reading", "oppositional reading"] },
  { id: "verstehen", t: "Verstehen (understanding)", a: "phen",
    d: "Interpretive understanding of human actions, expressions and institutions through the meanings they have for those involved, which Dilthey made the method of the human sciences.",
    x: "Dilthey contrasted the natural sciences, which explain phenomena causally through laws, with the human sciences, which understand lived experience through its expressions. Weber built Verstehen into sociology, combining interpretive understanding of meaningful action with causal explanation, and Schutz grounded it in phenomenology. The hermeneutic tradition of Heidegger and Gadamer went further, treating understanding as a basic feature of human existence.",
    s: "Dilthey, Introduction to the Human Sciences (1883); Weber, Economy and Society (1922), ch. 1",
    p: ["dilthey", "weber", "schutz", "gadamer"], r: ["hermeneutics", "ideal-type", "lifeworld"], br: ["phenomenology", "history", "science"], k: ["Verstehen", "understanding", "interpretive sociology", "explanation and understanding"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dilthey/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Wilhelm Dilthey”" },
  { id: "ideal-type", t: "Ideal type", a: "know",
    d: "Weber: a deliberately one-sided conceptual model, such as “bureaucracy” or “the Protestant ethic”, built by accentuating certain features of reality, and used to compare and explain historical cases.",
    x: "An ideal type is a tool whose value lies in how far real cases deviate from it; it describes neither an average case nor a moral ideal. Weber's three pure types of legitimate rule, traditional, charismatic and legal-rational, are the best-known examples, and no actual regime matches any of them exactly. The method belongs with his insistence on value freedom in social science.\n\nIn the 1904 essay Weber describes the ideal type as formed by the one-sided accentuation of one or more points of view and the synthesis of many scattered concrete phenomena into a unified analytical construct, which cannot be found anywhere in reality in its conceptual purity. The “economic man” of economic theory is an example, as are Weber's four types of social action: instrumentally rational, value-rational, affectual and traditional. Alfred Schutz, in The Phenomenology of the Social World (1932), argued that everyday understanding of other people also works through typifications.",
    s: "Weber, “‘Objectivity’ in Social Science and Social Policy” (1904); Economy and Society (1922)",
    p: ["weber"], r: ["verstehen", "disenchantment"], br: ["science", "political"], k: ["ideal type", "ideal types", "charismatic authority", "legal-rational authority"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/weber/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Max Weber”" },
  { id: "disenchantment", t: "Disenchantment", a: "crit",
    d: "Weber's term (Entzauberung) for the process by which the modern West came to see the world as governed by calculable forces, with no mysterious powers left to be won over by magic.",
    x: "In “Science as a Vocation” (1919) Weber described disenchantment as the knowledge or belief that one could, in principle, master all things by calculation. It belongs to the wider process of rationalization, bureaucracy and the iron cage. Weber held that the result was a world of competing value spheres, science, politics, art, religion, with no shared ultimate ground for choosing among them. Horkheimer and Adorno's Dialectic of Enlightenment and Charles Taylor's A Secular Age took up the theme.",
    s: "Weber, “Science as a Vocation” (1919); The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism (1904–05)",
    p: ["weber", "horkheimer", "adorno", "taylor"], r: ["instrumental-reason", "ideal-type", "critical-theory"], br: ["critical-theory", "religion"], k: ["disenchantment", "disenchantment of the world", "Entzauberung"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/weber/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Max Weber”" },
  { id: "absurd", t: "The absurd", a: "phen",
    d: "Camus: the confrontation between the human demand for meaning and clarity and a world that remains silent. The absurd lies in their encounter.",
    x: "The Myth of Sisyphus (1942) begins with the question of suicide and rejects both physical suicide and “philosophical suicide”, the leap into hope or transcendence by which Kierkegaard, Shestov and Jaspers evaded the absurd. The right response is revolt, freedom and passion: to live lucidly without appeal. Sisyphus, condemned to push his rock forever, is the absurd hero, and one must imagine him happy. The Rebel (1951) turned the analysis toward politics and the limits of revolt.",
    s: "Camus, The Myth of Sisyphus (1942); The Rebel (1951)",
    img: { file: "Punishment_sisyph.jpg", alt: "Titian's painting of Sisyphus carrying a great rock", cap: "Titian, Sisyphus, 1548–49 (Museo del Prado, Madrid). Public domain, via" },
    p: ["camus", "kierkegaard", "nagel"], r: ["existentialism", "bad-faith"], br: ["phenomenology"], k: ["the absurd", "absurd", "absurdism", "Sisyphus"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/camus/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Albert Camus”" },
  { id: "face", t: "The face (of the other)", a: "phen",
    d: "Levinas: the other person as encountered in their vulnerability and alterity, which addresses me with an ethical demand, “you shall not kill”, before I can comprehend or master them.",
    x: "Totality and Infinity (1961) sets the face against totality, the tendency of Western thought, from ontology to the state, to absorb everything other into the same. The face exceeds every idea I form of it, as the idea of the infinite does, and my responsibility for the other precedes choice and knowledge, so that ethics is first philosophy. Otherwise than Being (1974) radicalized this responsibility as substitution, one-for-the-other.",
    s: "Levinas, Totality and Infinity (1961); Otherwise than Being (1974)",
    p: ["levinas", "derrida"], r: ["transcendence", "recognition"], br: ["phenomenology", "ethics"], k: ["the face", "face of the other", "alterity", "the Other"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/levinas/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Emmanuel Levinas”" },
  { id: "gift", t: "The gift", a: "crit",
    d: "Mauss: in many societies exchange takes the form of gifts that create obligations to give, to receive and to reciprocate, binding people in relations of honor, debt and solidarity.",
    x: "The Gift (1925) drew on ethnography of the potlatch of the Pacific Northwest and other systems of exchange. A gift is never wholly detached from its giver, Mauss argued, so it creates a debt; refusing it rejects a social bond, and failing to return it costs status. He treated gift exchange as a total social fact, at once economic, legal, religious and moral. Lévi-Strauss, Bataille and Bourdieu built on the book, and Derrida argued in Given Time (1991) that a pure gift, given without any return, is impossible.",
    s: "Mauss, The Gift (1925); Bataille, The Accursed Share (1949); Derrida, Given Time (1991)",
    p: ["mauss", "levistrauss", "bataille", "bourdieu", "derrida"], r: ["expenditure", "general-economy", "use-exchange-value"], br: ["political"], k: ["the gift", "gift exchange", "total social fact", "reciprocity"] },
  { id: "bricolage", t: "Bricolage", a: "crit",
    d: "Lévi-Strauss: making with whatever is at hand. The bricoleur, like mythical thought, builds new structures from the remains of earlier ones, in contrast with the engineer, who designs from a plan.",
    x: "The Savage Mind (1962) used the image to argue that mythical thought is as rigorous as scientific thought, working by a logic of the concrete with signs drawn from the sensible world. The bricoleur's set of tools and materials is finite and heterogeneous, collected because they might come in handy. Derrida's “Structure, Sign, and Play” (1966) replied that every discourse is bricolage, including the engineer's, whose existence is itself a myth.",
    s: "Lévi-Strauss, The Savage Mind (1962), ch. 1; Derrida, “Structure, Sign, and Play” (1966)",
    p: ["levistrauss", "derrida"], r: ["structuralism", "deconstruction"], br: ["poststructuralism"], k: ["bricolage", "bricoleur", "logic of the concrete"] },
  { id: "new-riddle", t: "New riddle of induction", a: "know",
    d: "Goodman's puzzle: an object is “grue” if it is examined before a certain time and green, or not so examined and blue. Every emerald examined so far is both green and grue, yet only “all emeralds are green” seems a reasonable prediction.",
    x: "Fact, Fiction, and Forecast (1955) argued that Hume's old problem, justifying induction, is a pseudo-problem, since rules of inference are justified by fitting the inferences we accept. The real problem is saying which predicates are projectible. Goodman's answer was entrenchment: “green” has a long history of successful use in projections, “grue” none. The riddle shows that the form of an inductive argument cannot by itself make it good.",
    s: "Goodman, Fact, Fiction, and Forecast (1955), ch. 3",
    p: ["goodman", "hume"], r: ["induction-problem", "induction", "falsifiability"], br: ["epistemology", "science"], k: ["new riddle of induction", "grue", "projectibility", "entrenchment"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goodman/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Nelson Goodman”" },
  { id: "looping-effect", t: "Looping effect", a: "crit",
    d: "Hacking: classifying people changes how they understand themselves and behave, which in turn changes the classification. Kinds of people are moving targets in a way natural kinds are not.",
    x: "Hacking studied cases such as multiple personality, child abuse, autism and the nineteenth-century epidemic of compulsive travelers described in Mad Travelers (1998). He called his view dynamic nominalism: categories of people and the people they describe come into being together. The idea complicates claims that something is “socially constructed”, which The Social Construction of What? (1999) dissected.",
    s: "Hacking, “Making Up People” (1986); Rewriting the Soul (1995); The Social Construction of What? (1999)",
    p: ["hacking", "foucault"], r: ["social-construction", "subjectivation"], br: ["science"], k: ["looping effect", "looping effects", "making up people", "dynamic nominalism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/social-construction-naturalistic/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Naturalistic Approaches to Social Construction” (Ron Mallon)" },
  { id: "moral-luck", t: "Moral luck", a: "ethics",
    d: "The way factors beyond a person's control affect how we morally judge them, as when two equally reckless drivers are judged differently because one happens to kill a child.",
    x: "Moral luck occurs when someone is rightly praised or blamed for something that depends, in a significant way, on factors beyond their control. The term comes from a pair of essays by Bernard Williams and Thomas Nagel, both titled “Moral Luck”; Nagel's began as a reply to Williams's paper of 1976. They were collected in Nagel's Mortal Questions (1979) and Williams's Moral Luck (1981). Williams said that when he first introduced the expression he expected it to sound like an oxymoron.\n\nThe trouble is that a very plausible principle seems to rule moral luck out. Dana Nelkin's Stanford Encyclopedia article calls it the Control Principle: we are morally assessable only to the extent that what we are assessed for depends on factors under our control. It follows that two people should not be judged differently if they differ only in things neither controlled. Cases seem to bear this out: a woman pushed into stepping on someone's foot is not to blame, and of two equally careful drivers, the one whose path a dog happens to cross seems no worse a person.\n\nThe thought that morality is immune to luck has a long history. Kant wrote that a good will would shine like a jewel by itself even if it achieved nothing, and Nagel quotes the passage. Adam Smith held that praise and blame belong ultimately to the intention or affection of the heart. Nelkin notes that retreating inward in this way faces its own difficulty, since luck shapes intentions and characters too.\n\nNagel distinguished four kinds of luck. Resultant luck concerns how things turn out: two would-be murderers may differ only because one intended victim tripped. Circumstantial luck concerns the situations we face: people who collaborated with the Nazis in 1930s Germany might have led blameless lives had they been transferred to Argentina in 1929. Constitutive luck concerns who we are, our temperament and character as shaped by genes and upbringing. Causal luck concerns how our actions are determined by prior causes, which Nagel saw as the old problem of free will. Under this scrutiny, he concluded, the area of genuine agency seems to shrink to an extensionless point.\n\nWilliams's own examples were different. His fictionalized Gauguin sets aside his obligations to his family to devote himself to painting, and on Williams's account only success can justify the choice: if he fails, we may condemn him for making a mess of his own and others' lives. A driver who runs over a child through no fault of his own will feel what Williams called agent-regret, regret that it was he who did it, and it would be obtuse to tell him to feel nothing because he was not to blame. Williams took such cases to show that our ideas of praise and blame reach past what is voluntary.\n\nSome philosophers deny that there is moral luck. On the epistemic argument associated with Norvin Richards and Nicholas Rescher, we use outcomes as evidence of intentions, so different treatment reflects what we can know about people and leaves their desert unchanged. Michael Zimmerman separates the scope of responsibility from its degree and holds that the two drivers are equally blameworthy, though only one is responsible for a death. Williams later preserved the Control Principle for a narrow “morality system”, set apart from ethics as a whole.\n\nOthers accept some or all forms of moral luck. Robert Adams points out that we already blame people for attitudes they never chose, and Robert Hartman argues that whoever accepts circumstantial and constitutive luck has no stable reason to reject resultant luck. The question bears on law, as in the lighter punishment of attempted crimes, and on luck egalitarian theories of justice. If we hold people responsible only for what they control, almost nothing would remain to judge; yet we do not stop judging, and the tension is the problem.",
    s: "Williams, “Moral Luck” (1976); Nagel, “Moral Luck” (1976), in Mortal Questions (1979)",
    p: ["nagel"], r: ["compatibilism", "determinism", "consequentialism"], br: ["ethics"], k: ["moral luck"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-luck/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Moral Luck”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/williams-bernard/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Bernard Williams”" }] },
  { id: "interest-convergence", t: "Interest convergence", a: "crit",
    d: "Derrick Bell's principle that the interests of Black Americans in racial equality are accommodated only when they converge with the interests of whites.",
    x: "Bell's article on Brown v. Board of Education (1980) argued that the 1954 decision came when desegregation served white elite interests, among them the United States' image abroad during the Cold War, and that remedies receded when those interests diverged. The principle belongs with his racial realism, the view that racism is a permanent feature of American life.",
    s: "Bell, “Brown v. Board of Education and the Interest-Convergence Dilemma” (1980); Faces at the Bottom of the Well (1992)",
    p: ["bell", "crenshaw"], r: ["racial-formation", "intersectionality", "racial-contract"], br: ["race", "law"], k: ["interest convergence", "racial realism"] },
  { id: "matrix-of-domination", t: "Matrix of domination", a: "crit",
    d: "Patricia Hill Collins's term for the overall organization of intersecting oppressions of race, gender, class and sexuality; the second edition of Black Feminist Thought (2000) describes it working across structural, disciplinary, hegemonic and interpersonal domains of power.",
    x: "Black Feminist Thought (1990) used the concept to show that oppressions are organized together, and that a person may be oppressed in one respect and privileged in another. Collins also analyzed controlling images, such as the mammy and the welfare mother, that justify Black women's subordination, and the outsider-within position from which Black women see dominant institutions.",
    s: "Collins, Black Feminist Thought (1990; 2nd ed. 2000); “Learning from the Outsider Within” (1986)",
    p: ["collins", "crenshaw"], r: ["intersectionality", "standpoint", "hegemony"], br: ["feminist", "race"], k: ["matrix of domination", "outsider within", "controlling images"] },
];

